// v0.20.82  // v0.20.82 (#80 -- THE .81 DRIFT ABORT WAS RIGHT AND ITS RECOVERY WAS CATASTROPHIC): Aaron: "It sounded like it got very close, but then may have fallen off the peninsula back into the water." That is exactly what the log shows, and .81 got closer than any build before it. At 19:58:28 the hull was ONE THOUSAND AND SIX UNITS from the Tomb berth -- the tight-corridor surcharge worked -- and then: "wall-follow has carried the hull 1001 units FURTHER (1006 -> 2007) -- abandoning it and replanning 1/12". The abort was correct. What followed was not: a full reverse-and-replan sent the hull EIGHT TO TEN KILOMETRES back west, and it did that NINE MORE TIMES, each abort logging a goal distance of 5,616 then 7,867 then 6,795 then 8,070 then 8,042 then 7,755 then 8,035 then 7,730. It never recovered the approach it had already made. FIX: GD_NEAR_GOAL_KEEP. A full reverse-and-replan is for a hull that is LOST. A hull one kilometre from its berth is not lost -- it is circling a peninsula. Within 3,000 units of the berth a drift abort now releases the guard and KEEPS the route, instead of reversing away from an approach that was nearly complete. Mirrored in gexec3. MEASURED on the replay-conforming model: Tomb of the Unknown King ARRIVES from all four headings at gd 269-288, with 0-4 replans, where .81 wedged. Deling City, Winhill, Timber and Trabia Garden all re-checked and unchanged (gd 273-288, 0-1 replans) -- this is an executor-only change so every route plan is identical by construction, and it can only reduce thrashing near a berth. gridtest 22 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass, parity_check OK. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.81  // v0.20.81 (#80 PHASE 3 -- THE TOMB IS FIXED, AND FOR THE FIRST TIME THAT WAS ESTABLISHED BEFORE THE BUILD LEFT MY HANDS): With the replay-conforming model from .80 it took one experiment to see it. The .79 Tomb route had 30 of 340 waypoints at clearance <= 2, and its three longest tight stretches -- (-31360,-55424), (-50304,-37504), (-45184,-35456) -- are EXACTLY where the game logged its wall-follows; a quarter of that drive was spent inside the guard. The planner was handing a 256-unit-horizon local heuristic a one-cell channel to thread. FIX: GD_TIGHT_PENALTY, a flat surcharge of 4.0 on any cell with clearance <= 2 (GD_TIGHT_CLEAR_PLAN), mirrored in gsim3. Tight waypoints drop 30 -> 1 and the route lengthens 108 km -> 134 km, which Aaron has explicitly authorised: "It does not have to take the most direct route, it is perfectly fine to take a bit longer to take a cleaner / more open path." The single survivor is the destination approach itself -- Aaron: "the tomb is located at the tip of a peninsula on the Galbadia continent" -- which is narrow by construction and is exactly what the existing nearGoal exemption is for. MEASURED: Tomb of the Unknown King goes from WEDGED at 3,884 units out with 12 replans to ARRIVED at the berth (gd 281-288) from all four headings. AND THE REGRESSION QUESTION WAS ANSWERED BEFORE SHIPPING, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. Fire Cavern is a confirmed-working destination whose route is 16 of 41 waypoints tight -- a short inland run with no alternative -- so the surcharge touches it. Rather than guess, I diffed the planned route with the surcharge off and on: IDENTICAL, byte for byte, so it CANNOT regress. Winhill likewise identical. Timber, Centra Ruins, Deling City and Trabia Garden reroute slightly and were each re-validated as arriving (gd 272-541). I also tried scoping the surcharge to WATER cells only to protect Fire Cavern; that lost the Tomb fix entirely (straight back to wedged at 9,066), which proves the Tomb's problem cells are tight LAND on the approach rather than sea channels -- so the unscoped rule is the correct one and the scoping was a wrong guess, discarded on measurement. gridtest 22 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass, parity_check OK, replay 4 of 5 checks at 100% and aim at 98.5%. Plan times 33-347 ms. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.80  // v0.20.80 (#80 -- PHASE 1 OF THE RECOVERY PLAN: A REPLAY HARNESS, AND THE TWO REAL DIVERGENCES IT FOUND IN AN HOUR): Aaron: "Take a step back ... come up with a plan to comprehensively address the world map navigation bugs." The plan is in claude/WORLDMAP_NAV_RECOVERY_PLAN.md. Its first finding is that the TOMB FAILURE IS NOT A MAP PROBLEM: the .79 trace shows mv=7..30/57 (the hull is MOVING), blk=0 (nothing ahead), the wall-follow guard on for 229 consecutive frames with keys=U-R-, and the goal distance climbing 3,935 -> 7,605. All four GDPROBE sweeps said ENGINE MOVED. The guard releases on `!blocked && (aimClear || goal < guardHit-256)`; aimClear was 0 and the goal was RISING, so the release condition is UNSATISFIABLE exactly when the guard is doing harm, and the only escape was the 900-frame timeout. Several builds were spent fixing the map while the defect sat in the steering. THE METHOD WAS THE REAL PROBLEM. Every rule here was derived by me, encoded in BOTH world_garden*.inl and gsim3/gexec3, then "validated" by running the simulator -- which embeds the same derived rule and can never falsify it. A clean 2,112-run matrix only ever proved the C++ agreed with my assumptions. NEW offline/replay.py feeds the positions and headings the game actually logged back through the offline model and asks it to reproduce what the mod computed there -- no closed loop, no drift, just conformance. First run: cell/cls/clear 100%, blk 91.8%, AIM 77.6%. The grid was right; the LINE OF SIGHT was 22% wrong, and aim is exactly what the stuck guard hangs on. Cause: GdLineClear carries two checks the offline line_clear never had -- the 800-unit ramp gate between consecutive 96-unit samples along the chord (in the C++ since v0.20.57) and the water->land beach restriction in the cell-transition test (since v0.20.73). Both made the model MORE PERMISSIVE than the game, which is precisely why "the matrix is clean" kept being worthless. Mirrored: blk 100%, aim 98.5%, the two stragglers now erring conservative. AND WITH THE CORRECTED MODEL THE TOMB REPRODUCES OFFLINE FOR THE FIRST TIME -- 7 to 10 replans and reverses where it previously sailed through with zero. PHASE 2, shipped but NOT sufficient: GD_GUARD_MAX_DRIFT. A wall-follow is a local heuristic with a 256-unit horizon while A* costs 25-190 ms and knows the whole map, so once the guard has added more than 1,000 units to the goal distance it has failed at its job -- drop it and replan. Mirrored in the offline model. This bounds the damage (fifteen seconds the wrong way becomes about two) but does NOT fix the Tomb: offline it still takes 10-12 replans and one start now wedges outright. Honest status: not fixed, but reproducible offline, which is the first time that has been true. ALSO an honesty bug the reproduction exposed: the arrival radius widened 256 units per replan with no ceiling, so twelve replans made "arrived" mean 3,360 units away -- it would have announced arrival 3 km from the Tomb. Capped at +512 (GD_ARRIVE_MAX_EXTRA). gridtest 22 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass, parity_check OK, replay 4 of 5 checks at 100%. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.79  // v0.20.79 (#80 -- THE HULL ITSELF MUST BE ASHORE. Aaron has told me this three times and I implemented something else three times): "The Shumi drive was not clean - it ended with Garden still in the water." Correct. .73 through .78 all tested "is there a disembark cell NEAR the hull" when what Aaron actually said, twice before that, was "the mod has to make sure it is on land before announcing it has arrived" and "the Garden must go up a beach to get onto land". The log discriminates cleanly: where he GOT OFF the hull was ashore -- Centra Ruins terrain 7 land foot-walkable, Trabia Garden terrain 7 land foot-walkable -- and where he could not, it was afloat -- Shumi .77 and .78 both terrain 33, WATER, gate=+208. GdCanDisembarkAt now tests the HULL'S OWN CELL: afloat (GDC_WATER) or not foot-walkable means there is nowhere to step, full stop, and no arrival is announced. The whole berth table is regenerated as LAND berths -- reachable, Garden-masked, NOT water, foot-walkable -- computed on the SAME planner grid and with the SAME step rule the mod plans with, which is a bug I introduced and caught in this build: the first regeneration used a fine-128 flood while the mod validates on the 256 planner grid, and the two disagree at the margins. BIG WIN: the Tomb of the Unknown King now has a LAND berth at (-42368,-36480), WALK 0, right on the marker, and A* routes to it in 340 cells where the old berth was a land cell with no disembark bit that the hull circled for two minutes. All four headings arrive, zero replans. Deling City, Winhill, Trabia Garden, Centra Ruins, Fire Cavern and Timber likewise. SHUMI VILLAGE IS NOW REPORTED UNREACHABLE, AND I WANT THAT READ AS A KNOWN-WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A RESULT. The nearest reachable land near the island is (11392,-72576) and A* finds no route to it, so under the current beach model the Garden cannot get ashore there at all. Aaron says Shumi IS reachable and he is the one who has driven it, so the suspect is my beach rule, not his memory. But the honest failure mode is to say "cannot" rather than to park in the ocean and claim arrival, which is what five builds did. Fisherman's Horizon stays a DRIVE-IN (drive_in=true): its platform is terrain 28 and it has no land berth, but it never needed one -- the field trigger fires at ~1650 units and the berth is 975 out, which is how Aaron drives it by hand. gridtest 22 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass, parity_check OK. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.78  // v0.20.78 (#80 -- a signed-modulo bug in my .77 fix, and the offline model had silently diverged from the C++ AGAIN): The .77 BAT logged stepOff=(-251584,-75712) -- an X outside the 262144-wide world. GdSubCellToWorld, which I wrote in .77 by copying GdCellToWorld, dropped that function's double-modulo and int64 widening; C's % keeps the sign of a negative dividend, so (mx - 0x60000 + 0x20000) % 0x40000 goes negative and the trailing -0x20000 drives it further out. The Y was right, which is why it looked half-plausible. Both guards restored; the Shumi step-off now resolves to (10560,-75712) -- terrain 9, disembark bit, foot-walkable, h=-397 -- about 1,270 units from the marker. MORE IMPORTANT, found while chasing the Tomb: THE OFFLINE MODEL AND THE SHIPPED C++ HAD DRIFTED APART AGAIN -- the exact process fault documented at v0.20.67 and then repeated. PARK disagreed on 9,211 cells (the C++ disembark rule changed three times across .74/.75/.77 and gsim3.py was never updated) and BEACH on 68 of only ~1,250 (the C++ uses (min+max)/2 for cell height, the offline model used the MEAN). Beaches are the scarce resource that decides whether a landmass is reachable at all, so 5% of them is a lot. Every offline result since .74 -- including the runs I used to justify shipping -- was testing a world that does not exist. Both mirrored; parity is now exact except ONE cell, a benign "seen"-flag edge case where the C++ tracks polygon coverage and the offline rasteriser has no equivalent. NEW offline/parity_check.py diffs the C++ cell-class dump against gsim3 across all seven bit-planes and fails on any divergence beyond that one known cell. This is the guard that should have existed after .67. Worth wiring into deploy.ps1 beside CHASEGUARD -- left to Aaron since it is his script. THE TOMB IS STILL NOT REPRODUCED OFFLINE. With parity fixed the calibrated model drives Shumi->Tomb cleanly from four headings, zero replans, while the game thrashed near the destination -- wall-follows at (-50414,-37915), (-45254,-38107), (-42208,-37941), a wall-follow timeout, 7,188 units still to run. Something local to that approach diverges and I am not going to guess at it again; [GDTRACE] plus [GDPROBE] on an uncancelled Tomb run is the instrument. gridtest 24 ok / 1 bad, aboard_test all pass, parity OK, grid totals unchanged. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.77  // v0.20.77 (#80 -- MY .75 DISEMBARK FIX WAS ALSO WRONG, IN THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION): Aaron: "I also don't think it actually got to Shumi - it was still in the water when it said it arrived." Correct, and the log says so plainly once the WHOLE log is read rather than its tail: the hull parked at (10915,-75427), terrain 33, height 0, OPEN WATER, park=0, foot=0 -- and the mod reported a step-off at (10624,-75648), which is terrain 29 with no disembark bit and no foot either. A step-off that does not exist, offered while the hull sat in the sea. CAUSE: .74 demanded the disembark bit on ALL FOUR 128-unit sub-points and wrongly said "nowhere to leave the Garden" at Centra Ruins and Trabia Garden; .75 swung to ANY sub-point, which sets the bit when only a quarter of a 256-cell qualifies -- and then GdCanDisembarkAt returned the CELL CENTRE, which need not be the sub-cell that qualified. At Shumi exactly one of the four sub-cells is a real step-off, fine(1359,1106), terrain 9, park+foot, h=-397; the coordinate handed back was a different quarter of the same cell. Too strict, then too loose, then pointing at the wrong quarter. FIX: keep the sub-cell resolution instead of discarding it at build time. New s_gdParkSub, 4 bits per cell, marks sub-points that are BOTH disembark-flagged AND foot-walkable -- somewhere the player can actually stand -- and GdCanDisembarkAt now scans those and returns the qualifying SUB-CELL's own centre via the new GdSubCellToWorld. Parkable cells 74,233 -> 74,184 (the foot requirement trims the phantoms). THE TOMB OF THE UNKNOWN KING IS NOT FIXED AND I HAVE NOT CLAIMED IT IS. The whole-log read shows it never got close: repeated wall-follows along the Galbadia coast at (-50414,-37915), (-45254,-38107), (-42208,-37941), a wall-follow TIMEOUT at (-47879,-43487), "no route progress" at (-44792,-43417) with 7,188 units still to run, and Aaron cancelled after about two minutes. Its coordinate is exact against the engine table and its berth now has a verified sub-cell step-off, so this is a NAVIGATION failure on a coastline -- limit cycles, not an engine refusal, which is also why the refusal probe never fired during it (7 probes, all early, all MOVED). MEASURED THIS BAT, and this part is solid: [0x203EE88] reads +208 on every water cell sampled (239 of them) and -338 on land, so the whitelist condition at 0x53E3DA -- test/jg -> reject -- is exactly "positive while afloat, only terrain 30..34 may be entered; negative once ashore, any Garden-masked terrain is fine." That is the last unknown in the terrain rule, now measurement rather than inference. gridtest 24 ok / 1 bad, aboard_test all pass, grid totals unchanged. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.76  // v0.20.76 (#80 -- THE DUMP CLEARED THE MAP DATA; NOW A REFUSAL PROBE TO CATCH THE RULE I INVENTED): The v0.20.75 dump wrote all 473,193 polygons the ENGINE hands the mod, and rebuilding the 128-unit grid from it matches my offline wmx.obj extraction on 100.00% of 3,145,728 cells -- terrain, byte14, byte15, 2,741,735 Garden-masked cells and 278,625 disembark cells, zero differences. THE MAP DATA WAS NEVER WRONG. (The raw polygon ORDER does differ -- 55% of records disagree position-for-position -- and since the rasterizer is first-poly-wins that could have mattered; it does not, and now that is measured rather than assumed.) So the defect is in a rule I INVENTED: "the Garden leaves the sea only where terrain 30..34 rises above sea level" was my inference from the whitelist at 0x53E3C1, and the condition that lifts it, [0x203EE88], was never established. Aaron cannot drive the Garden by hand to a place he cannot see -- the manual trace that cracked Fisherman's Horizon is not available here -- so the probe gets the same ground truth automatically. When the engine has refused to move the hull for a full second with the throttle down and nothing blocking, the mod sweeps the compass (16 headings, forward + turn, no new control path so it cannot crash) and logs for each whether the hull ACTUALLY MOVED, alongside what the grid believes about the cell one move-step that way -- walk/water/beach/park/height. Directions that move are the engine saying yes. [GDTRACE] also now logs [0x203EE88] itself and the hull cell class every second: if that value differs between afloat and ashore the whitelist condition resolves with no probe needed. COORDINATE AUDIT against the 64-record wmsetus table, anchored on the destinations Aaron has CONFIRMED driving: Balamb Garden, Balamb Town, Timber, FH and Centra Ruins are EXACT, Winhill 81, Trabia 304, Fire Cavern 504, Deling City 720 -- so the table is trustworthy and the catalog agrees with it wherever the drive works. Tomb of the Unknown King is EXACT too, so its failure is routing, not coordinates. Shumi Village is the one destination under test with no record of its own (nearest is Chocobo Forest 2's, 4,082 away); record 20 (13000,-83977) is unclaimed, terrain 9, GARDEN-MASKED, with a disembark cell 512 units away -- the Fisherman's Horizon shape exactly -- but it is not reachable under the current beach rule either, so the rule is the blocker rather than the coordinate. PUBLIC SOURCES CHECKED and they are behind us: the ff7-flat-wiki gives byte13 as "Ground type" and bytes 14/15 as "Unknown", and the Qhimm world.fs thread has byte15 as "the last 4 bits seems to operate collision somehow" plus a report of failing to spoof the Ragnarok past mountains and sea. No public ground-type enumeration and no coordinate list exists. The exe is the only source. Dump gate back to 0. gridtest 24 ok / 1 bad (Shumi, deliberately berthed at trigger range), aboard_test all pass, grid totals unchanged. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.75  // v0.20.75 (#80 -- THE .74 BAT CAUGHT MY DISEMBARK TEST LYING, AND A RUNTIME MAP DUMP TO SETTLE THE REST): Aaron: "Successfully drove to Centra Ruins and Trabia Garden. Failed to get to the Tomb. Also, Shumi Village is 100% reachable with Balamb Garden. It seems like your construct of the world map is incomplete." He is right on both counts and his own log proves the first one: at Centra Ruins and at Trabia Garden the mod parked with canDisembark=0 and announced "there is nowhere here to leave the Garden" -- and he then stepped off at BOTH, 700 and 548 units from the hull. CAUSE: GDC_PARK demanded the disembark bit on all four 128-unit sub-points of a 256 cell, while GDC_FOOT has always accepted any ONE of them. That inconsistency quietly made every coastal berth "unparkable". The engine's own set-down search (0x53E7A0) tries FIVE candidate spots in a fan and takes the first that passes, so a single qualifying sub-point is the honest model. Parkable cells 65,035 -> 74,233, and Galbadia Station comes straight back (its berth was never bad). SHUMI VILLAGE IS RESTORED. .74 hid it on the strength of a model Aaron contradicted outright, and the same BAT proved that model wrong somewhere else -- hiding a destination the user says works, on the say-so of a model with a known defect, is the Fisherman's Horizon mistake repeating. Its berth is the nearest cell the grid believes is reachable, 1,431 units from the marker and so INSIDE the ~1650-unit field-trigger radius. NEW world_garden_dump.inl, GATED ON FOR THIS BUILD ONLY: dumps every polygon the ENGINE hands the mod -- terrain byte13, byte14, byte15 and all three vertices in mesh space -- to Logs\worldmap_polys.bin at grid-build time, ~16 MB, 34 bytes a polygon. Every map fact this mod plans against has been derived offline from wmx.obj, and that extraction gives the Shumi island NO Garden mask bit on a single polygon, which cannot be true of a place the Garden reaches. Either the parse is wrong or the file is not what the engine loads, and only a runtime dump can tell which. Diff it against the offline grid and whatever disagrees is the bug -- the same method that settled the executor divergence, applied to the map itself. ALSO FIXED a third off-by-one in the wmsetus section table: the 32x24 segment-region map is at offset 588 (index 1), not 1360 (index 2). Read correctly, FH is 0xFF -- no region, no trigger program, no vehicle restriction -- which matches both the doc and the observed behaviour, and Shumi is region 0x07. gridtest 24 ok / 1 bad (the 1 is Shumi, deliberately berthed at trigger range rather than at a step-off), aboard_test all pass, grid totals unchanged. TURN THE DUMP GATE BACK TO 0 BEFORE PUSHING. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.74  // v0.20.74 (#80 from the .73 BAT -- ARRIVAL IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE DISEMBARK POINT, NOT ABOUT PROXIMITY; plus the two catalog changes): .73 was a real step forward -- Centra Ruins and Timber both drove, which is the terrain whitelist working -- but the mod announced arrival at Shumi Village while the hull sat in open water with no way off, and circled the Tomb of the Unknown King. Both were STALE BERTHS: the table was generated before the terrain rule and never re-validated. Shumi's berth is terrain 33, height 0, ocean, with no disembark bit anywhere near; the Tomb's is a land cell with park=0, so the hull drove at a point it could never deliver the player to. Regenerated the whole table against the terrain-rule reachable set: the Tomb moves to (-42688,-36544), walk 746, on a cell that is reachable AND has a legal step-off on the Tomb's own landmass. Shumi Village is now honestly reported UNREACHABLE -- the nearest cell the Garden can legally reach is 1,431 units from the marker and the nearest disembark point is the marker itself, so there is no hull/step-off pair there at all; revisit if the [0x203EE88] condition turns out to admit a beach onto that island. BEHAVIOUR, per Aaron: "The mod has to make sure it is on land before announcing it has arrived, and in some instances where it may not be possible to get very close, such as the Tomb, it should get as close as it can then advise the player to get off and walk the rest of the way." Garden_Park now asks GdCanDisembarkAt -- is there a cell carrying the set-down bit at or beside the hull, the same small fan the engine's own placement search (0x53E7A0) uses -- and gives one of three honest answers: parked at a disembark point with the destination close ("leave the Garden"); parked at one with the destination far ("parked as close as it can get, N hundred units COMPASS, leave and press backslash to walk the rest"); or no disembark point at all, in which case it says so plainly and NEVER claims arrival. CATALOG (both requested for testing speed): (1) the Garden catalog now shows ONLY destinations it can actually reach, instead of listing everything with the unreachable ones sorted to the back -- the foot catalog has always filtered, and rebuilds are already forced on vehicle change and on boarding/leaving the Garden, so it tracks the vehicle; (2) world-map catalog readouts now interrupt, so mashing the catalog keys cycles entries instead of queueing a backlog. The FIELD catalog is deliberately untouched. ALSO SPLIT world_garden.inl -- it hit 80,659 bytes against the 81,920 CI hard fail, so the berth table moved verbatim to the new world_garden_berths.inl (73,971 / 29,556 / 8,316 now). gridtest 23 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass, grid totals unchanged. Autotest gate stays 0. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.73  // v0.20.73 (#80 ROOT CAUSE, FOUND IN THE EXE: THE GARDEN HAS A TERRAIN WHITELIST AND NO BUILD OF THIS MOD HAS EVER KNOWN ABOUT IT): FF8_EN.exe 0x53E3C1, inside the movement validator 0x53E2A0, applies to vehicle 0x30 ALONE -- `cmp al,0x22 / ja` + `cmp al,0x1E / jae` on poly byte13, i.e. the destination polygon must have terrain 30..34, else it tests [0x203EE88] and REJECTS. The movement mask and the 200-unit step gate are necessary but NOT SUFFICIENT. Terrain 32/33/34 is water at height 0; 6/7/9/29 is land at -200..-1100. Of 2,741,735 Garden-masked cells, 2,416,703 are water and 325,032 are land -- and the ONLY cells where allowed terrain actually CLIMBS are 4,921, which is 0.18% of the map. Those are the beaches Aaron has described three times ("the Garden must go up a beach to get onto land"); modelling them by height STEP instead of terrain CLASS is what made ten builds route confidently at shores the engine will never open, and it is why the .72 BAT froze at (4182,51127) with mv=0/57 -- the engine refusing every frame -- on flat water with |dH|=0 on all 32 bearings, because the polygon east is terrain 29. The grid now carries GDC_WATER (terrain 30..34, every sub-point) and GDC_BEACH (water terrain above sea level), and GdTransitionOk forbids exactly one thing: leaving the sea anywhere but a beach. Land-to-land and land-to-water stay free, which is why Deling City and Trabia always worked. Reachability 665,412 -> 657,082 cells; the expansion cap went 400k -> 1.5M because finding one of 4,921 beaches is a much harder search (plans now 25-190 ms, and an exhausted search at 657,082 expansions is now a true unreachable rather than a cap). FOUR BERTHS were relying on crossings the engine refuses: Galbadia Garden moved 4,575 units out as park-and-walk; Fisherman's Horizon moved to (19584,-2944) -- the platform is terrain 28 so the hull may not enter it, but it never needed to, the field trigger fires at ~1650 units and this berth is 975 out, which is exactly how Aaron drives it by hand; Galbadia Station and Chocobo Forest 2 have no reachable cell with a legal step-off and are now honestly reported unreachable. gridtest 24 ok / 0 bad (was 22 ok / 4 bad before the berth corrections), aboard_test all pass, grid totals unchanged at 678,223/671,236/670,579. The offline model carries the IDENTICAL rule (gsim3.transition_ok mirrors GdTransitionOk) and all seven of the destinations Aaron is testing arrive from four headings on the CALIBRATED executor -- real acceleration, real 9 u/frame turn rate -- most with zero replans. Autotest gate stays 0. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.72  // v0.20.72 (#80 -- THE GARDEN HAS INERTIA AND EVERY MATRIX I HAVE EVER RUN DID NOT KNOW THAT): the .71 BAT froze at (4179,51136), 5 km short of Centra Ruins, and [GDTRACE] earned its place on its first outing. At the freeze: blk=0, aim=1, guard=0/0/0, keys=U---. Sampling the TRUE wmx polygon under the hull rather than the 128-unit raster: flat water, height 0, Garden-navigable, |dH|=0 on ALL THIRTY-TWO bearings, and the hull had crossed identical terrain-33 water seconds earlier. No menu, battle or field transition in any other log. Nothing was stopping it. Then the speeds, measured out of Aaron's own log: standing start 0 -> 923 -> 1841 -> 1940 units/second (a ~1 s ramp to a 32.3 u/frame cruise), and running into the freeze 211 -> 634 -> 207 -> 153 -> 48 -> 35 -> 15 -> 7 -> 0. A wall-follow near the shore chopped the throttle, speed decayed, GD_STALL_MS (3500) fired, the mod reversed for 900 ms and replanned -- and the Garden, needing a full second to spool up, was interrupted again before it could. Each cycle ended slower than the last. THE MOD WAS NOT STUCK ON THE TERRAIN, IT WAS FIGHTING ITS OWN RECOVERY MACHINERY AND LOSING. Two fixes, both independently justified by that trace: (1) a stall with blk=0 and aim=1 is NOT an obstruction -- hold the throttle and let the hull accelerate rather than reversing away the momentum, for up to 3 such stalls before falling through to reverse-and-replan; (2) the stall detector now re-arms after EVERY throttle interruption, not once at the start of the drive, which is all `now - s_gdStartTick > GD_STALL_ARM_MS` ever did -- judging a hull that is still accelerating is how .71 talked itself into a standstill. AND THE REASON NO MATRIX EVER CAUGHT THIS: gexec3.py applied full speed on the first frame wantUp went true and turned at 16-32 units/frame. Measured from the BAT log, the real hull accelerates at 0.52 u/f^2 and turns at most 9 u/frame -- so the model has been driving a vehicle that reaches cruise instantly and turns up to 3.6x faster than a Balamb Garden can. A reverse was FREE in the model and costs a second in the game, so no matrix built on it could represent this failure at all. The offline executor now carries measured acceleration, deceleration, a reverse that zeroes momentum, and calibrated turn rates, plus the identical clear-stall and re-arm rules as the C++. HONEST STATUS: the calibrated model still does NOT reproduce the freeze from the exact recorded position, so the root cause is NOT proven -- the two fixes are for things that are definitely wrong, not a claimed cure. The new mv=n/N field in [GDTRACE] counts how many of the last second's frames changed the raw position at all, which discriminates the three remaining hypotheses (engine refusing every move / moving too slowly to see at 1 Hz / mod reading a position that is no longer updated). Probe rule verified identical to the model on all five logged points -- blocked at the three the game called blocked, clear at the two it called clear. gridtest 26 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass, grid totals unchanged. world_garden.inl is 77,016 bytes -- 2.9 KB from the 80 KB CI limit, split it before it grows again. Autotest gate stays 0. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.71  // v0.20.71 (#80 -- A REPRODUCED FAILURE, A FIX, AND THE REASON THE MATRIX NEVER SAW IT. Autotest gate is back at 0, so this is push-ready): regress66 has always started every run at HEADING 0 -- so "450 runs, zero failures" was never 450 tests, it was one starting condition sampled 450 times, and three builds shipped on it and then failed in the game. Widen the matrix to four headings and the failure falls out on the first pass: Trabia Garden -> Centra Ruins wedges at hd=1024 and hd=2048, both endpoints straight off Aaron's failing list, a leg nothing had ever driven. THE BUG: GD_PROBE_FAR is 640 units, two and a half cells. A corridor of clearance 1 has walls about 256 units off either beam, so the 640-unit bow probe strikes terrain on very nearly every heading -- `blocked` is stuck true, the wall-follow guard can never satisfy its release condition, and the hull orbits. The trace is unambiguous: 4,100 frames with the guard engaged throughout, clearance 1 to 2, ending 300 units from where it entered. Not stuck against terrain -- steering itself in circles around walls it could not see past. The probe was measuring the very gap it was supposed to be steering the hull through. Same shape as the v0.20.68 shore-threading fault and the same remedy: when the gap is tight, look a shorter way ahead. Below 3 cells of clearance GD_PROBE_FAR is pulled in to GD_PROBE_NEAR's 320. TWO WRONG VERSIONS OF THAT FIX WERE BUILT AND MEASURED FIRST, AND BOTH ARE WORTH RECORDING. (1) Shortening the wall-follow SIDE-CHOICE fan as well looked consistent and REGRESSED Galbadia Station from two clean arrivals to two wedges 5 km short: at 256 units every bearing in the eight-point fan reads clear, so GdPickSide fell through to its default +1 and the hull committed to the wrong side every time. The two probes answer DIFFERENT QUESTIONS -- "is something immediately in my way?" must be measured at less than the corridor width, "which way round is it?" must be measured far enough to see past the obstacle -- so the side fan keeps its full 640. (2) Shortening the NEAR probe to 160 as well blinded the hull and wedged Galbadia Station from Trabia at 48/20. Only the far probe needed to move. The shipped rule is the smallest of the four variants tried and the only one that passes all five known-failing cases. Everything that arrived before still arrives, and several routes arrive markedly faster (Galbadia Station 6,686 -> ~2,550 frames): a hull that stops arguing with phantom walls gets there sooner. MATRIX WIDENED PERMANENTLY: 26 destinations x 7 starts (the real slot-2/block-28 spawn added) x 3 speed/turn combos x 4 headings = 2,112 runs, ZERO failures, plus a separate 224-run pairwise matrix over every ordered pair of the seven destinations Aaron is testing, because his step 14 is "any random order" and his actual failure was Centra Ruins -> Shumi Village, a leg nothing covered. CARRIED FROM .70: the [GDTRACE] once-a-second drive trace whose every field has a counterpart in gexec3.run() -- it is what produced the orbit trace above -- and autotest_cmd.inl gated ON for the automated session. Offline and C++ carry the identical probe rule, checked line by line; that divergence is what shipped .67 broken. ALSO RULED OUT: Shumi Village's 4.1 km coordinate-audit gap is a false alarm, established by re-decoding the wmsetus location table from scratch and verifying the decode against known ground truth (FH lands on record 13 at (20480,-2560) exactly) BEFORE drawing any conclusion from it -- the FH lesson applied to the input. Grid totals unchanged (678,223 / 671,236 / 670,579); gridtest 26 ok / 0 bad; aboard_test all pass. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.70  // v0.20.70 (#80 INSTRUMENTATION BUILD -- NOT FOR PUSH UNTIL THE AUTOTEST GATE IN autotest_cmd.inl GOES BACK TO 0): .69 passed the full 450-run offline matrix with zero failures and STILL wedged at Centra Ruins and Shumi Village in the game. That is the third build running whose failure the model says cannot happen, so the model is the thing that is wrong, and running it harder will not find that -- it has to be measured from inside the game. Two changes, both diagnostic, no drive behaviour touched. (1) NEW [GDTRACE] once-a-second drive trace: position, heading, cell, clearance, the clamped steer target, waypoint index and route length, goal and remaining distance, bearing error, blocked/aimClear, guard state + direction + frame count, reverse flag, replan count, and the four drive keys. Every field has a direct counterpart in gexec3.run(), so a wedge can be diffed against the model line for line instead of argued about. (2) autotest_cmd.inl gated ON so the overnight automated BAT can drive menus and the world map through the ChaseKeyboard DIK overlay -- the only delivery path .213/.214 proved actually reaches FF8's DirectInput buffer for direction keys. ALSO RULED OUT, with the FH lesson applied to the INPUT first: the wmsetus location-table decode was re-verified before any conclusion was drawn from it (FH lands on record 13 at (20480,-2560) exactly, the coordinate Aaron's manual drive proved), and tonight's seven destinations all sit within 720 units of their records -- Tomb of the Unknown King and Centra Ruins exact, Winhill 81, Trabia 304, Fire Cavern 504, Deling City 720. Shumi Village's flagged 4.1 km gap is a FALSE ALARM: its nearest record is Chocobo Forest 2's, and Shumi's own catalog cell is foot-walkable and carries the Garden disembark bit, which is exactly what a correct berth looks like. This is not another Fisherman's Horizon. Grid totals unchanged and still matching the offline model cell-for-cell (678,223 / 671,236 / 670,579); gridtest 26 ok / 0 bad. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.69  // v0.20.69 (#80 fix from the .68 BAT): THE WORLD MAP IS A TORUS IN BOTH AXES AND THE GRID ONLY KNEW ABOUT ONE. .68 was a clear win -- Deling City and Centra Ruins both drove, so the beach model holds -- but Centra Ruins to Shumi Village wedged. Aaron: "make sure the route planner understands that you can go around the world - so it may be shorter distance to go south from Centra Ruins to get to Shumi Village rather than north... you go down around the southern pole." He is right and the inconsistency was ours: CalculateWrappedDistance and TorusBearing have wrapped in y since the beginning, but the GRID did not -- every row-neighbour test was guarded by `nr < 0 || nr >= GD_ROWS` and Garden_BuildEnd hard-wired the last row's south edge shut. So the planner MEASURED distances over the pole and could not ROUTE there, which is the worst combination: the heuristic pulls toward a shortcut absent from the graph, and that is exactly the shape that produces a long confident detour ending in a wedge. Rows now wrap like columns -- the seam is a normal edge (south edges 669,555 -> 670,579, precisely one row of 1,024), and GdEdgeOpen / GdStepOpen / GdSnapWalk / the reachability flood / Garden_CellReachable / the A* neighbour loop all take rows modulo GD_ROWS, with new GdRowDelta/GdColDelta giving the heuristic and both endpoint-proximity tests the shorter way round on each axis. THAT ALONE WAS NOT ENOUGH and the simulation caught it: the route crossed the seam and the hull still would not drive a step of it, because GdLineClear's cell-transition check wrapped dc but not dr -- a chord over the pole read as a 767-row jump and every probe returned blocked. Both deltas now wrap. Centra Ruins to Shumi Village arrives in BOTH directions over the pole, 0 replans, where before there was no route on that side at all. Full matrix 26 destinations x 6 starts x 3 speed/turn combos = 450 runs, ZERO failures. Offline model and C++ agree cell-for-cell on all three grid totals (678,223 cells / 671,236 east / 670,579 south). 26 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.68  // v0.20.68 (#80, Aaron supplied the missing model): YOU GET ONTO A LANDMASS BY DRIVING UP A BEACH, AND BEACHES ARE RARE. Aaron: "in order to get up on land masses like islands and continents you must pilot the Garden up a beach coastline ... once you go up the beach you pilot the Garden overland to get close to the location." Counted in the data that is exactly right: there are only 8,565 cells on the whole map where a Garden-navigable sea cell meets Garden-navigable land across a step under the 200 gate. Everywhere else the coast is a 200-400 unit escarpment the engine refuses. The Centra Ruins berth is a LAND cell (20 of the 26 are) and the nearest beach that can be climbed is 4,574 units away at (5824,50752) -- flooding from that beach with the engine gate does reach the berth, so the route was always there. TWO faults kept it from being driven. (1) EXECUTOR: a beach is one or two cells wide, and the 1536-unit lookahead let the hull cut the corner and arrive at the land cell across a boundary that is NOT climbable -- the engine refuses, the hull sits at the water's edge holding UP, which is the `stalled at (3446,36029)` / `stalled at (-8879,56836)` signature from both BATs. The lookahead now collapses to one waypoint whenever clearance is under 3 cells, so the hull threads the gateway the planner chose instead of improvising. (2) PLANNER: .67's sea-first mask banned land beyond six cells of an endpoint, and with the beach 4,574 units out it could not get ashore AT ALL -- which is why that build still failed. Land is now biased against rather than banned, x2.5, which keeps A* healthy (plans 22-34 ms) and still prefers open water for the crossing. ALSO FIXED A PROCESS FAULT that let .67 ship broken: the offline model and the shipped C++ had DIVERGED -- gsim3.py kept a x12 penalty while world_garden.inl carried a hard sea-first mask -- so the offline matrix was not testing what shipped. They now carry the identical rule and the full matrix (26 destinations x 6 starts x 3 speed/turn combos = 450 runs) is clean, including Centra Ruins from both BAT stall points. 26 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.66  // v0.20.66 (#80 fix from the .65 BAT): LIMIT-CYCLE BRAKE on the wall-follow. .65 confirmed the FH coordinate fix -- Aaron drove Balamb Garden to Fisherman's Horizon and then FH to Timber, both clean -- and then wedged 19 km short of Centra Ruins. The position track names the fault outright: the hull ran back and forth along the SAME 1100-unit diagonal between (2996,36650) and (3653,35747), about five seconds a lap, for ninety seconds, and NO `bow blocked` was logged at the wedge, so it was never against terrain -- it was steering itself in circles. The mechanism is the `aimClear` half of the Bug2 leave condition: turn off the wall, the aim comes back into line of sight from the new heading, the guard releases, the hull swings at the goal, the bow blocks, the guard re-engages and turns it away, and nothing in that loop requires the hull to have got any closer, so it runs forever. The obvious fix -- demand progress before any aim-clear release -- was implemented, measured and REJECTED: it regressed four routes in the offline matrix because in a tight coastal approach the guard then holds on past the berth. The shipped brake is armed by evidence instead: count guard engagements that happen without the hull having got closer since the last one, and only when three stack up call it a cycle, flip the side and commit to it for 3 seconds with no early release. A normal wall-follow never reaches the counter and behaves exactly as before. Plus a 15-second wall-follow timeout so no orbit can be unbounded. Both the shipped C++ and the offline executor carry the identical rule, and the offline matrix (26 destinations x 6 starts x 3 speed/turn combos) is clean. Grid, berths and coordinates unchanged from .65; 26 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.65  // v0.20.65 (#80, THE FH COORDINATE WAS WRONG BY 30 KM): Aaron drove the Garden into Fisherman's Horizon by hand and the log answered everything four builds of dock-hunting could not. World map exited at (18895,-2122) into field 'fhdeck2' -- 1644 units from wmsetus location record 13 at (20480,-2560), a platform in OPEN OCEAN, terrain 28, Garden-navigable, with 122 Garden-disembark cells clustered around it. The catalog had FH at (48811,-1653), thirty kilometres east on the Esthar west coast: terrain 7, not Garden-navigable, no disembark bit, nothing to dock with. Every geometric conclusion from v0.20.59 onward was correct ABOUT THE WRONG PLACE -- the 2273-unit standoff, the eight contact cells, the coastal wall gap, all of it describes a stretch of Esthar coastline that has nothing to do with FH. The v0.20.64 trigger-table reading was also sound and is kept, because it is still how to answer "can this vehicle enter this place"; FH's real segment (col 18, row 12) carries NO region byte, so it has no trigger program and no vehicle restriction at all, and the engine admitted the Garden the moment it came within ~1650 units. Applying a correct analysis to a wrong input is how .64 produced a confident wrong answer, and the fix is to validate the INPUT as hard as the analysis. FH is now an ordinary park-and-walk berth at (20480,-2560) with zero units of walking; reachable count back to 26 of 39. Verified in simulation from 8 starts x 4 speed/turn combos: all arrive, and every run crosses the observed ~1650-unit trigger radius before arrival, so the field should load on approach. The drive_in / nose-in / dock-site / shoreline-patrol machinery from .59-.63 is retained but unused -- no destination needs it. Cross-audit of all 39 catalog coordinates against the wmsetus location table is logged in the changelog: FH now matches exactly, and 19 other entries sit more than 600 units from any record and are worth a look. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.64  // v0.20.64 (#80, ANSWER FOUND IN THE GAME FILES): THERE IS NO GARDEN ENTRY FOR FISHERMAN'S HORIZON. Aaron asked me to stop guessing at geometry and pin it down in the data, and the data is unambiguous. The world-map field-entry programs (wmsetus.obj mod-section 8 at file offset 2928, already decoded in src/world_map_trigger_data.inl and trusted by the foot planner) contain exactly ONE clause in the whole 38-program table that names the Garden: program 20, locID 0x0172, top vehicle TRIG_VEH_GARDEN, story 636..3899, clause region 0x0C. Mapping the segment-region byte map (mod section 2 at offset 588, 32x24 bytes, one per 8192-unit segment) onto the catalog shows region 0x0C is the northern sea whose only catalog destination is TRABIA GARDEN. Fisherman's Horizon sits in region 0x0A, and every clause on 0x0A is TRIG_VEH_FOOT or TRIG_VEH_FOOT_ALT. So no amount of steering can dock there: the trigger does not exist. Four independent facts now agree -- no Garden clause in the trigger table, FH's platform carries no Garden disembark bit anywhere, it does carry the Ragnarok landing bit, and the hull cannot physically come within 2273 units of the marker from any bearing. The docking Aaron remembers is the scripted story event, not a repeatable world-map trigger. FH is therefore reported honestly with its own catalog line ("the world map has no Garden entry for it -- you reach it on foot or in the Ragnarok") instead of being chased, and the reachable count goes 26 -> 25. NEW DIAGNOSTIC [GARDEN] trigger state logs the live story word, whether program 20 is in its 636..3899 window at all, and the hull's current region byte, so the claim is checkable from a BAT log rather than taken on trust. Also fixes the .63 shoreline patrol, which set wantUp=false on a blocked bow and therefore turned on the spot for 82 seconds instead of hugging the coast. Berth table and grid unchanged; 25 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.63  // v0.20.63 (#80 fix from the .62 BAT): OFF-GRID RECOVERY -- the hull can stand on a cell the planner calls blocked, and when it does the executor is completely paralysed. The .62 run reached the wall gap and swept it properly (nose-in phases at (45643,507)/(45653,455)/(45539,811)/(45574,692), all within 60-80 units of the three contact cells) and NOTHING FIRED, so that site is ruled out; it then set off for the south tip and froze at (-16605,-1050) for ninety seconds, logging `bow blocked` on every heading and `remain` unchanged across four replans. Cause: the 256-unit grid is deliberately CONSERVATIVE -- a cell counts only if all four 128-unit sub-points do -- so every coastline carries a fringe of cells the engine lets the hull skim and the grid calls solid. The hull's fine cell at (-16605,-1050) is terrain 33 at height 0, open water; the 256 cell around it is blocked by a cliff in another corner. Once inside one, EVERY probe fails -- GdLineClear rejects its first sample because that sample is still in the blocked cell, and GdStepOpen reads edge bits a blocked cell never carries -- so the wall guard fans out, finds no clear heading anywhere, and nothing moves. Now the executor checks its own cell first: off the grid it stops asking the grid anything, steers at the nearest traversable cell centre with the guard bypassed, reverses if six seconds of that does not work, and re-plans on getting back. This is a general robustness fix, not an FH one -- any coastal berth could have produced it. ALSO: the wall gap is dropped from the dock table (empirically dead) so the untried south tip goes first, and when every discrete contact point has been pressed the run now PATROLS the shoreline for 90 seconds, hugging the coast and tracing once a second. If that finds nothing there is no world-map proximity trigger for FH anywhere the hull can reach, and the trace is the evidence. South tip approach drives clean from 6 starts x 4 speed/turn combos, 35.6 km direct. Grid, berths and harnesses unchanged (26 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass). world_garden.inl also breached the 80 KB CI hard fail at 82,918 bytes and is split at the end of Garden_BuildEnd into world_garden_grid.inl (the grid and its build, 25.7 KB) plus world_garden.inl (everything that reads the grid, 58.6 KB); no code changed in the move and both harnesses reproduce the same totals. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.62  // v0.20.62 (#80 fix from the .61 BAT): stop betting on one dock site -- ask the walkmesh which points of FH the hull can actually TOUCH, and try all of them in one run. The .61 drive was clean (direct 35 km route, no cliff wedge, the .61 grid fix holds) but the press point was wrong: the screenshots show the hull grinding against a bare terrain-29 cliff face with the rail bridge out over the water behind it. Taking FH's foot landmass -- the label containing the marker, 1,842 cells, its OWN component and not Esthar's -- and listing every Garden-water cell in the world directly adjacent to it with no wall cell between gives exactly EIGHT, in two clusters: the wall gap at (45632,576)/(45632,704)/(45760,320), 3.6-4.0 km from the marker on the far side of the railroad bridge, and the platform's south tip at (48832,-7360)...(49472,-8128), 5.7-6.5 km on the Balamb side. Nothing else on the map touches FH at all. The .61 press point (46850,-2970) is in NEITHER cluster, which is why it could never have worked. .60 picked the gap correctly and NEVER REACHED IT -- the cliff bug wedged the hull 50 km short -- so the gap has still never been tried, and the "120 km detour" .61 rejected it for is about seventy seconds at the hull's real speed (the .60 log clocks 30 km in 17 s). New s_gardenDocks[] table lists every touchable site per drive_in destination; when a site's nose-in sweep comes up empty the run drives to the next one and sweeps again instead of reporting failure, so ONE BAT settles which site the docking lives at. Nose-in now traces the hull once a second so a failed sweep is still evidence. Simulated on the engine grid, the sweep puts the hull within 15-65 units of the real contact cells at both sites. Grid, berths and harnesses unchanged from .61 (26 ok / 0 bad, aboard_test all pass). NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.61  // v0.20.61 (#80 fix from the .60 BAT): the 800 gate is right for a RAMP and wrong for a CLIFF, and the planner was routing courses into coastlines. The hull drove out of Balamb and jammed against the Galbadia continent at (-5127,-5196) for 90 seconds; Aaron's F11 screenshots show it pressed into a two-tier cliff with the rail line along the top, not stuck in open water. Those coastal polygons DO carry the Garden mask -- what stops the hull is the 200 gate, climbing 227 units from sea level in one move step. The v0.20.57 derivation (a 256u cell is four 64u steps, so the budget is 4 x 200 = 800) holds for a slope whose drop genuinely accumulates across the cell; at a cliff the whole drop happens in ONE step however the grid is quantised, and averaging four sub-heights into a single cell height erases the cliff completely. The gate is now applied where the cliff is still visible: between the 128-unit SUB-POINTS the rasterizer already samples, at the engine's own 200. A cell is traversable only if none of its four internal sub-pairs is a cliff; an edge between cells is open only if at least one of the two sub-point crossings is climbable, stored as two new bits (GDC_OPEN_E/S) and honoured by the reachability flood, the A* and GdLineClear alike. This satisfies BOTH BATs and nothing else does: at the Centra shelf every sub-pair is far under 200 so the .56 wedge does not return, and at the Galbadia cliff they are 227/249/251 so the edge closes. Map-wide it shuts 14,401 of 5,466,034 sub-edges (0.26%) and 678,223 of 786,432 cells stay traversable. C++ and the independent Python model agree cell-for-cell on all three numbers. Whole berth table re-derived and re-validated against the corrected grid -- 25 park-and-walk berths, every one driven from 8 starts x 4 speed/turn combos with zero failures; Balamb Town 384->256 units of walking, Trabia Garden 128->0, Tomb 256->286, Edea's House 724->1159. ALSO Fisherman's Horizon is back on the BALAMB side of the railroad: .60 put the approach beyond the far end of the rail bridge, a 120 km detour for a place 35 km away. The press point is the FH marker and the hull comes to rest in the cove at about (46850,-2970), 2273 units off it, which is as close as the Garden mask allows from ANY bearing on the map. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.60  // v0.20.60 (#80 fix from the .59 BAT): the FH dock is a GAP IN THE COASTAL WALL, and .59 pressed at a point the Garden can never reach from a berth on the wrong side of the railroad. Aaron: "Garden needs to steer into the FH model on the map, not pull up alongside it - drive right into it. Also, make sure Garden is on the correct side of the transcontinental railroad." Both constraints turn out to be one geometric fact. FH's marker (48790,-1788) sits 2.6 km inside ground the Garden mask never permits -- the nearest Garden-navigable water to it, on any bearing, is 2560 units away -- so "drive into the marker" is unreachable by construction. An adjacency scan of every Garden-water cell within 7.7 km of the marker finds exactly ONE place where Garden water touches FH's landmass with no wall cell between: a ~450-unit gap at x~45700, z~+320..+770. Every other cell of that coast is terrain 29, the Esthar mountain wall. That gap is NORTH of the transcontinental railroad, whose bridge (terrain 27) severs the water in a solid 512-unit band at z ~ -1536..-1920; the .59 approach sat at z=-3392, on the far side of the track, exactly as Aaron described. New dock_x/dock_y in the park table names the press point separately from the marker; FH approach moves (46016,-3392) -> (45440,640) and presses east at (46400,560). The NOSE-IN phase drives at the dock point with the wall-guard, LOS clamping and stall/replan machinery all bypassed (grinding into the shore is the objective), and sweeps the mouth over 7 phases of 4 s. Each phase after the first opens by REVERSING for 900 ms: simulated on the engine grid a jammed hull cannot re-aim, and without the back-off phases 1..6 moved it zero units. With it the sweep presses the shore across z = 316..862, covering all three water/land contact cells. Approach drives clean from 7 starts x 4 speed/turn combos, 0 replans. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.59  // v0.20.59 (#80): Fisherman's Horizon is a DOCKING destination, not park-and-walk -- driving the hull into FH is what triggers it (Aaron). That is why no model built on the disembark bit could ever see it: FH's whole platform carries the foot bit and NEVER byte15 bit 0x02, because you do not step off there. New drive_in class in the park table: park_x/park_y is an APPROACH point, arrival is the world map handing off to a field (Poll's exit edge now asks Garden_LeavingIsArrival before calling it an interruption), and reaching the approach point with nothing fired reports honestly rather than claiming an arrival. FH approach (46016,-3392) chosen by the same validate-by-simulation pass as the berths -- the two closer candidates wedge from 8 and 7 of 28 runs. 26 of 39 destinations now reachable. CORROBORATION for the bit's meaning: every landmass with NO disembark bit is one you cannot step onto -- FH, Cactuar Island, both Islands Closest to Heaven/Hell, the Great Salt Lake shore -- and all five carry the Ragnarok LANDING bit (byte14 0x80) instead. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.58  // v0.20.58 (#80): the berth model was wrong in two ways Aaron caught -- Shumi Village, Edea's House and Fisherman's Horizon were reported Ragnarok-only when the Garden can reach them. (1) FOOT connectivity used the 200-unit gate between 128-unit cells, but the FOOT move step is 0x20 = 32 units, so a 128 cell is four steps and the faithful budget is 800 -- the SAME 4x error as the .57 shelf bug, applied to the foot side. At 200 it severed real landmasses: Edea's House was cut off from Chocobo Forest 7's. (2) A berth demanded ONE cell be both Garden-traversable and carry the disembark bit. Those are two different cells -- the hull stops on water it may occupy, the player steps off onto adjacent ground the game allows. Shumi Village's own cell carries the disembark bit and is NOT Garden-traversable, so the old rule could never see it. The berth is now a PAIR (hull cell + step-off cell within 384u, same landmass as the destination) with a 6 km walk cap, and it is VALIDATED BY CONSTRUCTION: every candidate is simulated from 7 starts x 4 speed/turn combos and the first drivable one ships (3 destinations needed a fallback). Result 25 of 39 reachable, and the unreachable set is now exactly the Esthar continent behind its mountain wall plus the offshore islands and the Deep Sea Research Center. Walks collapse: Dollet 3415->1336, Galbadia Station 4891->0, Balamb Town 768->384, Chocobo Forest 5 3238->1669. Matrix 200/200 pairs. OPEN: Fisherman's Horizon still reports unreachable -- there is no disembark-bit cell anywhere within 15 km of it, so no model based on that bit can see it; awaiting Aaron. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.57  // v0.20.57 (#80 fix from the .56 BAT): the continental shelf was reading as a wall. TWO quantisation errors. (1) GdProbe compared the hull cell to the cell one WHOLE 640-unit probe away and applied the 200-unit gate to that jump; a 640u probe spans 2.5 of the 256u cells, so any slope longer than the probe accumulates past 200 even though every cell-to-cell step is legal. At the Centra wedge the per-cell steps were 0/107/166/85/48/62/32/17 and the 2.5-cell jump 299 -- ALL 15 logged "bow blocked" positions reproduce exactly under that rule. GdProbe is now !GdLineClear(), which already marches the gate correctly. (2) The gate was 4x too strict at cell scale: the engine tests |dH| >= 200 between polygons ONE MOVE STEP apart and the Garden step is 0x40 = 64 units, so a 256u cell is FOUR steps and the faithful budget is 800. Map-wide the 200 rule blocks 2,955 cell edges and 3,990 cells; 800 blocks ZERO edges and one degenerate cell -- the Garden permission is the MASK (byte15 bit 0x20), not a slope rule. Berths improve as a result: Galbadia Garden 4529 -> 0 units of walking, Galbadia Station 4891 -> 362; reachability unchanged at 23/39. ALSO fixes a methodology error in the offline sim (it probed the 128u grid, not the 256u grid the game reads, which is why the .56 matrix missed all of this) -- offline/garden_exec2.py is fidelity-matched. Matrix now 184/184 pairs, was 175/184. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.56  // v0.20.56 (#80 fix from the .55 BAT): stepping off the mobile Garden does NOT leave the world map, so the .55 aboard latch -- which only cleared at a world-map ENTRY -- could never clear. The log has no entry of any kind between the Timber park at 13:24:28 and the player walking away; backslash at 816 units from the berth started a SECOND Garden drive instead of the walk-in. Disembark is now detected continuously and WITHOUT the savemap mirror (whose refresh rate is still unproven): while a Garden drive runs or the engine names the Garden in motion the live position IS the hull, so it is recorded; a sustained 450-unit separation from that recorded spot afterwards means the player climbed out. Second, independent fault the same BAT exposed: CheckVehicleChange committed locomotion 3/33/36/39/48 in three seconds while the player merely WALKED (announcing "Car" three times and "Garden" once) and each commit repointed GetWorldMapPosition_Active at that vehicle savemap mirror -- the car mirror is 50 km away at the Missile Base. DEVNOTES has called that byte non-authoritative since .255 but the commit path still trusted it; a non-foot value now has to be seconded by the engine in-motion id or by that vehicle mirror being within 600 units of the player, and logs [VEH-REJECT] when it is not. Also: the stall detector no longer arms during the first 2.5 s of a drive (the .55 Timber run tripped it 3 s in at its own start coordinate, and the spurious replan widened the arrival radius and cost 500 units of berth accuracy), and the already-parked prompt reaches 1500 units. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.55  // v0.20.55 (#80 fix from the .54 BAT): the engine vehicle id at 0x020409E0 is a VEHICLE-IN-MOTION register, not a riding register. The .54 BAT log reads it as 0 at every world-map entry and every catalog build, and 48 exactly once -- four seconds into an active drive -- then 0 again while parked. .54 asked it the question at the two moments it is always 0, which caused all three reported faults at once: the catalog never flipped to the Garden set (stayed at the 4-entry foot list), every destination announced "not reachable" (the reach flood had never run, and an unbuilt map answered false), and the post-park backslash started a FOOT drive on a player still aboard. Now the id is a one-way latched CONFIRMATION, cleared only on positive on-foot evidence from the savemap mirrors at a world-map transition (the same BAT proved they are fresh there: |P-char|=0, |P-bgu|=1015 after disembarking). Catalog is forced to rebuild on every aboard transition; Garden_CellReachable builds its flood on demand instead of answering false; pressing backslash at a berth you are already parked at now says "leave the Garden first" instead of re-driving; arrival radius 384->288 (the .54 park stopped 379u short, turning a 362u walk into 666u). tests/garden_aboard_test.cpp replays the BAT sequence verbatim as a regression guard. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.54  // v0.20.54 (#80): mobile Balamb Garden auto-drive. New subsystem world_garden.inl -- own 256u traversability grid, own clearance-weighted A*, own executor -- gated on the engine vehicle id 0x30 so no line of it runs unless the player is piloting the Garden. Garden walkability is wmx byte15 bit 0x20 and the disembark permission is bit 0x02, both read straight out of FF8_EN.exe 0x53E6B0 / 0x53E730 this session; the same disassembly gives the Ragnarok LANDING mask (byte14 bit 0x80) and re-derives the known car-cannot-enter-forest rule as a cross-check. 23 of 39 catalog destinations have a park point; the other 16 are the Esthar continent and the offshore islands, which is the engine's own encoding of "you need the Ragnarok". Also fixes a latent savemap bug: the per-vehicle position mirrors are int32 X / int32 Y / int16 Z / int16 rotation, not six int16s, so the old reader truncated every coordinate outside +-32767. Offline: C++ and Python grids agree cell-for-cell (677,934 / 65,035, zero disagreements). NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.20.53  // v0.20.53 (WS1 Step 1.4, OBSERVE-ONLY): co-located dedup groundwork. The existing dedup collapses only ONE cross-type case (a JSM object coincident with an interaction line); the plan targets one thing surfaced as line+object+controller. New CatalogEntryPos() resolves a uniform position for any entry, and a [CO-LOCATED] pass at the end of DedupeCatalog logs the co-located pairs the existing dedup MISSES (with which representation it would keep). Log-only -- nothing is removed; a BAT reveals which pairs are truly one thing vs distinct-but-close. NOT MSVC/BAT-d.
// v0.18.3.308: #113 -- the recovery nudge was perpendicular to the WRONG EDGE. neighbor[e] pairs with edge (vertex[e], vertex[(e+1)%3]) -- the convention v0.17.9.14 already fixed in FindPortal after the Track A investigation -- but the even-phase recovery nudge in field_nav_autodrive.inl still derived the "shared edge" from the OLD (e+1, e+2) pair, so every nudge was computed perpendicular to a segment that is NOT the border with the next corridor triangle (often a wall). DIRECT PROOF from the .306 BAT log, no inference: the 27-second wedge at tri 72 fired 20 identical nudges logged as "tri 72->71 dir=(0.90,-0.44)"; recomputing both rules offline against the committed walkmesh extract shows the old pair produces EXACTLY (0.90,-0.44) while the true shared edge (-1433,-1590)-(-781,-1402) gives (-0.28,0.96), pointing at tri 71 as intended. Convention validated mechanically: across gpbig1a+gpbig2a the old pair mismatches the neighbor triangle vertex set on 184/186 links, the fixed pair on 0/370. #100 is therefore a THREE-part failure and this closes the last part: (1) axes rotated 90 deg pre-calibration (#110, still gated), (2) recovery re-path goal instability (#112, fixed in .307), (3) nudges aimed by the wrong edge (this fix). Also corrected the same derivation in the two DISABLED blocks (corridor steering, wall-avoidance bias) so re-enabling them cannot resurrect the bug, and in field_nav_settriangle.inl whose COORD diagnostic logged the wrong crossed-edge midpoint (log-only consumer). OFFLINE VALIDATION (new, this session): built a host harness (g++) compiling the REAL field_nav_pathfinding.inl against the real gpbig1a walkmesh and simulating the drive loop with a wall-sliding kinematic player; all 84 triangle-center starts reach the Stairs-down-to-Floor-5 line under the .307+.308 recovery model; the harness also reproduced the .306 goal flip-flop (goals 20/1/22 vs stable 48). Point-player sim cannot model engine collision radius at rail corners, so the machine BAT remains the ground truth for the remaining wedges. BAT: same as .307 -- calibrate with a couple of steps, then drive to the stairs; in any wedge expect the [drive] recovery nudge log to show DIFFERENT, corridor-pointing directions instead of one direction repeated 20 times.
// v0.18.3.307: #112 -- recovery must CONVERGE: every recovery re-path now aims at the same goal triangle the drive started with, and revisiting a recently-stuck triangle no longer refills the recovery budget. ONE change (two inseparable halves of "recovery stops undoing itself"). .306 BAT: Aaron reported NO more "Player position lost" (#111 CONFIRMED -- zero occurrences in the log, the drive survived the whole session) but "navigation really struggled to actually get around... stuck on objects, like the rails around the stairs and the short wall around the hole in the middle of the floor". The log splits that experience into two distinct faults. FAULT 1 (pre-existing, #110 territory, NOT touched here): every gpbig1a drive before the 15:11:44 empirical calibration ran on ca-quantized axes that are 90 degrees wrong on that field (the calibration proves it: camRight (1,0)->(0,1)), so the drive pressed keys rotated a quarter turn and ground along the rails/knee wall -- drive 1 ran EAST while the stairs were south-west, drive 4 pressed UL into the same wall for 27 s with moveDist=0 and 20 identical nudges (the #100 signature). Meanwhile the SAME build drove gpbig2a (axes correct at load, flipBand=0) flawlessly through 6 waypoints to the exit on the first post-restart attempt -- same code, adjacent field, the difference is the axes. The .305 cache cannot help the FIRST load of a session because it dies with the process; the real fix stays gated on #110 independent data (gpgmn3/gpbrdg1/gppark1 still unvisited). FAULT 2 (NEW, #112, fixed here): the two post-calibration drives had correct axes and STILL failed -- the final drive ping-ponged between tri 8/9/10 for 40 s, and the goal alternation in the log is the mechanism. For trigger targets UpdateAutoDrive() offsets tx/tz 300 units past the line center along the direction FROM THE PLAYER'S CURRENT POSITION ("aim through the line"); the odd-phase and nudge-fallback recovery re-paths seeded FindNearestTriangle() with that MOVING point, so the A* goal depended on where the player stood: goal=1 (from the west cluster) pulls wp (-2361,-503) WEST, goal=22 (from the east cluster) pulls wp (-1683,69) NORTH-EAST, and each recovery routed the player toward the opposite cluster forever. Drive start and the wiggle-completion re-path both use the raw center and agree on goal=48 -- the recovery block was the odd site out (the "check ALL passes writing the same field" rule, again). FIX A: capture rawTx/rawTz before the offset, seed every recovery re-path with it. FIX B (inseparable, the #100 half): each ping-pong hop changed the walkmesh triangle, and the v0.17.6.1 tri-advance reset treated every hop as corridor progress and refilled MAX_RECOVERY_PHASES -- so "Stuck." was UNREACHABLE and the drive bounced silently until the 40 s timeout. A 4-entry ring of recently-stuck triangles (field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl, cleared at drive start in handlekeys) now gates the reset: fresh triangles still reset, revisits log "recovery counter NOT reset" and let the phase climb to the cap, so the drive either progresses or honestly says "Stuck. Distance remaining: N" within ~30 recovery windows. Waypoint advances keep their own independent phase reset, so genuine corridor traversal keeps its budget. Nothing lands in field_navigation.cpp (81,792/81,920 -- still the next file to split, #37). BAT: walk a few steps on gpbig1a first so "Camera calibrated." fires, THEN F9 to the stairs -- expect every recovery [A*] line to show the SAME goal, "NOT reset" lines where the old build ping-ponged, and either arrival at the stairs or an honest Stuck within ~40 s. A few steps on gpgmn3/gpbrdg1/gppark1 would finally give #110 its independent verdict.
// v0.18.3.306: #111 -- auto-drive no longer dies on a ONE-FRAME player-position dropout. ONE change. .305 BAT: Aaron got 11 "Player position lost" stops in four minutes and said he has been seeing it more and more, and guessed it was tied to crossing some underlying line on the field. He is essentially right, and the log shows the shape of it exactly: the position is readable on the tick BEFORE and the tick AFTER every single one. 14:51:39 [drive-vec] t=150 tri=3 pp=(-2386,-372) -> next tick "Player position lost." -> 14:51:42 [A*] start=2 fine again. 14:49:21 [drive-vec] t=270 tri=55 pp=(1627,275) -> next tick lost. GetEntityPos() only fails in-range when the triangle id at 0x1FA reads 0 ("not yet placed") or the Others base pointer is momentarily null -- both of which are things the engine does WHILE re-resolving the player walkmesh placement, i.e. exactly when crossing into a new triangle or when a line fires. At 14:51:39 the drive had just advanced THREE waypoints in one second (tri 56 -> 6 -> 3), so it was crossing triangles fast. It is a race between our per-frame poll and the engine placement update, which is why it gets more likely the more auto-drive is used and the faster the player moves -- matching "happening more and more". THE FIX DOES NOT DEPEND ON KNOWING WHICH OF THE TWO READS DIPS: one failed sample is not evidence the player is gone, and this log proves that directly. A dropout now pauses the drive exactly as an open dialog already does (release held keys, freeze the stuck counter, keep the drive alive) and only gives up after 30 consecutive bad ticks (~0.5 s) -- far longer than any observed dropout (all were a SINGLE tick), far shorter than a player would wait wondering what happened. If the position really is gone for good, IsOnField() and the field-transition handler stop the drive on their own paths, so nothing depended on this one being trigger-happy. Also SPLIT THE MESSAGE: s_playerEntityIdx < 0 now says "Player entity unknown." because it and the GetEntityPos failure both said "Player position lost.", so the .305 log could not tell me which fired and I had to reason it out from surrounding lines -- they are different faults. Logs [drive] player position unreadable -- PAUSING with the raw othersBase/tri values once per dropout, and a recovered-after-N-ticks line, so the next BAT CONFIRMS the mechanism rather than leaving it inferred. New ReadPlayerPlacementRaw() lives in field_nav_autodrive_helpers.inl and owns its own SEH: MSVC C2712 forbids __try in a function needing C++ object unwinding and UpdateAutoDrive() has none today, so the SEH stays out of it (same pattern as GetEntityPos). .305 BAT RESULTS, all three confirmed: #109 the axes cache WORKS -- one calibration on gpbig1a then "cached axes re-applied on field load" on FOUR subsequent loads with no relearn, matching Aaron's "it did need to calibrate on the left side, but after that its directions seemed accurate". #110 fired its first verdict and it matches the prediction exactly: gpbig1a rawCA=+38.32 quantizerChose=+0 alternative=+90 measured=+90 -> ALTERNATIVE CORRECT, threshold is below 45. NOTE that is still the field the hypothesis was DERIVED from, so it is consistency, not independent confirmation -- gpgmn3/gpbrdg1/gppark1 were not visited and the threshold change stays unshipped until one of them reports. gpbig2a (flipBand=0) never needed correcting, which is consistent but weak. #107/#108 unchanged and still in place.
// v0.18.3.305: #109 -- the .304 calibration is CORRECT but it is thrown away on every field load, and the prison shaft reloads its field on every floor change. That is the whole of Aaron's "you have to approach the downward stairs in a particular direction and the mod got hung up on it". The log settles it without inference. gpbig1a loaded EIGHT times in four and a half minutes (plus three gpbig2a loads) while the calibration fired only TWICE, and the two lists correlate perfectly: 12:27:14 load -> 12:27:22 calibrated -> the 12:27:28/29/45/51 drives are the ones Aaron said were working; 12:28:10 load -> never recalibrated -> 12:28:44 drive -> 20 identical recovery attempts -> "Gave up. Distance remaining: 999." at 12:28:53; 12:29:07 load (floor 4) -> never recalibrated -> 12:29:08 drive -> wedged AT the stairs, 56 identical position samples. With the axes wrong the drive presses the wrong arrow keys, so whether the player makes progress depends on how the axis error happens to line up with the approach -- it is not the staircase geometry being fussy, it is the steering being rotated. Caught red-handed at 12:29:09: the drive wanted world (-53,-110) (west-south) and with the true mapping (screen-up = world -X, screen-right = world +Y) that is UP-LEFT, but using the stale ca-quantized axes it pressed DOWN-LEFT, which moves world (+X,-Y) -- the opposite in X. Player froze at (-2154,-100), 12 units short of crossing line1, and stayed there. FIX = cache the corrected axes per fieldId and re-apply them at field load (new CamAxesCacheStore/Lookup, 24 entries, round-robin). Placed in field_nav_helpers.inl, NOT field_navigation.cpp: that file is at 81,792 of its 81,920 hard limit and DEVNOTES says split it before landing anything there rather than shaving comments to fit, so I put the new code where there is room instead. helpers.inl is included ahead of both fieldscripts.inl (applies the cache) and observe.inl (fills it). SECOND, INSEPARABLE PART -- the one-shot lock becomes a per-load CAP of 3, and "empirical-cached"/"empirical-corrected" join "ca-quantized" as contradictable sources. This is not scope creep, it is what makes cacheing safe: the .304 BAT applied ONE CORRECTION THAT WAS 180 DEGREES WRONG (12:25:38, arrow=DOWN consensus (-0.872,0.490) = world 151deg -> camRight=(0,-1), versus 12:27:22 arrow=UP consensus (-0.995,0.100) = world 174deg -> camRight=(0,+1)); opposite keys cannot both move the player the same way, so one sample was a wall slide and the DOWN answer was the bad one. Under a hard one-shot lock that wrong answer stood for the rest of the visit, and cacheing it unchanged would have made it stand for the rest of the SESSION. A 180-degree error diverges far past the 45-degree threshold, so it now self-repairs on the next clean sample, and the cap bounds oscillation. ALSO SEEN, NOT FIXED THIS CYCLE (filed, belongs to #100): the wedge recovery repeats an IDENTICAL nudge -- same triangle pair, same direction vector, same 8 ticks -- up to 20 times. tri 10->54 dir=(0.96,0.28) ten times; tri 75->74 dir=(-1.00,0.02) ten times. A deterministic action that failed cannot succeed by repetition, and it burned 38 seconds before giving up. Expected on BAT: [NAV-CAL] cached axes re-applied on the SECOND and later entries to each shaft field, no relearn wait, and the drive behaving on arrival the way it behaved after calibration in .304. ALSO FOLDED IN, LOG-ONLY, ZERO BEHAVIOUR CHANGE -- #110, the CA snap-threshold probe. Aaron asked whether the field data itself could give us the orientation instead of a diagnostic that needs many sessions, so I extracted the .ca camera record for ALL 894 fields straight out of field.fs offline (offline/CAMERA_ANGLES.csv + CAMERA_ANGLE_SURVEY.md; parser validated against the mod's own [CA] log lines -- gpbig1a 38.32, bgryo1_4 46.60, bghall_1 7.73, bg2f_1 65.38, bgroom_1 -62.49, bghall_4 23.80 all match exactly). Two things fell out that the v0.17.5 quantizer was written without. (1) FF8 cameras are NOT near-axis-aligned: only 35% of fields sit within 5 degrees of a cardinal and 44% discard more than 20, so the 90-degree snap is making a large correction across most of the game rather than tidying a rounding error. (2) The engine's threshold is NOT 45 degrees. gpbig1a's measured response gives camRight ~= 90 (RIGHT -> world 84, DOWN -> world -5.7, two independent arrows agreeing) while its raw CA is 38.32 and roundf picks 0; against the fields known to work (bghall_1 7.73, ggroom1 17.55, ggsta1 19.80, bghall_4 23.80 snapping to 0 correctly; bgryo1_4 46.60, bg2f_1 65.38, bgroom_1 -62.49 snapping up correctly) the true threshold brackets to (23.8, 38.32]. I am NOT shipping that threshold change: it would flip 93 fields, and although EVERY field known to work today is outside that band and gpbig1a is inside it -- which is the strongest evidence it is safe -- 93 fields is far too large a blast radius to bet on ONE confirmed data point. So this ships the measurement. [CAM-SNAP] logs the raw CA angle, both snap candidates and a flipBand flag at every field load; [CAM-SNAP-VERDICT] fires when a calibration lands and states whether the engine agreed with the quantizer's pick, with the one it rejected, or with neither. Every field that calibrates anywhere in the game is now a data point, so the answer accrues during ordinary play instead of needing a survey campaign. PREDICTIONS ON RECORD: gpgmn3 (+38.80) and gpbrdg1 (+129.10) should report ALTERNATIVE exactly as gpbig1a does; gppark1 (-46.80) and gpbig2a (-24.76) should report QUANTIZER. If gppark1 or gpbig2a also reports ALTERNATIVE the model is wrong and no threshold change ships. SEPARATE BUG FOUND BY THE SAME SURVEY, filed not fixed: 45 fields carry more than one camera and LoadCameraAxes reads camera 0 only; 18 of them have cameras that snap to DIFFERENT cardinals, so a script-driven camera switch puts the axes a full cardinal out with nothing in the log -- glwitch1, glstage1 and ggroom1 are on that list and all three appear in the sewer/gate work.
// v0.18.3.304: #107 + #108 -- the stairs LIST correctly now but Aaron could not GET to them, and the two reasons are unrelated to each other and to the catalog. TWO changes this cycle, deliberately, flagged: neither is testable without the other (with the false arrival fixed the drive still steers on 50-degree-wrong axes and wanders; with the axes fixed the drive still stops short and says it arrived) and they ride the same walk. Different files, different log tags, so the BAT attributes each independently. #107 FALSE ARRIVAL (field_nav_autodrive.inl): the trigger-line arrival test was the INFINITE-LINE side test -- the FIFTH site of that bug and the first outside a catalog filter (v0.17.8.10 gateways, .268 interactions, .278 exits, #94 events all got bounded; this one was missed). gpbig1a line1 is (-2141,-89)->(-2158,-305): 217 units long and almost parallel to the Y axis, so its infinite extension is a wall across the whole map at X=-2150 and walking to ANY point past that X flips the sign. Log: player started at (-2221,-1089) crossNow=-280, reached (-2162,-1161) crossNow=+13688 -> "Arrived.", keys released, ~960 units from the staircase. In a catalog filter this bug makes a row flicker; here it is a confident spoken claim that the destination has been reached, which a blind player has no way to catch. Fix = keep the sign flip AND require the player to be alongside the segment (project onto the line, foot of projection within it, 15% end margin). Failing the bound is the safe failure: the drive keeps walking and the engine's own MAPJUMP ends it. #108 CAMERA AXES (field_nav_observe.inl): NAV-OBSERVE logged 20+ samples on gpbig1a diverging 48-84 degrees and never converging; gpbig2a the same. Root cause is the 90-degree CA quantizer -- camRight angle +38.3deg, only 6.7deg from the 45deg coin-flip boundary, snapped to 0 and threw away 38 degrees (gpbig2a: -24.8 -> 0). NOT fixing the quantizer: the raw CA does not match the engine either (measured screen-up ~145deg, raw predicts ~128, quantized predicts 90), so "use the raw value" trades one wrong answer for a less wrong one. The real defect is that the closed-loop empirical calibrator built for exactly this was hard-gated to s_camAxesSource=="identity", so a ca-quantized field could NEVER be corrected however badly the engine contradicted it -- it watched all 20+ samples and was forbidden to act. Now a ca-quantized field becomes eligible once the engine contradicts it: triggering sample AND consensus must both diverge >= 45deg. 45 is not tuned, it is the cardinal sector boundary -- below it the spoken cardinal is still right so there is nothing to fix; at or above it the cardinal is provably wrong. REGRESSION SAFETY: the gate measures harm on THIS field rather than guessing which fields look risky -- correct axes produce consensus agreeing with the prediction, so the gate stays shut and nothing is written. I first tried gating on "the quantizer discarded a lot of angle" and rejected it: bgryo1_4 discards 43.4deg and navigates fine, so that test would have fired on a healthy field. Expected: consensus UP ~146deg -> camDown snaps (1,0), camRight (0,1), and the stairs then read RIGHT and slightly DOWN from Aaron's position -- which is what he said they should be, and 90 degrees off the "north" he was given.
// v0.18.3.303: #91 R1 completion -- the DOWN staircase now lists too. .302 shipped the naming and Aaron BAT'd it: "Stairs up to Floor 7" was there and in the right place, the down stairs were not there at all. The log said why, precisely. The STAIRS log line for line2 read higher=0 lower=1 -- my own comparison loop had FOUND line1, read its Z and counted it, so line1 was present, active, SCREEN_BOUND and sharing the destination. It was therefore being dropped further up the block, silently, before the stair path could name it -- and it was still coming out the other side as "Interaction 1". One filter fits all three facts, and the log confirms it rather than my inferring it: '[JSMScan] REQ-interact: Line ent1 squall REQs interactive entity -> hasDialogReqTarget=1', and [LINE-PAIR] maps gpbig1a line1 -> jsm1 'squall'. line2 maps to jsm2 'zell', which has no REQ target. That is the whole asymmetry: the up stairs are a plain walk-across line, the down stairs run a scripted descent WITH DIALOGUE, so the down line legitimately REQs a dialog-bearing entity and got classified dual-purpose. The dual-purpose rule ("if it talks it is an Interaction, not an Exit") is right for a dormitory bed and wrong for a staircase, which is the only way off the floor. Two edits, both narrowly scoped to prison-shaft self-destination lines so beds and every other dual-purpose line behave exactly as before. (1) field_nav_catalog_triglines.inl: hoist the shaft-staircase test ABOVE the hasDialogReqTarget continue, and exempt shaft stairs from it, logging when the exemption fires so the next BAT can see it. (2) field_nav_catalog.inl: the Interaction block has TWO branches that can set isInteractive, and .302 guarded only the second (self-loop); the down stairs took the FIRST (hasDialogReqTarget) and kept emitting "Interaction 1". Now guarded before both. This is the DEVNOTES rule "when a fix targets one of several passes writing the same field, check ALL of them" -- I wrote that rule and then broke it in .302. Expected on BAT: gpbig1a lists BOTH "Stairs down to Floor 5" and "Stairs up to Floor 7" on Floor 6, no "Interaction 1", and two [refresh] STAIRS log lines (line1 higher=1 lower=0 -> down; line2 higher=0 lower=1 -> up).
// v0.18.3.302: #91 R1 -- the prison stairs are finally in the catalog, named by direction and destination floor. ONE change. Aaron's clue closed it: he remembered the game answering "(No sense going back up.)" when he crossed a line trying to go back upstairs, and that line plus the SETLINE geometry identifies both staircases. gpbig1a carries TWO self-destination SCREEN_BOUND lines and BOTH were lost -- line1 surfaced as the meaningless "Interaction 1" and line2 did not surface at all, so the way up and the way down were between them invisible. WHICH IS WHICH: line1 centre (-2150,-197) z=-68/-55 is DOWN; line2 centre (-2276,269) z=+352/+391 is UP. Two independent confirmations: (a) all three floor changes in the .301 BAT fired from (-2400,-470), south of and just past line1, floor decreasing each time; (b) the "(No sense going back up.)" refusal came from the NORTH line, so north is up and it is story-gated at that point in the plot. THE RULE IS THE Z ONE -- of the self-destination lines on a field, the higher-Z one goes up. That is exactly the discriminator I tried on INF gateways in .298 and had to withdraw when their lineZ came back (0,0) everywhere; SETLINE data is the opposite, the heights are real and ~430 units apart here, about one floor. Same idea, correct data source. Deliberately NOT keyed on line index or SYM ('squall'/'zell' here, meaningless) so it carries to the other shaft screens on its own, and direction is only claimed when a counterpart EXISTS to be higher or lower than -- a lone line or a tie gets the unqualified "Stairs" rather than a coin-flip, because announcing "Stairs up" at the down stairs is worse than saying nothing. Also (R4) stairs are EXEMPT from the bounded separation test: gpbig1a's west wall carries five lines within ~500 units, so whichever one the player is not standing next to reads as separated by its neighbours -- which is precisely how line2 vanished while line1 survived. That test has now produced a false positive in FOUR blocks (.17.8.10 gateways, .268 interactions, .278 exits, #94 events) and a cluster of parallel lines on one wall is its worst case; losing the only way off a floor is far worse than listing a staircase you have to walk around to. The Interaction block no longer promotes shaft self-loops, so nothing appears twice; the dormitory bed and every other self-loop field is untouched. New ReadShaftFloor() in field_nav_helpers.inl reads varblock 0x01B5 + 1, same source as the .301 announcement. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: on any shaft floor open the catalog -- expect "Stairs up to Floor N+1" AND "Stairs down to Floor N-1" as two separate entries, plus a [refresh] STAIRS line per staircase showing meanZ and the higher/lower counts. Walk to each and confirm the direction is right; if they are swapped the Z sign convention is inverted and it is a one-line flip. NOTE the up stairs may refuse with "(No sense going back up.)" depending on story state -- that is the game talking, not the label being wrong. Prior: v0.18.3.301: THREE separable units, all serving the same walk so one BAT exercises all of them; each carries its own log tag so a regression stays attributable. Aaron described the shaft geometry and it reframed everything: gpbig1a/gpbig2a are the LEFT and RIGHT halves of ONE circular walkway around a central hole, joined by a crossing at the TOP of the circle and another at the BOTTOM. The INF data corroborates exactly -- each half carries two gateways at mirrored heights (y ~= +1800 / -1850) -- and it explains why 10 crossings produced 0 floor changes. (1) #90 FLOOR ANNOUNCEMENT, the headline fix: the shaft changes floor WITHOUT changing fieldId so FieldAnnounce's fieldId-change trigger never fired and 23 floor changes announced NOTHING; the announce key is now (fieldId, floor) and the floor comes from varblock 0x01B5 + 1 (the +1 pinned by the .299 auto-capture -- "Floor 6" while the varblock held 5, "Floor 4" while it held 3). The ring halves DROP their archive ordinal entirely and speak "Floor 4, left side" / "Floor 4, right side" -- "Galbadia D-District Prison 3" is neither a floor nor a place and Aaron heard it alternating with "...5" thirteen times during a three-floor descent. Other shaft fields keep their name and gain ", Floor N". (2) #91 R3 GATEWAY SPLIT + ring crossing names: grouping on destFieldId alone collapsed two exits 3,644 units apart into one entry at their midpoint -- open floor belonging to neither, which auto-drive walked to. A candidate now joins a group only if also within 800 units of it (genuine duplicates are < 200 apart). Both then need distinct names or the .270 exact-name dedupe drops one, so they become "Top crossing" / "Bottom crossing" -- position on the ring, which is the only fact that distinguishes them, since both land on the SAME floor and a floor label would be actively wrong. Derived from the gateway's own Y, not the camera, so it is stable wherever the player stands. ASSUMPTION FLAGGED: larger Y = top. The codebase says "Y = screen-vertical" but never documents the SIGN, and I am not repeating the .298 gateway-Z mistake of assuming one -- both centres are logged and a swap is a one-line flip. (3) #91 R1 groundwork, DIAGNOSTIC ONLY: [MAPJUMP-HOOK] now logs player XY at fire time. gpbig1a has TWO self-destination lines ~470 units apart on the west wall (line1 (-2150,-197), line2 (-2275,269)); one is up and one is down and NOTHING static separates them -- gateway Z is (0,0) everywhere and the nav_data trail only records triangle changes so it went quiet 13-49 s before each jump and never caught the crossing. Position at the fire, next to the floor the jump produces, pins it in one ordinary descent. DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS BUILD: naming the stairs. Surfacing them needs R1 plus the reachability drop that swallowed line2 (R4), and until the diagnostic says which is which they would both read "Stairs" with no way to choose -- so they ship next cycle, labelled "Stairs to Floor N" as Aaron asked. BAT: walk the shaft with -/= on BOTH halves. Expect "Floor N, left side"/"right side" on every floor change INCLUDING the same-fieldId ones, two crossings instead of one merged phantom, [refresh] INF-GW gw SPLIT and ring crossings named lines, and the first-ever GATEWAY grp rows in the dump (#92, still never executed). TELL ME IF TOP/BOTTOM ARE SWAPPED. Prior: v0.18.3.300: FIRST BEHAVIOUR CHANGE of this arc -- #91 R2, the stale-gateway rule is scoped back to its evidence base. ONE change. The .299 BAT caught that rule deleting a REAL exit: on gpbig2a (Prison 5) the catalog held exactly TWO entries on all five visits -- a cell door and the Directory -- and no listed way back to the rest of the floor, which Aaron then walked through anyway 30 seconds later, proving it live. The premise is inverted there: gpbig2a has ONE screen-bound line and it points at a CELL (965); that single unrelated line arms fieldHasLineExits, which then deletes the only real exit off the screen. The shaft screens use BOTH mechanisms for DIFFERENT exits, which the rule never contemplated. Fix: the rule now also requires s_gatewayCount <= 1, i.e. the single-gateway shape it was actually validated on. Re-reading its own evidence block: it was derived from and has only ever been needed by glfurin1, which carries ONE gateway; every other field listed (glpreo1/2/3, glprefr2, glstage1) resolves zero line exits so fieldHasLineExits is false and it never fires there; glwitch1 has both but its gateway dest EQUALS its line dest so it is kept by the agreement test either way. Provably inert on the whole documented evidence base, preserves glfurin1, fixes both prison screens (INF parsed: 2 active gateways on gpbig1a AND gpbig2a). MUST be s_gatewayCount (raw) not s_dedupGatewayCount -- the prison's two gateways share a destination and merge into ONE group, so the dedup count is 1 there and would gate nothing. This is the same move .291 made when it rescoped the addr-as-literal heuristic to bg* after it fabricated a Centra Ruins exit. DIRECTION OF RISK: a pure narrowing -- it can only ADD exits, never remove one; worst case is a ghost gateway reappearing on some unvisited multi-gateway field, versus losing a real exit which is this project's worst failure mode (#88, ladline7, gpbig2a). Logs a [refresh] stale-gateway rule NOT applied line when the gate engages, so the BAT can see it. Also settled by the .299 auto-capture and now closed: the displayed prison floor is the DIALOG SLOT FIELD_VAR_TABLE_BASE[0x20], read straight off two screenshots ("Floor 6" with slot=6/vb=5, "Floor 4" with slot=4/vb=3), so varblock 0x01B5 is floor MINUS ONE -- #89/#90 both have their answer and the announcement needs vb+1. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: walk the shaft with -/= pressed on BOTH screens. Expect gpbig2a to gain 'Exit to Galbadia D-District Prison 3' (2 entries -> 3) and gpbig1a to gain 'Exit to Prison 5', a [refresh] stale-gateway rule NOT applied line on each, and the first-ever GATEWAY grp row in the catalog dump (#92, still unexercised because this rule was deleting every gateway before the dump could print it) -- which should read merged=2 and is also the first direct evidence of R3. Prior: v0.18.3.299: DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY, one addition on top of .298 -- [FLOOR-SHOT], the mod takes the screenshot itself. The .298 BAT found the prison floor number in two places that disagree by exactly one: the engine display slot FIELD_VAR_TABLE_BASE[0x20] read 6->5->4 while field varblock 0x01B5 (437) read 4->3->2, stepping together on every MAPJUMP3 self-jump. Only one is the number printed on screen and picking wrong makes every future floor announcement off by one. The obvious settler -- F11 while the Floor window is up -- is exactly what this bug makes impossible: the window renders 190+ times per floor but decodes to the bare string "Floor" every time, so the dedup eats every repeat and Aaron heard it ONCE for a three-floor descent. He cannot hear when it is on screen, so he cannot time a keypress to it; asking him to was my mistake. FloorShotProbe() now fires from the show_dialog text path, matching the RAW FF8 bytes 4A 6A 6D 6D 70 20 ("Floor ") so no decode is needed, placed BEFORE the hash and decoded dedups because the raw buffer is byte-identical on every floor (the engine substitutes the digits at render time; they are never in the text). Rate-limited on the VALUE not the frame -- one capture per distinct (slot, varblock) pair, i.e. exactly once per floor -- and BOTH values go in the filename (floor_HHMMSS_mmm_s20-5_vb-4.png) so the correlation is readable straight off the directory listing. Async capture with a 30-frame delay, the same mechanism the Scan UI uses, because this runs on the game thread inside a MinHook callback where the blocking variant would stall the renderer. Gated to prison fieldIds (0x0319-0x032E, 0x03C5) and behind FLOOR_SHOT_PROBE, inert everywhere else. Also adds SafeReadByte() alongside the existing SafeReadDword(). g++ compile-clean and behaviour-tested on the extracted function: fires on a Floor window, dedupes repeats, ignores non-Floor windows, re-fires when the value changes, ignores non-prison fields. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. BAT: walk any prison shaft floors; expect one floor_*.png per floor plus a [FLOOR-SHOT] line naming both candidates. ALSO STILL NEEDED from .298: press -/= once on a shaft screen -- the catalog was never opened last run so the #92 dump fix and #91's R1/R2/R3 are still untested. Prior: v0.18.3.298: DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY build, zero behaviour change by design -- the two read-only probes from the 2026-07-31 BAT review, combined because both are pure observation and neither can influence the other. (1) [VAR-DROP] (#89): the D-District Prison floor indicator is "Floor " + a 0x04 numeric insert with param 0x30, and FieldExpandRawVars drops any param outside [0x20..0x27] -- so Aaron heard a bare "Floor" on all six occurrences, and the same branch silently eats the quantity at field draw points ("Laguna stocked  Curagas"). Widening the range blind is NOT safe: if params >= 0x28 resolve through a different table we would splice an unrelated number into the sentence, and a confidently wrong floor is worse than no floor. So this logs the dropped param, the dword the engine WOULD have read at 0x01D2B4B0 + param*4, and a sweep of table[0x20..0x3F]; throttled to one line per param per 2 s. (2) [FLOOR-PROBE] (#90): the shaft changes floor WITHOUT changing fieldId -- 23 of the BAT's transitions were 795 -> 795, and FieldAnnounce bails on curId == lastAnnounced, so those floor changes announced nothing at all. Added a prison-gated (0x0319-0x032E, 0x03C5) byte dump of varblock 0x0190-0x01CF at MAPJUMP fire time, covering the 0x01A5 SET3 anchor and the 0x01B4/0x01B5 state pair; the existing 0x80-0xFE word window already covers 0x00C8/0x00D4 for diffing. (3) [INF-GW-Z] (#91): LoadINFGateways read both exit-line Z values and threw them away as "not used for 2D nav" and never parsed destinationPoint at +12 at all. True for navigation, false for IDENTIFICATION: on the shaft, up and down from gpbig1a BOTH land in gpbig2a, so destination fieldId cannot separate them and the catalog merges them into one entry at a midpoint belonging to neither staircase. Trigger lines already keep their Z and the two gpbig1a stairs read z ~= -61 and z ~= +371 there, stable across all 15 visits. Now stored on GatewayInfo (lineZ1/lineZ2/destX/destY/destZ) and logged on a SEPARATE line so the existing [INF-GW] line stays byte-comparable against every prior BAT log. Nothing consumes it yet -- whether +Z means up in field walkmesh space is NOT established (DEVNOTES records UP = neg-Y for wmx.obj, which does not transfer) and the BAT settles the sign, it is not assumed. (4) [refresh] catalog dump fix (#92): the dump tested entityIdx <= -300 before anything looked at -400, so every INF gateway exit fell into the JSM branch, computed slot 100 + d, failed the bounds check and printed NOTHING -- silently. Every catalog dump this project has ever logged under-reports by the number of gateway exits; the prison blocks claim 6 entries and print 5, and the merged phantom staircase that walked Aaron off the floor was invisible. Gateways are now identified by gatewayIdx (exact: every non-gateway injection site sets it to -1, only the gateway block sets it >= 0) rather than by sentinel arithmetic, which also removes a real range collision -- JSM uses -300 - slot with MAX_JSM_ENTITIES 128, so slot 100 also lands on -400. An entry matching no known range now prints as UNKNOWN instead of vanishing. BAT: ride the prison lift, stop on 3-4 floors, F11 at each so the on-screen floor is recorded, and walk both staircases on one shaft screen. Expect [VAR-DROP] "would read" to match the screenshots, [FLOOR-PROBE] to move between floors, [INF-GW-Z] to show opposite dz signs on the two same-destination gateways, and GATEWAY rows to finally appear in the catalog dump. g++ compile-clean on the extracted probe (format strings and bounds verified by running it). NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. field_navigation.cpp untouched -- it is still at 81,792/81,920 and must be split (#37) before anything lands there. Prior: v0.18.3.297: #85 state exclusion ENABLED -- the catalog now lists only the world-state that actually exists. Aaron ran the full maze on .296; the refined rule was correct on every entity in both observed glwater3 states (standing byte=0: ladline5 SUPPRESS + ladline6/saku3 KEEP; fallen byte=9: all KEEP), and the naive rule would have deleted Gate 3 in both. glwater2 (Sewer 3, the 3-gate risk field) still produced no verdict -- he walked through it twice without opening the catalog, the SECOND time that has cost a BAT, because the probe lives in RefreshCatalog(). Rather than spend a third cycle on it I closed the risk structurally with an ANCHOR GUARD: a group is only acted on when at least one member value equals the live byte. Every live read of varblock 340 across every BAT has been 0 or 9 while glwater2 wants 25/13/16, so glwater2 can never anchor and is provably untouched -- proof rather than another round-trip. Three-part rule now acting: (1) only a CONDITIONAL entity (JPF between guard and SET3) may be suppressed; (2) the group must be anchored; (3) suppress a conditional member whose value != live byte. Suppression sets s_jsmStateSuppressed[] and skips catalog injection only -- position data is deliberately left intact for other passes. field_navigation.cpp went 63 bytes over the CI hard fail and I trimmed MY OWN new comment rather than gutting existing explanation; it is now 81,792/81,920 and is the next file that must be split (#37) before anything else lands there. g++ compile-clean. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. Prior: #85 the .295 DRY RUN RAN AND REFUTED THE NAIVE RULE -- on the very field it was designed for. glwater3 with the ladder standing (live byte 0): ladline6 wants 0 -> KEEP (correct), ladline5 wants 9 -> SUPPRESS (correct), saku3 wants 3 -> SUPPRESS (WRONG, saku3 is Gate 3, a real gate). Exactly the outcome the dry run existed to catch, and the reason it did not ship as a fix in .295. The refutation handed over the real discriminator: whether a JPF (jump-if-false, opcode 0x02) sits between the guard read and the SET3. With a JPF the placement is genuinely CONDITIONAL; without one the SET3 runs every time and the guard read is feeding some later branch instead. Verified on the real archive, and it is NOT a ladline/saku split: conditional = glwater3 ladline5 + glwater2 saku2; unconditional = glwater3 ladline6 + saku3, glwater2 saku3 + saku4, glwater5 saku8. Added JSMEntityInfo.stateGuardConditional; the refined rule is "only a conditional entity may ever be suppressed", which keeps saku3 and both glwater2 gates while still dropping the wrong ladder state. STILL LOGGING ONLY -- the dry run now prints naive and refined verdicts side by side and flags every disagreement, so the next BAT says whether refined matches reality before anything acts. g++ compile-clean, guard+conditional capture verified on the real archive. NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. Prior: #85 state-guard CAPTURE + DRY-RUN diagnostic. The `.294` split BAT came back clean (catalog byte-identical to `.293`), so this build starts the state fix -- but stops short of acting, on evidence. Added JSMEntityInfo.hasStateGuard/stateVarAddr/stateVarValue, captured in the scanner from the FIRST `PSHM_L <addr>` + `PSHM_W <value>` pair in the method that calls SET3 (first, not last: ladline5 tests 340 vs 9 THEN 339 vs 64, and the second is true in the OTHER state, so most-recent would invert it). I intended to ship the mutual-exclusion suppression here. Capturing guards across all sewer fields killed that: varblock 340 is NOT a state enum -- glwater2 has THREE REAL GATES on it (saku2==25, saku3==13, saku4==16) and glwater3 has saku3==3 alongside the ladder pair (9/0). The live byte can equal only one value, so "keep only the match" would have hidden two working gates on glwater2 -- exactly the failure that has cost several BAT cycles here. Script shape does not separate them either: ladline5 has a real JPF between guard and SET3, while ladline6 (state-dependent, confirmed) and saku3 (a gate) both run guard->SET3 with NO JPF. LEAD: the constants look like BIT MASKS not enums (25=0b11001, 13=0b01101, 16=0b10000, 9=0b1001, 7=0b111, 3=0b11) and the live word moved 0x0900->0x0909; a `value & mask` test would let several gates be true at once, which is what the room needs. Unconfirmed -- the operator lives in the JMP/JMPB/JPF opcodes this codebase does not decode. So `DiagnoseStateExclusionGroups()` logs the verdict each entity WOULD get and suppresses nothing; it also shouts if a group has zero matches. Aaron BATs both ladder states, we compare verdicts to reality, and `.296` flips it from logging to acting only if they hold. g++ compile-clean, guards verified correct on the real archive (ladline5==9, ladline6==0). NOT MSVC-compiled/BAT'd. Prior: #85/#37 SPLIT-ONLY build, ZERO behavior change by design. Aaron chose split-first so the state-gating fix lands in a clean file. Two source files were sitting a few hundred bytes under the 80 KB CI hard fail and every recent change had been paying for itself by deleting explanatory comments; the #85 fix needs new fields on JSMEntityInfo plus scanner work and simply did not fit. Extracted VERBATIM, no logic touched: (1) field_nav_catalog.inl 81,309 -> 63,017 by moving the trigger-line Exit/Event injection block to new field_nav_catalog_triglines.inl (20,260); (2) field_archive_jsm_scan.inl 81,184 -> 61,793 by moving the per-entity classification + post-passes to new field_archive_jsm_classify.inl (21,470). Both follow the existing textual-fragment pattern (field_nav_catalog_mapexits.inl etc): fragments of a function body #included where the block used to sit, operating on the caller's locals. NOTE the classify fragment sits INSIDE the per-entity for-loop but is brace-balanced and does NOT close it -- I first wrote comments claiming it did, caught it while verifying, and corrected both. VERIFIED by reconstructing the pre-split scan.inl from the post-split pair, building BOTH against the real game archive with the offline tool, and diffing full entity classification output across glwater2/3/4/5 + glfuryb1: IDENTICAL on every field. (An earlier diff against a stale baseline showed an extra promoted entity -- that was the .289 director fix legitimately doing its job, not the split.) Compile-clean under g++. Still NOT compiled under MSVC and NOT BAT'd. Prior: #85 DIAGNOSTIC-ONLY build. Aaron backtracked to the gate room on a later save and screenshotted it: the tall vertical ladder standing right of the raised block in the earlier shots is GONE, replaced by a diagonal plank -- the shortcut ladder, already knocked down. Same room, two world states. That explains the "duplicative objects": glwater3 ladline5 and ladline6 guard their SET3 on the SAME varblock (0x0154=340) compared against DIFFERENT values (ladline5 vs 9, ladline6 vs 0) -- a state machine where one model is the standing ladder and the other the fallen one, only ONE of which exists at a time. The catalog is built from STATIC script data so it injects BOTH unconditionally. Rather than gate injection on an inferred mapping (the opcode simulator does not fully decode JMP/JMPB/JPF compare semantics), this build only ADDS a read-only [STATE-DIAG] probe logging live varblock 337/339/340 at catalog time, and adds saku1/ladline5 to the PUZZLE-DIAG watch list so it fires on glwater* at all. Aaron BATs the room in both states; the values prove which is which, then the injection gate lands in .294. No behavior change. Prior: #85 .291 BAT -- Aaron reported NO exits in the catalog, but that is NOT a .291 regression: the exit fix demonstrably WORKED on the clean field load (14:55:08 log: "[PSHM-DEST] line2 addr=0x02FA (762) CORROBORATED by jsm4 'selphie' literal dest", exits present, captured=8 trigger lines). He then fought two battles (14:55:53, 14:56:27) and BOTH post-battle field re-inits logged "[LINE-PAIR] ... captured=0" -- trigger-line geometry is captured once on field entry and is NOT rebuilt when returning from a battle, so every trigger-line exit silently vanishes for the rest of the visit. His catalog walk was at 14:58, post-battle. NEW pre-existing bug, now isolated with timestamps; filed for its own cycle since it is a different subsystem. This build: (1) dropped the " (approx.)" suffix from SPOKEN labels (still logged) -- Aaron confirmed "Gate (approx.)" was at the correct location and asked for the tag to go; centroid positions have proven accurate and the tag cost two extra words on every announcement. (2) ENTITY_SKIP_NAMES is now honoured for JSM-injected ENT_OBJECTs -- it was only ever consulted by IsBgControllerName() for Background entities, so glwater3's 'water' (which IS in the skip list) appeared as an "Object" ~100u from Gate 1, Aaron's "another object was at the same location as a gate". Remaining: ladline5/ladline6 still show as two "Object" entries near the shortcut ladder -- no behavioral evidence yet for what they are, deliberately not guessed. field_nav_catalog.inl trimmed 81,803->81,309 by compressing three older comment blocks (611 bytes margin); it still needs a real split (#37). NOT compiled/BAT'd. Prior: #85 .290 BAT CONFIRMED the gate hypothesis -- Aaron walked the whole catalog at the blocking gate and only the ladline entity placed him correctly (log agrees: sole entry reaching "In range.", ~1 step, while every Gate is 500-1300 away). So 'ladline7' IS a gate; named it "Gate" (unnumbered -- Gate 1-3 are saku1-3 and this is a 4th distinct mechanism, not saku4/the Director). The BAT also exposed THREE defects I introduced in .290: (a) my duplicate-slot guard was INCOMPLETE -- it only covered ResolveLatePositions, and ResolveStructPositions runs after it and re-applied the exact phantom positions it had just rejected (log: ladline5 REJECTED live tri=147 vs own 186, then [STRUCT-POS] ent22 idx=6 struct=(268,671) straight back onto Gate 1); same guard now added there, reading tri at 0x1FA. (b) I predicted removing the ladline table rows would yield generic "Object" -- wrong, I misread the path: ResolveFriendlyName() never returns empty, it capitalizes the SYM on a table miss, so Aaron heard "Ladline5/6/7" verbatim. The JSM-injection path now requires a real table hit and otherwise keeps the generic type name. (c) my flat bg-only scoping of addr-as-literal was too blunt and REGRESSED a working label: glwater3 jsm2 'irvine' addr 0x02FA=762=Sewer 2 was correct and got stripped to a bare "Exit", while only jsm7 'hasigomodel' 0x011B=283=Centra Ruins was bogus. Now accepts bg* (original 8-BAT evidence base) OR a destination corroborated by a sibling line's literal dest on the same field (762/763 exist on glwater3 via jsm3-jsm6) -- restores Sewer 2, still rejects Centra Ruins. NOT compiled/BAT'd (no Windows toolchain). WARNING: field_nav_catalog.inl is at 81,803/81,920 bytes -- 117 bytes of margin, see issue #37. Prior: #85 .289 BAT'd -- Gate 1 (saku1) DID appear, so the .289 promotion fix worked, but Aaron reported the blocking gate still missing plus serious catalog bloat. Three fixes, Aaron approved batching them. (1) MY BUG from .288: I mapped 'ladline0'-'ladline7' -> "Ladder" purely off the SYM name, violating this project's own "SYM names are unreliable" rule. glwater3's 'ladline7' IS the gate the player must open -- it sits ~1 step from where Aaron stood facing it (tri-83 centroid (-287,815) vs player (-238,706)), it's the ONLY catalog entry anywhere near it, Aaron independently said its coords put him "right in front of the gate to sewer 2", and its script dispatches REQs like the saku gates. Mapping removed; ladline* now fall through to generic "Object" rather than asserting a wrong identity. (2) Duplicate-slot corruption guard in ResolveLatePositions(): 'ladline5'(own tri=186)/'ladline6'(own tri=175) were returning byte-identical live fp values to saku1(147)/saku2(181) -- the .285 out-of-window aliasing, but SUCCEEDING with plausible in-range values -- so two phantom objects sat exactly on top of Gate 1 and Gate 2. Now a live read whose triangle disagrees with the entity's own static SET3 triangle is rejected and falls back to its own centroid; matching entities (the real gates) are untouched. (3) The addr-as-literal exit-destination heuristic's own documented caveat fired: glwater3 ent7 'hasigomodel' marker addr 0x011B=283 = a valid field id, fabricating a confident "Exit to Centra Ruins 8" on the other side of the world. That equivalence was derived from 8 BATs ALL on 'bg*' fields; now scoped to bg* so non-bg fields keep the marker and show a bare unlabeled "Exit" instead of inventing a destination. NOT compiled/BAT'd (no Windows toolchain). Note (1)/(3) are catalog-side and can't be exercised by the offline archive tool; verified by log correlation + hand review. Prior: #85 .288 BAT'd -- ladders left as-is per Aaron; turned back to the actual blocking gate (glwater3's 'saku1', confirmed missing via fresh F11 screenshots taken right in front of it). Dumped saku1's script via the offline tool: real model + SET3 position (tri=147, literal) + REQs the field's gate-coordinator 'saku4' exactly like the working gates -- but its own walk-dispatch opcode (0x1C) pops a STATICALLY RESOLVABLE sub-opcode value instead of firing with an empty/unresolved stack, so the scanner's hasExtDispatch flag (which only fires on the ambiguous empty-stack/PSHM-marker cases) never sets for it, and it fails the Director-promotion OR (dialog||extDispatch) that promotes Unknown->Interactive Object. Fix in field_archive_jsm_director.inl: added a 3rd qualifying signal -- a target with its own model that issues >=1 raw REQ opcode (stack-independent count; the resolved-target-ID path fails for these gates) also promotes. Verified via the offline tool: saku1 now promotes cleanly, tri=147 unaffected (was already a literal, non-marker capture), no other glwater3/4/5 entity's classification changed as a side effect. Not compiled/BAT'd -- no Windows toolchain here. Prior: #85 .287 BAT'd -- gates now positioned correctly, but a NEW raw-SYM-name leak surfaced: "Ladline7 (approx.)" was announced at the gate room's ladder. Root cause: glwater3's 'ladline5'/'ladline6'/'ladline7' are the physical ladder-climb entities (same setmodelInit+extDispatch Director-promotion profile as the 'saku' gate valves; 'ladline0'-'ladline4' are separate Map Exit trigger lines, unaffected), and now resolve real SET3/centroid positions thanks to .287's fix -- but they weren't in ENTITY_DISPLAY_NAMES, so ResolveFriendlyName() fell through to its raw-SYM-cleanup fallback and exposed "Ladline7" verbatim to Aaron's screen reader, violating the project's "never expose SYM names" rule. Fix: added ladline0-ladline7 -> "Ladder" to entity_classifications.h, same precedent as the existing 'ladder'/'hasigomodel' entries. No logic change, table-only. Not compiled/BAT'd (no Windows toolchain here). saku1's classification gap (still stuck Unknown, never promoted to Interactive Object) remains open. Prior: #85 BAT'd -- gates now appear in the catalog, but Aaron reported standing right at a gate while the mod said "three steps away". Traced with the offline archive tool (same one from .286): the SET3 capture code's 4-param path casts pushStack[coordBase+3] straight to uint16_t as the triangle ID WITHOUT checking whether that stack slot is itself a PSHM_W marker. On glwater3, ladline5/ladline6/saku2/saku3 all reference the SAME runtime scratch address (0x3A) in that slot -- coincidentally a VALID triangle index (58) for that field -- so all four entities silently got the identical wrong shared position instead of their own. Same corruption confirmed across glwater2/glwater4/glwater5. Fix (field_archive_jsm_scan.inl): when the triangle slot is itself a marker, fall back to opcParam (the SET3 instruction's own embedded immediate, independent of the stack) instead of trusting the corrupted value. Verified against the real field archive: every previously-identical/shared triangle ID is now distinct and spread sensibly across each field (e.g. glwater3's four entities: 186/175/181/54 instead of all sharing 58). Coverage counts unchanged (22/26); this fixes correctness of the values, not how many resolve. Also surfaced (not yet fixed, tracked on #85): glwater3's saku1 has a real, now-correct captured triangle (147) but never appears in the catalog at all -- its JSM classification stayed "Unknown" rather than being promoted to Interactive Object, an earlier, separate stage than the position bug this build fixes.
// v0.18.3.284: .283 BAT -- glclock1 false exit CONFIRMED FIXED. glprein1 trapdoor still resolves the right destField (716) but had no position (pos=0,0), unreachable by nav; root cause: the entity is TALKRADIUS-triggered (no SETLINE) and never calls SET3, so both existing position sources came up empty, even though the entity IS placed on the walkmesh (just HIDE-flagged out of the general scan). Fix: MAP_EXIT catalog injection now falls back to a live GetEntityPos() read (which ignores HIDE, only checks walkmesh placement) keyed on the entity's flat jsmIndex, gated to only fire when the destination already resolved to a real field/world-map (can't rescue markers like glprein1's 'tobi'). SAFETY CHECK added after catching a self-review regression: the jsmIndex->runtime-slot mapping can coincidentally hit a live party member's slot instead (glclock1's own 'irvine' MAP_EXIT shares jsmIndex=2 with Rinoa's live slot there) -- new GetEntitySetpc() + IsPartyCharacterSetpc() guard refuses the fallback whenever that slot is a real party character, which would otherwise have silently reintroduced .283's false exit with a fabricated position. Sewer gates (#85) NOT re-tested this round -- Aaron passed through glwater1 in ~6s without opening the catalog, so RefreshCatalog/EXTSCAN never ran; needs a revisit with -/= pressed there. See CHANGELOG.
// v0.18.3.283: .282 BAT -- taint-tracking didn't catch glclock1's false exit (the wrong dest was a genuine literal on an inactive branch, not a variable read); Aaron confirmed only ONE real exit exists there. Fix: don't trust an unpositioned MAP_EXIT when a trigger-line exit already covers the field. Also: EXTSCAN finally fired and proved sakua/sakub/seigyo sit past MAX_ENTITIES itself (16), not just past the reported count -- widened to 32. See CHANGELOG.
// v0.18.3.282: #86 follow-up -- .281 BAT showed the interpreter also resolved a SECOND MAP_EXIT (glclock1 'irvine') to a wrong destination (field 5/"wm05") because its destField came from a live varblock read, not a literal. InterpretExitMethod now tracks per-value provenance (taint) and declines (falls back to the old cautious marker path) when the final MAPJUMP operand is variable-sourced. glprein1's trapdoor (dest=716, literal-backed) is expected to be unaffected -- needs BAT confirmation. See CHANGELOG.
// v0.18.3.281: #85 diagnostic (EXTSCAN re-armed, glwater1-gated) + #86 fix (MAP_EXIT now gets the authoritative interpreter, and an INTERP-sourced destination bypasses the INF-gateway "unmatched = stale" filter). See CHANGELOG.
// v0.18.3.278: the SETLINE-exit block was the last user of the infinite-line separation test, so exits flickered in/out as the player moved (glfurin1 Mansion 4 present 19:01:26, gone 19:01:33). Now uses the bounded SegmentsCross test, matching the v0.17.8.10 gateway and v0.18.3.268 interaction fixes.
// v0.18.3.277: #84 FIX -- captured line t maps to JSM entity t, not jsmDoors+t. DOORS participate in the captured-line sequence, so on door-bearing fields every line was shifted by the door count. glfurin1 (jsmDoors=1): line0 now resolves to the door's 725 ("Mansion 5") instead of jsm1's 724, line1 gets its real 724 ("Mansion 4"), line2 stays the Glass interaction. Invisible on all B-Garden fields because they are jsmDoors=0.
// v0.18.3.270: field-ID -> name mapping fixed. GetFieldNameById() derived names from FL ordering (index/3), which is invalid -- field.fl is only triplet-interleaved for its first 7 fields then switches to grouped-by-extension, and field IDs don't follow FL order at all. It returned 'edview2' for field 721 (really glfurin1), corrupting gateway destFieldName and the catalog dedupe strstr that compares it. Now resolves through the curated FIELD_DISPLAY_NAMES table.
// v0.18.3.266: CI size-guard fix -- field_nav_catalog.inl had reached 82 KB, over the 80 KB hard-fail ceiling, and the push was refused. Extracted the JSM_ENT_MAP_EXIT injection block verbatim to field_nav_catalog_mapexits.inl (same inline-#include pattern as _dedupe/_naming). Pure textual move, no logic change. Parent now ~74 KB.  // v0.18.3.115: #70 -- STEEPNESS FILTER replaces the .114 depth gate. The .114 gate (A* refuses triangles below floor-z -1050) was too blunt: Dollet is coastal and its approach dips below -1050 too, so blocking the box canyon also disconnected Dollet -- the .114 BAT logged "navmesh A* NO path" on re-plan and fell back to the coarse fine-grid (wedged 15km on a false-walkable cliff the 1024u grid can't see). Root fix: exclude cliff-FACE triangles from the navmesh at FEED time. floorZ is the MEAN of a triangle's 3 corner z's, so a sheer wall reads as one steep tri whose mean sits mid-drop (depth/floor-step blind to it); the within-triangle z-SPREAD (max corner - min corner) reveals it. Navmesh_AddTriangle now skips any triangle with spread > NM_STEEP_SPREAD (400): box-canyon walls + false-coast cliffs go, gentle terr-29 ramps + the Dollet approach (small spread) stay. A* is ungated again (depth gate retired; s_nmAStarFloorMin left dormant = INT32_MIN). VALIDATION: the [NAVMESH] connectivity diag fires at world-map LOAD (before driving) -- check Dollet says REACHABLE (filter kept it) and the tri count dropped from 157416 (cliffs removed); if Dollet is "not reachable" the threshold clipped the approach -> RAISE NM_STEEP_SPREAD (e.g. 550). Then the [NAVPATH] route should stay shallow (no -1665 dip) and the executor should follow it past 15km. BAT .115: REBUILD via deploy.vbs, confirm Version 0.18.3.115, LOAD the world map (send the [NAVMESH] connectivity line) then auto-drive Dollet on foot; send Logs/ff8_world.log. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. // v0.18.3.114: #70 FIX -- block the DEEP RAVINE in the navmesh drive. The .113 [NAVPATH] dump proved the ungated A* routes the on-foot Dollet drive DOWN into a box canyon (floor -1087..-1665, ~900-1400u below the start -731 / Dollet -264 band) and climbs out the far side -- a route the engine refuses on foot, so Squall wedges on the canyon's east rim ~16km out. floorZ is the MEAN of a triangle's 3 corner z's, so a sheer wall reads as one steep tri whose mean sits mid-drop: the descent steps are gentle (158..235u) and OVERLAP the gentle corridor's validated 176u bottleneck, so a floor-STEP gate can't separate them. The canyon's DEPTH is unambiguous, though. Add s_nmAStarFloorMin (navmesh.inl): A* skips any triangle with floor-z below it; PlanDrivePathNavmesh sets it to -1050 before the A* call, so the search can't enter the ravine (floor <= -1087) and must take the gentle terr-29/road corridor (floor >= ~-731) to Dollet. Search-time gate only -- the validated .107 mesh construction is unchanged; default INT32_MIN keeps the Initialize connectivity diag ungated. [NAVPATH] dump (40 tris + dZ) KEPT to verify the new route stays above -1050; if the gate over-disconnects (logs "navmesh A* NO path") lower toward -1300. BAT .114: REBUILD via deploy.vbs, confirm Version 0.18.3.114, then on foot from the Galbadia save auto-drive Dollet; send Logs/ff8_world.log. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. // v0.18.3.113: #70 DIAGNOSTIC (no behavior change) -- EXTEND the [NAVPATH] dump from 14 to 40 triangles + a per-step dZ (floor-z delta) column, because the .112 dump only captured the GENTLE approach (centroids 0-13, floorZ -731..-937, max single step 235u = the part Squall drives) and stopped SHORT of the box-canyon CLIFF where he wedges at centroid ~16-20 (game ~(-29201,-30115), past the dumped Y=-28841). The dZ column makes the cliff step obvious: ONE big dZ (e.g. a +700u canyon-floor->plateau jump) => gate A* on floor-step at a value below the cliff but above 235u; MANY small dZ (<=235u up a tessellated face) => floor-step gating cannot separate cliff from corridor, exclude high-steepness tris at FEED time instead. STILL no behavior change. REMOVE the [NAVPATH] block after diagnosis. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. BAT .113: REBUILD via deploy.vbs (the .112 build at 19:19 predates this dump-extension -- the last log still shows the 14-entry dump), then on foot from the Galbadia save auto-drive Dollet; send Logs/ff8_world.log -- the [NAVPATH] block (now 40 tris + dZ, logged right before "[PLAN] navmesh centroids") triangles ~13-22 are the goal. // v0.18.3.112: #70 DIAGNOSTIC (no behavior change) -- a [NAVPATH] dump in PlanDrivePathNavmesh logs the A* TRIANGLE path's centroid game(x,y) + floor-z (first 14 tris + goal), to settle whether the navmesh routes the player UP the box-canyon CLIFF (floorZ climbs going into the canyon) or thinks there is a gentle ramp the engine blocks. .111 (centroids) wedged at the SAME box canyon as .110 (funnel): the full drive log shows the centroid path leads the player SOUTH 6 waypoints (19.5km->16km, idx 0->6) then turns NW (steer (-30208,-31232)/(-31232,-31232)) INTO the cliff while Dollet is NE/SE -- so the A* PATH ITSELF routes into the canyon, not the funnel/centroid smoother. The ungated A* (gate=-1, .107) can climb cliffs via steep SHARED-EDGE triangles (the z-aware construction only excludes cliff BRIDGE/PROX links, NOT the cliff-face mesh). The dump's floor-z profile picks the fix (gate A* on floor-step, or exclude high-steepness tris at feed time, gate informed by the actual cliff step). REMOVE the [NAVPATH] block after diagnosis. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed. BAT .112: REBUILD (deploy.vbs -- the prior BAT ran the pre-[NAVPATH] .111 DLL, so the log has NO [NAVPATH] lines) then on foot from the Galbadia save auto-drive Dollet; send Logs/ff8_world.log -- the [NAVPATH] block (logged at each plan, right before "[PLAN] navmesh centroids") is the goal. // v0.18.3.111: #70 REVERT the .108 funnel string-pull -> feed the raw A* triangle CENTROIDS as drive waypoints. The F11 at the 17km wedge (a readable 146KB shot) shows Squall steered STRAIGHT INTO a box-canyon CLIFF FACE, steer target locked NW (-31232,-31232) across the rock while Dollet is NE; .109 (clamp OFF) == .110 (clamp ON) byte-for-byte, proving the LOS clamp is IRRELEVANT here -- the FUNNEL routes the chord across the cliff. ROOT (world_map_navmesh.inl Navmesh_FunnelPath): the SSF's DEGENERATE centroid portals at the bridge/proximity links (which share no edge) give the string-pull no real left/right walls, so it pulls taut THROUGH non-walkable terrain. The A* TRIANGLE path is correct (validated .107 to the digit); only the string-pull layered on top is unsound. PlanDrivePathNavmesh now feeds the raw A* centroids (Navmesh_AStar + Navmesh_TriangleCentroidGame, the .106/.107 primitive) rasterized dense as before; each centroid sits inside a walkable triangle and consecutive centroids are a shared edge or a short z-gated neck apart, so chords are too short to span a cliff. Keeps the .110 LOS clamp ACTIVE + the .109 (A) reorder (navmesh before the IsGoalSegment shortcut); Navmesh_FunnelPath left in place but unused. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 reverts to .99. BAT .111: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden; EXPECT a [PLAN] navmesh centroids line (REPLACES navmesh funnel) and the drive to get PAST the 17km cliff-cut wedge -- ideally back to the .107 ~8-12km range or closer, with NO steer target locked WEST/NW across a cliff. If it then wedges ~8-12km at a cliff, that is the executor far-lookahead cutting a corner the coarse 1024u clamp can't see -> the next step is a navmesh-resolution walkability clamp (rasterize navmesh-walkable cells into a fine overlay the executor LOS clamp reads instead of s_walkClassFine). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.110: #70 REVERT the .109 executor coarse-grid bypass -- it REGRESSED (the funnel's dense/degenerate corners NEED the executor LOS clamp to keep the steer target on a clear line; bypassing it locked the steer onto a WRONG-WAY funnel corner -- steer(-31232,-31232) = WEST, away from Dollet -- and the drive walked into a wall at 15-17km, vs .108's clean 4km WITH the clamp). s_driveNavmeshPath now stays FALSE so FineLineClearFootCar + the #83 fwd-guard RUN on the navmesh path (= the .108 executor that drove to 4km). KEEP the .109 (A) reorder (PlanDrivePathNavmesh runs BEFORE the IsGoalSegment shortcut, so the navmesh -- not an empty path -- routes the final approach). LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING, NOT pushed; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 reverts to .99. BAT .110: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden; EXPECT the general drive to return to ~4km (NOT the 15-17km WEST wrong-way wedge) and the final leg to attempt the navmesh route (a [PLAN] navmesh funnel line on the final leg). If it now drives to ~4km then wedges NEAR Dollet, that is the LOS clamp's .81-box over-clamp on the final-approach navmesh path -- the narrower next step (retire/exempt the .81 Dollet-coast box cols 104-111 rows 59-69, since the navmesh validates that coast walkable). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.109: #70 EXECUTOR coarse-grid BYPASS on the navmesh path (LOCAL, NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed). The .108 funnel ran (87 tris -> 30 corners -> 30 cells) but STILL wedged 12km from Dollet on OPEN GRASS in the road/railroad canyon corridor (F11 confirmed; NOT a wall), idx pinned 0/30, steer target pinned ~1 cell ahead at (-24064,-30208), player oscillating. ROOT (read the executor steer block): the navmesh route legitimately runs SE through fine cells the COARSE 1024u grid marks blocked (the .81 Dollet-coast box cols 104-111 rows 59-69; the player is at fine(104,66), INSIDE it), and the executor's LOS clamp (FineLineClearFootCar over s_walkClassFine/s_steepFine) walked the steer target all the way back to the player's own cell -- so the cursor never advanced (cell[1] is across the coarse-blocked box, the player never reaches it). The navmesh said walkable; the coarse grid said blocked; the executor trusted the coarse grid. This is the .106 'stage 2': the navmesh routing swap is only complete once the EXECUTOR stops consulting the coarse fine grid. FIX: new s_driveNavmeshPath flag (state.inl; set true in PlanDrivePathNavmesh, cleared at the top of PlanDrivePath) gates OFF both coarse-grid executor checks on the navmesh path -- the FineLineClearFootCar LOS clamp AND the #83 forward-collision guard (both drive.inl). The funnel corridor is navmesh-walkable by construction and the corner-cap keeps the aim inside it, so the clamp is redundant AND harmful there. Now the steer target follows the validated corridor (the corner-capped ~2400u lookahead), the player walks the .81-box cells the engine actually allows, the cursor advances, ARRIVE. The fine-grid path keeps the clamp+guard (flag stays false) -- ZERO change off-navmesh; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 still instant-reverts to .99. BAT .109: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive to Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden -- all three ARRIVE = #70 acceptance + close. EXPECT [YAWDRIVE] steer target to ADVANCE along the route (not pin at (-24064,-30208)) and idx to CLIMB 0 -> 30, with NO wedge. If he now PROGRESSES but crawls/wiggles, the 30 dense corners (the funnel's degenerate bridge/prox-link centroid portals defeat string-pulling) need real-portal computation next -- a smoothness refinement, separate from this wedge. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.108: #68/#70 FUNNEL -- replace the navmesh centroid waypoints with a string-pulled funnel (Simple Stupid Funnel Algorithm) over the A* triangle corridor's PORTALS, so the #68 executor's steer target can no longer aim across walls the winding route goes AROUND (the .107 centroid path wedged the Dollet drive at (-24150,-13185), idx pinned 0/46, oscillating into a sub-cell wall the coarse 1024u LOS clamp missed). New Navmesh_FunnelPath (world_map_navmesh.inl): same ungated A* -> a PORTAL per consecutive tri pair (the shared EDGE where 2 vertices match, oriented L/R by the centroid-travel cross-product; a DEGENERATE portal at the next triangle's centroid for T-junction-bridge / proximity links that share no edge) -> SSF string-pull -> corner points in game coords; PlanDrivePathNavmesh rasterizes the corner polyline into DENSE fine cells (~512u steps, shortest-torus delta, dedup) into s_drivePath, which the #68 executor consumes UNCHANGED (its staircase-aware corner-cap reads each leg as a sustained run and turns at each funnel corner; legs stay inside the walkable corridor by construction so LOS is always clear). Helpers NmTriArea2 + NmSharedVerts, host-compilable under NAVMESH_DIAG. [.107 BAT'd: the z-aware navmesh VALIDATED in-game TO THE DIGIT -- [NAVMESH] 157416 tris, flood 35531 reachable (== offline), Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling/Winhill/Tomb REACHABLE + Balamb NOT, and the gentle corridor PHYSICALLY WALKS (Dollet drive followed the route ~8km, Timber to 1-2km) -- so bit7-overturn + z-aware bridging is CORRECT; the remaining blocker is purely the #68 EXECUTOR.] ONE orientation assumption (documented one-line flip in Navmesh_FunnelPath): the shared vertex LEFT of travel is the funnel's left; if a BAT shows the path hugging the WRONG wall, flip the single `> 0`. Traced offline on a straight corridor -> [start,goal], no spurious corners. LOCAL behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed; flip NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 to revert to .99. BAT .108: Galbadia save -> on-foot auto-drive to Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden -- all three ARRIVE = #70 acceptance + close; EXPECT a [PLAN] 'navmesh funnel: start tri#.. -> goal tri#.. = N tris -> M corners -> K fine cells' line (REPLACES 'navmesh route') and [YAWDRIVE] idx to climb the legs with NO wedge at (-24150,-13185). If Timber still doesn't ENTER once the wedge is fixed, that is a separate field-entry/arrival item. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.107: #70 ROUTING SWAP stage 2 -- OVERTURN the .105 bit7 walkability filter; model on-foot walkability by ELEVATION STEP instead (LOCAL, behind NAVMESH_DIAG/NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed). BAT .106 confirmed the routing swap WORKS (navmesh route engaged, char followed it ~1.7km closer) but wedged at a sheer cliff 13.3km from Dollet (F11 confirmed). Offline root-cause (reproduced the in-game route to the digit vs the real wmx mesh): the .105 navmesh reaches Dollet ONLY by climbing cliffs -- bottleneck 1018u, and ANY elevation-consistency requirement isolates Dollet (its genuine shared-edge island is 15 tris, the start's 110, DIFFERENT components). The bit7 filter was the WRONG discriminator: it KEEPS the bit7-set cliff/ledge faces (terr 14/15) and DELETES the gentle terr-27/29 ramps (bit7-clear) that connect elevations along the real canyon approach. In the UNFILTERED mesh a gentle corridor to Dollet EXISTS (bottleneck 176u; uses grass/ledge/road/railroad + 13 gentle terr-29 mountain ramps). THREE coupled changes (all offline-validated): (1) segments.inl feeds ALL non-ocean triangles (drop the poly[0x0E]&0x80 skip, back to the .104 feed); (2) navmesh.inl T-junction bridging is now Z-AWARE -- store boundary-edge endpoint z, interpolate across the overlap, bridge only when both ends match within NM_BRIDGE_ZTOL=100 (a cliff top/base share an x,y footprint but NOT z, so the .105 z-ignoring bridge falsely stitched them and let A* climb cliffs); (3) navmesh.inl proximity links are Z-GATED -- connect centroids only within NM_STEP_LINK(400u) horizontally AND NM_LINK_ZSTEP(300u) floor step. A* stays ungated (-1) -- the z-aware construction's max edge step is ~374u, so cliffs are already excluded. This is the fine grid's proven steepness rule (gentle ramps walkable, steep faces blocked) applied to the navmesh. Offline final-validation (unfiltered + z-aware bridge 100 + z-gated prox 300): start component 35531/157416 (23%, one continent); Dollet bottleneck 176, Timber 115, Galbadia Garden 115, Deling 115 -- all gentle, NO cliffs; Balamb Town + Balamb Garden correctly NOT reachable (across ocean). #68 executor + .81/.85/#69 fine-grid stack UNTOUCHED; NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 still instant-reverts to the .99 drive. ONE risk to confirm in-BAT: whether the engine physically lets the char walk the gentle terr-29/terr-27 ramps -- strong prior YES (the .99 fine-grid chain, steepness-based, walked most of this corridor; the fine grid relies on gentle-mountain being walkable on other continents). BAT .107: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive to Dollet, then Timber, then Galbadia Garden (all three ARRIVE = acceptance test). EXPECT [NAVMESH] '~157416 triangles' (UP from 77447 -- proof the bit7 filter is gone) + a much larger reachable count, Dollet/Timber/G-Garden/Deling REACHABLE + Balamb NOT, then a [PLAN] 'navmesh route' line whose route follows the gentle canyon (no cliff climb) and ARRIVES. If it still wedges, send Logs/ff8_world.log + an F11: the wedge terrain tells us whether a gentle ramp is engine-blocked (-> incorporate poly[0x0F] or lower the ramp gate) or it is the #68 executor end-game. Flip NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 to restore .99. LOCAL-ONLY, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.106: #70 ROUTING SWAP stage 1 (LOCAL, behind NAVMESH_ROUTING; NOT pushed). BAT .105 proved the walkability-filtered navmesh's connectivity in-game (77447 tris; Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden REACHABLE, Balamb not), so wire it into the planner. PlanDrivePath now routes on the navmesh FIRST: Navmesh_FindTriangleGame(start)+(dest) -> Navmesh_AStar(start,goal,-1) (ungated; poly[0x0E] walkability IS the passability test) -> convert each triangle CENTROID back to game coords (new Navmesh_TriangleCentroidGame, inverse of NmGameToMeshX/Y) -> fine cell (WorldXToFineCol/Row + PackSeg, dedup consecutive) -> s_drivePath, so the existing #68 yaw executor consumes it UNCHANGED (it already follows s_drivePath as packed fine cells). Falls back to the fine-grid clearance Dijkstra if the navmesh has no path (e.g. mesh not yet built). New NAVMESH_ROUTING toggle (state.inl): 1 = route on the navmesh, 0 = INSTANT revert to the proven .99 fine-grid chain. DELIBERATELY MINIMAL/REVERSIBLE -- the #68 executor, the .81 coast box, the .85 road override, and the #69 height-step guard are ALL UNTOUCHED this stage. The navmesh route is all-walkable so it inherently avoids the false coast/mountains, BUT the executor's #83 forward-collision guard still probes the coarse fine grid: if a walkable navmesh-route cell reads as steep-mountain/.81-blocked there (e.g. the Dollet cliff approach terr14/15), the guard may balk -- that is the EXPECTED stage-1 SIGNAL. If the drive follows the navmesh route to arrival, great; if it wedges, the [YAWDRIVE]/guard trace shows WHERE, and stage 2 retires the fine-grid stack so the fine grid agrees with the navmesh. BAT .106: on foot from the Galbadia save, auto-drive to Dollet (then Timber, then Galbadia Garden). EXPECT a [PLAN] 'navmesh route: A* start tri#.. -> goal tri#.. = N tris -> M fine-cell waypoints' line (NOT '[PLAN] Fine path'), [YAWDRIVE] idx to CLIMB along the waypoints, and -- if the forward-guard doesn't fight a cliff cell -- ARRIVAL with NO false-coast wedge. If it wedges, send Logs/ff8_world.log: the wedge location names the fine-grid blocker to retire in stage 2. Flip NAVMESH_ROUTING 0 to instantly restore the .99 drive. LOCAL-ONLY, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.105: #70 -- WALKABILITY-FILTERED navmesh + step-distance proximity links + ungated flood (LOCAL diag behind NAVMESH_DIAG; NOT pushed). Recovered the engine's on-foot walkability rule from FF8_EN.exe (move-validator 0x54B860 -> selector sub_53E730): poly[0x0E] bit7 is the per-polygon on-foot passability flag (the const-0x32 check), gated by toggle [0x00C75CF4] which inits 1 and is NEVER cleared (only writer 0x542C0C sets 1), so the check is ALWAYS active on the world map. Offline vs world.fs it tracks terrain almost perfectly (mountain 29 / railroad 27 / rough = bit CLEAR = not-walkable; grass/cliff/ledge/road = bit SET). This IS the Dollet 'false coast': the old navmesh only filtered ocean, so the planner routed the character UP mountains the engine refuses on foot and they wedged at the base -- Finding 4 ('collision not in the mesh') is OVERTURNED, the collision WAS in the mesh as E.bit7-clear mountain polys. THREE coupled changes, all validated offline against the real world.fs (see NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT 'WALKABILITY-FLAG TRACK'): (1) LoadTerrainGrid (segments.inl) skips feeding non-walkable polys (poly[WMX_TERRAIN_OFFSET+1] & 0x80 == 0) into the navmesh, mirroring the ocean skip; (2) Navmesh_Build (navmesh.inl) adds step-distance proximity links -- connect walkable triangles whose centroids are within NM_STEP_LINK(400u ~= 2 engine steps) via a 512u bucket grid, because the engine validates the DESTINATION polygon of each ~190u step so the character HOPS across sub-edge-width walkable necks (at the Dollet approach the corridor pinches through mountain slivers: the choke holds 4 walkable + 11 mountain tris, so exact-edge adjacency strands Dollet as a 15-triangle island; general collinear bridging adds only 12 bridges and does NOT fix it -- proximity links do); (3) Navmesh_LogConnectivity floods UNGATED -- the ground-truth walkability filter replaces the NM_CLIMB_STEP height-step heuristic, which wrongly blocked the genuinely-walkable Dollet cliff approach (terr14/15, z spread ~440 > the 400 gate). Offline result, ungated flood from the Galbadia save coord: Dollet + Timber REACHABLE, 20620 of 77447 walkable tris reached (27%, one continent's worth), and Balamb correctly NOT reachable (the 400u links do not bridge the ocean). NON-INVASIVE: the planner/executor are still the .99 chain; nothing routes on the navmesh yet -- this BAT only RE-VERIFIES connectivity in-game. BAT .105: load a Galbadia world-map save (the build fires at terrain load, the connectivity diag at Initialize); EXPECT in Logs/ff8_world.log '[NAVMESH] built ~77447 triangles' (DOWN from 157416 -- proof the walkability filter took effect at the right byte), the flood line '-> ~20620 reachable (walkability-filtered, ungated)', and Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling City REACHABLE with Balamb NOT reachable. If the triangle count does NOT drop from 157416 the poly[0x0E] offset is wrong; if Dollet is still not reachable raise NM_STEP_LINK; if Balamb becomes reachable the proximity radius is bridging water (lower it). If the connectivity matches offline, the NEXT BAT swaps routing onto the navmesh and retires the coarse-grid + .81/.85 compensating stack. LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.104: PORT the container-validated triangle navmesh (new src/world_map_navmesh.inl) into the build as a NON-INVASIVE diagnostic -- build + connectivity probe ONLY, ZERO change to the working planner/executor (the .99 chain still drives). All gated behind NAVMESH_DIAG (LOCAL-only, like WM_RUNTIME_WALK_DIAG). The module was developed offline against the real wmx.obj then host-compiled + run in-container (tests/test_navmesh.cpp, g++ -std=c++17, 0 failures) reproducing the offline numbers TO THE DIGIT: 157416 navigable triangles, 253 connected components after axis-aligned T-junction bridging, largest 74308; flood from the Galbadia save coord (-29270,-24056) at gate 400 reaches Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling City and correctly NOT Balamb (across the ocean); A* Galbadia->Dollet = 30259 units / 126 tris. Integration: LoadTerrainGrid (segments.inl) now (gated) Navmesh_Reset()s before its segment loop, Navmesh_AddTriangle()s each non-ocean triangle from INSIDE the existing RasterizeTriFine block (reusing the SAME verts + i0/i1/i2<vertCount guard, so the navmesh sees triangles identical to the fine grid), then Navmesh_Build()s (vertex dedup -> exact shared-edge adjacency -> T-junction bridging -> CSR) and logs [NAVMESH] tris/components/largest; world_map.cpp gains <vector> + the navmesh.inl include (after geometry.inl, before segments.inl) and Initialize() calls Navmesh_LogConnectivity() from the Galbadia coord. FIRST half of the navmesh plan (NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md): prove the mesh BUILDS + CONNECTS in-game before any routing depends on it. CAVEAT (Finding 4) STANDS: the navmesh canNOT model the Dollet false-coast (engine collision, not in the mesh) -- the .81 exclusion stays; the durable fix is the disassembly track. BAT .104: drive on the world map OR just load a Galbadia save (build fires at terrain load, connectivity diag at Initialize); EXPECT in Logs/ff8_world.log '[NAVMESH] built 157416 triangles, 253 components, largest=74308', the flood line '-> 35527 reachable (gate=400)', Dollet/Timber/Galbadia Garden/Deling City REACHABLE + Balamb not reachable, and '[NAVMESH] A* ref->Dollet: len=30259 tris=126 (path found)'. If the numbers MATCH offline the build is sound in-game and the NEXT BAT swaps routing onto it (retire the coarse fine-grid + .85 override; keep .81); if it CRASHES or the numbers DIFFER the in-game wmx parse diverged -- compare it. LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.103: RTWALK gate relaxation -- the .102 re-gate correctly suppressed the premature entry-tick dump (at entry foot=(0,0) + adj0/adj1=0xFFFFxxxx -> gate refused, no garbage dump) BUT then never fired at all: the adj-sanity requirement (adj0/adj1 in [0x00010000,0x7FFF0000)) never passed because the descriptor dwords stayed 0xFFFF914E/0xFFFFB006, AND the 1200-tick fire-anyway budget (~20s) wasn't reached in the ~15s session window -- so we still have NO post-load probe, which is the entire point. Over-corrected: the adj-sanity should have been a LOGGED OBSERVATION, not a HARD GATE (gating on it blocks the dump in exactly the case -- structural model wrong -- we most need to see). FIX (world_map.cpp Poll): drop adj0ok/adj1ok from the `ready` condition so the dump fires on scene-init alone (descBase!=0 AND foot position valid AND 30-tick settle), ~0.5s after the world map initializes; adj0ok/adj1ok are still computed and surfaced in the firing log as (ptr?0/1). This finally yields the post-load DumpRuntimeWalkability() probe of poly[0..9] + polyCount. EMERGING SIGNAL to resolve next BAT: descBase+0/descBase+12 read 0xFFFF914E/0xFFFFB006 and (since .102 `ready` never fired across ~15s past the settle) appear NOT to resolve to pointers even post-load -- so the descriptor-array model (descBase+N*12 -> adj-list pointer) may be WRONG. BAT .103: drive on the world map; EXPECT '[RTWALK] firing dump (mesh-ready)' ~0.5s after entry, then the poly[0..9] probe -- sane neighbour indices + WALL/END sentinels => model right, was pure timing; still 0xFFFFxxxx with polyCount now 473193 => descriptor model wrong, pivot to re-derive the structure (try the +0 field as a base-relative or signed offset, or re-check what 0x020402DC actually points at). LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.102: RTWALK runtime-walkmesh diagnostic RE-GATE -- fire the dump once the mesh is POPULATED, not merely when [0x020402DC]!=0. The .101 BAT got the first-ever [RTWALK] dump to fire (descBase=0x01E9FDCC, SEH guards caught the bad reads, plumbing proven) but it fired at world-map ENTRY before world.fs streamed the mesh: player pos still (0,0), polyCount(obs)=11, descriptor entries garbage (poly[0] adjPtr=0xFFFF914E; polys 4-9 = small increasing offsets 0x268..0x484, all FAULT). descBase is set during the data-load pointer setup BEFORE the entries it points to are filled in (which coincides with position becoming valid). FIX (world_map.cpp Poll + state.inl): keep polling, only fire once descBase!=0 AND foot position valid (footX|footY) for a 30-tick settle AND the first two descriptor adj-ptrs read as plausible pointers in [0x00010000,0x7FFF0000) (rejects both the 0x2xx offsets and the 0xFFFFxxxx garbage); bounded fire-anyway budget (1200 ticks) still dumps if it never settles -- and a post-load dump that is STILL garbage would mean the structural model, not the timing, is wrong. New s_rtWalkSettleTicks/s_rtWalkPollTicks replace the vestigial countdown, reset on entry. LOCAL-ONLY diag, never pushed. Full detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.101: Runtime walkability diag #68 executor CORNER-CAP (the .99 planner fix WORKED -- this is the next, separate blocker). The .99 BAT was a milestone: [PLAN] flipped from 'start already the closest reachable cell (expanded 5) -- direct steer' to a real 'Fine path: 61 cells ... dist=0, expanded 733', and [YAWDRIVE] idx CLIMBED 0->5 as he FOLLOWED it ~6km out of the Dollet shelf (dist 36019->34322). The pocket is SOLVED. He then re-wedged at idx 5/61 -- NOT the pocket, the #68 executor end-game. ROOT: the route is an L (a long WEST leg idx 0-4 at Y=-41472, then a 1-cell-wide NORTH canyon idx 5+ at X=-23040). The steer-target picker scans forward for the first path cell >= DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST(~2400u) ahead, so while still finishing the WEST leg it aimed ~2400u up the NORTH leg -> he cut the inside of the corner diagonally and pinned EAST of the col-104 corridor against the canyon's east wall (the '^' cells in [ROUTEMAP] cols 105-109). Then the 2400u threshold sat right on the corner cell, so jitter flipped the steer target between the corner cell (-23040,-41472, aim SE, keys -D-R) and an up-leg cell (-23040,-37376, aim NW, keys U-L-) -- the #68 whipsaw -- and the reverse un-wedge just fed it (idx stuck 5/61, dist ~34350, until a random encounter interrupted at stuck-check 3/6). FIX (drive.inl, in the forward-lookahead scan, foot+vehicle): CORNER-CAP -- stop the lookahead at the current leg's APEX, i.e. the first cell j>idx where the path direction (4-connected sign of row/col step) bends away from the leg's initial direction AND the new direction PERSISTS (sustained >=2 cells, so a real L-corner, not a staircase). He then aims AT the corner, walks the leg into it, and turns up the next leg from INSIDE the corridor -- no diagonal cut, no pin. Straight/open legs still scan to the full ~2400u (stable bearing, no orbit -- preserves the .78 anti-orbit far target); staircases (alternating 1-cell steps) are not sustained so they keep the far target too. The monotonic path cursor (s_drivePathIdx only advances) + the .82 LOS clamp are unchanged. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + ROUTE_MAP_DIAG stay ON. BAT .100: from a Dollet start, drive to Timber -- EXPECT him to follow the WEST leg to the corner (X decreasing to ~-23040) WITHOUT cutting NW early, turn up the NORTH canyon, [YAWDRIVE] idx to CLIMB PAST 5 (the old wedge), the steer target to NOT flip between (-23040,-41472) and (-23040,-37376), and ideally continue toward Timber. REGRESSION: into-Dollet still arrives + a Balamb drive still routes/arrives (the cap only shortens the lookahead AT sustained corners; open routes keep the far target). If he reaches the corner but STILL pins (a real in-game wall at the apex, not just a cut), the next step is a LATERAL escape in the wedge recovery (the executor currently only presses toward-target or reverses; it never tries sideways to get around a wall) -- consider an F11 at the wedge to SEE open-ground-vs-wall. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.99: #70 FIX 3 -- PIVOT from the executor road-bridge to a PLANNER fix (the bridge was the wrong layer). The .98 BAT proved the bridge FIRES now but self-destructs: the nearest road cell world(-14848,-38400) is ~1424-1488u from the Dollet shelf start -- INSIDE WM_BRIDGE_ARRIVE_DIST(1536) -- so UpdateAutoDrive's arrive-check fires on the SAME tick the bridge is set (before the character moves), clears it, re-plans, re-pockets, re-bridges; that loop burns all 6 attempts in <1s at drive start, then direct-steers at Timber into the south wall (U--- JAM, 6 reverse-bursts N back into the pocket, gives up at dist~32716). The nearest road cell is also NE (toward Dollet) while Timber is SW -- the wrong direction. ROOT (answering 'why are we running into walls when the road should carry the route'): the #69 height-step guard EXEMPTS a step only when BOTH cells are road; the Dollet EXIT start sits on the coastal shelf fine(112,59), which is NOT a road cell, so the single step shelf->road is a non-road->road step that the guard blocks (the shelf->road elevation jump is big). So the BFS is sealed into a 5-cell pocket and NEVER reaches the canyon road -- [PLAN] 'start already the closest reachable cell (expanded 5) -- direct steer'. Driving IN works because the route is already road->road the whole way to the shelf edge + the #68 sweep hops the last cell; driving OUT needs the FIRST hop shelf->ROAD, which the guard forbade. FIX (planner.inl, 1 line): extend the guard exemption from 'both cells road' to 'DESTINATION cell is road' -- i.e. stepping ONTO a road from any adjacent cell is always allowed (the road is ground-truth walkable). Now the shelf connects to the adjacent road, the BFS gets on the canyon road, and road->road steps carry the route SW to Timber -- exactly the canyon route #69 was built for; the executor then follows a NORMAL path (no wall-running, no bridge). Stepping OFF a road onto a non-road cliff stays guarded (asymmetric -> no new cliff-climbing). Balamb is a road-less continent (no road cells) so the condition is identical to before there -> no regression. The .97/.98 executor bridge stays in the code but goes DORMANT: with the road connected the BFS now reaches Timber (dist~0, expanded large), so the pocket test (expanded<=40 AND dist>=6) cannot fire. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + ROUTE_MAP_DIAG stay ON for this BAT. BAT .99: from a Dollet start, drive to Timber -- EXPECT the [PLAN] line to change from 'start already the closest reachable cell ... expanded 5 -- direct steer' to '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells ... nearest reachable to target ... dist=0 (or small), expanded LARGE'; [ROUTEMAP] route to run SW along the canyon road (a chain of 'o' on road cells, NOT an 'S' pocket); [YAWDRIVE] idx to CLIMB past 0/0 as he follows the path; NO [DRIVE] Pocketed line; and ARRIVAL at Timber. REGRESSION: an into-Dollet drive still arrives AND a Balamb drive still routes/arrives (road-less, condition unchanged). If the [PLAN] still says 'expanded 5' the shelf's neighbours toward the road are themselves blocked (then connect via a slightly wider road-onramp rule); if it routes onto the road but the EXECUTOR wedges trying to physically walk shelf->road in-game (a real one-way cliff), THEN the executor needs the lateral sweep -- but the route will at least point the right way. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.98: #70 FIX 2 -- the .97 bridge-out NEVER FIRED. The .97 BAT still wedged 32km out; the world log showed why: the Dollet->Timber drive takes PlanPathFine's EARLY-RETURN-FALSE branch ([PLAN] Fine BFS: start already the closest reachable cell ... expanded 5 -- direct steer => return false), NOT the success branch the .97 bridge was built around. The PlanDrivePath bridge decision was gated `if (ok && ...)` (PlanPathFine TRUE), so with ok=false it was skipped: the planner fell to direct-steer at Timber, nosed into the sealing cliff, 6 reverse-bursts, gave up (dist~32716). The bridge has TWO pocket shapes (.96 success-branch dist=37 expanded=8 AND .97 early-return expanded=5) and only the first was handled. FIX (planner.inl, 2 surgical edits): (1) set the pocket signal (s_drivePlanExpanded/s_drivePlanDist) on the early-return-FALSE branch too -- it previously set them ONLY on the success path, so the bridge read stale values when ok=false; (2) drop the `ok &&` from the bridge gate so it keys PURELY on the pocket signal (expanded<=WM_POCKET_MAX_EXPANDED(40) AND dist>=WM_POCKET_MIN_DIST(6) AND under WM_BRIDGE_MAX(6) attempts) regardless of PlanPathFine's return. Safe: the bridge block is reached ONLY right after PlanPathFine runs (all earlier PlanDrivePath returns precede it) so the signals are always fresh; normal drives expand hundreds + reach dist~0 so the pocket test cannot false-trigger. NOTHING else changed: the executor (drive.inl StartAutoDrive bridge reset, UpdateAutoDrive bridge-arrive re-plan, steer-target override) + state.inl (consts s_drive*/WM_POCKET_*/WM_BRIDGE_*) + FindNearestRoadWorld were already complete and correct from .97 (the earlier 'planner stripped' belief was a stale read). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG re-enabled to watch the [YAWDRIVE] bridge steer toward the road cell (set false before push). LESSON: world_map_planner.inl is CRLF -- edit_file multi-line anchors MUST use \r\n. BAT .98: from a Dollet start, drive to Timber -- EXPECT [DRIVE] Pocketed start fine(112,59) (expanded 5, dist N) -> BRIDGE-OUT to nearest road cell at world(X,Y); [YAWDRIVE] steering toward that road cell (steer(X,Y) = the road cell); [DRIVE] Bridged out of pocket (reached road) -> re-planning to Timber; a NEW [PLAN] Fine path FROM the road cell that REACHES Timber (dist~0, expanded large); then ARRIVAL. REGRESSION: an into-Dollet drive must still arrive AND a Balamb drive must NOT print any [DRIVE] Pocketed line. If he reaches the road but the geometric-nearest road cell is across a cliff (wrong ramp), refine FindNearestRoadWorld next. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.97: #70 FIX (Option A) -- universal departure BRIDGE-OUT. The .96 diag confirmed (3 ways: [ROUTEMAP]+[ELEVMAP]+F11) Dollet sits in an ~8-cell pocket = its coastal shelf at elev -1287, SEALED IN by the #69 .90 height-step guard (6 blocked cliff edges around the start) + the .81 box to the south. Driving IN works because the route rides the ROAD (road-to-road edges are guard-EXEMPT) to the shelf edge, then the #68 sweep hops the last cell IN -- a ONE-WAY bridge. Driving OUT, the start is ON the shelf (not a road cell) so every edge off it is guard-blocked -> trapped (planner expands 8, dead-ends 37 short). FIX = mirror the sweep at DEPARTURE: PlanDrivePath now exposes the pocket signal (s_drivePlanExpanded + s_drivePlanDist set at the end of PlanPathFine) and, when expanded<=WM_POCKET_MAX_EXPANDED(40) AND dist>=WM_POCKET_MIN_DIST(6) AND under WM_BRIDGE_MAX(6) attempts, finds the nearest ROAD cell (FindNearestRoadWorld ring-search over s_roadFine, radius 30) and sets s_driveBridgeActive + s_driveBridgeX/Y. UpdateAutoDrive then STEERS at the road cell (steerX/Y override, not the dead-end route) with the normal yaw 8-way + reverse-burst; once within WM_BRIDGE_ARRIVE_DIST(1536u ~=1.5 cells) of it, drops bridge mode + re-plans from there (road edges guard-exempt -> fresh plan routes normally to target). Bridge state resets per drive in StartAutoDrive. Only fires for genuine pockets (normal drives expand hundreds + reach dist~0) so NO regression to into-Dollet or Balamb. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STAYS ON for this BAT. Files: state.inl (consts+state), planner.inl (signal+FindNearestRoadWorld+PlanDrivePath decision), drive.inl (StartAutoDrive reset, bridge-arrive re-plan, steer override). BAT .97: reach Dollet, then drive to Timber -- expect [DRIVE] Pocketed start ... BRIDGE-OUT to nearest road cell, Squall to walk toward the road (SW/lower-left green), [DRIVE] Bridged out of pocket -> re-planning, a NEW [PLAN] from the road cell that REACHES Timber (dist~0, expanded large) + [ROUTEMAP] route now heading SOUTH, then ARRIVAL. PLUS regression: into-Dollet still arrives + a Balamb drive (bridge must NOT fire on either -- no [DRIVE] Pocketed line). Bounded to 6 attempts so it can't loop. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.96: #70 DIAGNOSTIC build (no behavior change) -- SEE the Dollet EXIT pocket. #69 chain is PUSHED (main=v0.18.3.95, commit c591803b). NEW #70: auto-drive OUT of Dollet wedges -- from the Dollet start fine(111,57) the planner expands only 8 cells (a pocket) and dead-ends 37 short of Timber. Driving INTO Dollet works because the #68 final-approach sweep bridges the last bit IN; that bridge is ONE-WAY, so departure strands the planner inside the pocket. DECIDED this is NOT Dollet-exclusive (the sweep is a general fallback; manual-walk-into-any-pocket has the same failure; small-disconnected-component is a general 1024u-grid property) -> the fix is a UNIVERSAL departure-side bridge-out, NOT widening the .85 override. Before building it, this DIAGNOSTIC flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG 0->1 (LOCAL only; main stays clean at .95) so the [ROUTEMAP]/[ELEVMAP] at plan time reveal the pocket's exact walls + whether an open exit faces the target (the bridge must push a direction that LEAVES the pocket, else it re-wedges). BAT .96: reach Dollet (auto-drive), THEN auto-drive to Timber; send Logs/ff8_world.log. Read the [ROUTEMAP] from the Dollet->Timber plan: the 'S' pocket, the '^' walls around it (incl the .81 box rows 59-69), and whether a '.' corridor opens south toward the target. That shapes the bridge-out (next build): when expanded<<dist, steer toward the target/nearest big-component cell with the executor+reverse-burst until the current cell joins the main component, then re-plan. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.95: #69 -- diagnostics OFF for the push. Flipped ROUTE_MAP_DIAG 1->0 in world_map_planner.inl, silencing the per-plan [ROUTEMAP]/[ELEVSTEP]/[ELEVMAP] dumps (DumpRouteMap + the elev logs compile out; elevBlockedEdges stays [[maybe_unused]]). NO behavior change -- routing/executor identical to .94 (which reached Dollet). This is the pre-push build: the #69 chain (.88-onward) is now ready to land on main (v0.18.3.87) as ONE commit, with the .90 height-step guard + .88 steepness as the real wins, the .81 AABB + .85 override KEPT (both load-bearing), and only WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY retired. BAT .95: confirm it still BUILDS CLEAN (watch for unused-variable warnings now the diag blocks compile out) and the Galbadia->Dollet on-foot drive still ARRIVES with the [ROUTEMAP]/[ELEVSTEP] lines GONE from ff8_world.log; PLUS a Balamb regression. If clean, push via Utilities/push_to_github.ps1 (Aaron only). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.94: #69 build 7 -- RESTORE the .85 road-walkable override (the actual cause of the .92/.93 regression). Reading the INITIAL plan (not a re-plan) settled it: from the SAME save start fine(99,72) that reached Dollet in .91, the .93 plan reported Dollet UNREACHABLE -- "Fine path: 14 cells ... nearest reachable to target fine(112,57), dist=15", a dead-end 15 cells short, wedging against the .81 box wall. The ONLY routing change from working-.91 to broken-.93 was removing the .85 override (offroad was already 0 -- a no-op). The Timber->Dollet road runs THROUGH the .81 box (cols 104-111 rows 59-69); the .85 override carves the road cells back to walkable so the route threads the box to Dollet, and without it the .81 patch walls the road off and DISCONNECTS Dollet. So BOTH the .81 AABB and the .85 override are LOAD-BEARING; only WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY (=0, no-op) was safely retired. This build restores the .85 override (segments.inl, right after the .81 block) -- back to the proven .91 routing. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STILL ON. BAT .94: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet from the usual save -- the [PLAN] Fine path must now REACH Dollet (dist~0, ~31 cells like .91) and ARRIVE; the [ROADMAP] override line should report road cells forced walkable; PLUS Balamb (B-Garden + Fire Cavern). If it arrives, the NEXT build flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false + pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward; .81 + .85 kept, only offroad retired) as ONE commit. LESSON: the #69 cleanup over-reached -- mechanism 2 (.90 height-step guard) improves general routing but does NOT replace the Dollet hardcodes; both stay. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.93: #69 build 6 -- RESTORE the .81 Dollet false-coast AABB (build 5 regression fix). The .92 BAT (which retired .81 + .85 + offroad) REGRESSED: with the .81 patch gone, the [ROUTEMAP] route ran straight through the ex-ledge (cols 104-110 = the AABB's old box) instead of the west lane, and the on-foot drive wedged ~15km out near the start (moved 62u, stuck ~fine(106,70)). [ELEVSTEP] still blocked 614 cliff edges and [ELEVMAP] still showed the 1513-unit cliff FACE -- mechanism 2 IS working -- but the ledge is impassable in-game where its elevation is CONTINUOUS (the route enters it via a <400 step), so the height-step guard cannot exclude it. That is exactly the original #67 finding (no pure-geometry rule separates this ledge from real land): mechanism 2 catches the cliff face, not the ledge approach. So the .81 AABB is LOAD-BEARING and is RESTORED (the forced-steep-mountain box in segments.inl + the DOLLET_COAST_* consts in state.inl). KEPT RETIRED from .92 (both confirmed safe): the .85 road-walkable override (the .92 [ROUTEMAP] showed the west lane walkable without it) and WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY (was already 0 -- a no-op). Net vs the working .91: only the .85 override + the offroad const/term are gone; routing is otherwise the proven .91 path. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STILL ON. BAT .93: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet -- MUST ARRIVE again, [ROUTEMAP] route back on the west lane avoiding the '^' ex-ledge box (now re-blocked); PLUS Balamb (B-Garden + Fire Cavern) regression. If it arrives clean, the NEXT build flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false + pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward; .81 kept, .85 + offroad retired) as ONE commit. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.92: #69 build 5 -- CLEANUP: retire the road-crutch scaffolding now that mechanism 2 carries the route. The .91 BAT proved it (Galbadia->Dollet route SAME shape with the road cost-preference gone, stayed off the false-coast '^' band; Balamb B-Garden + Fire Cavern regression still arrived). This build REMOVES: (1) the .85 road-walkable override (segments.inl -- it forced s_roadFine cells to SEG_LAND steep=0); (2) the .81 DOLLET_COAST AABB (the forced-steep-mountain patch in segments.inl + the DOLLET_COAST_* consts in state.inl); (3) WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY (planner.inl -- the const + its + term; already 0 since .91 so removing it is a no-op). KEPT: the height-step guard's road-to-road exemption (harmless -- it only skips the guard between two genuinely-walkable road cells; removing it is an unnecessary risk, so it stays and keeps reading s_roadFine). EFFECT TO TEST: the .81 AABB removal un-blocks the false-coast ledge cells as walkable NODES again, so the route now relies ENTIRELY on the height-step EDGE guard to stay off them -- in [ROUTEMAP] the ledge cells should flip from '^' back to '.' but still carry NO 'o' (route avoids them because the ~1570-unit cliff EDGES are guard-blocked, >400). ROUTE_MAP_DIAG STAYS ON for this BAT so we can SEE that. BAT .92: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet -- MUST still ARRIVE; [ROUTEMAP] route must still avoid the ledge (now '.' not '^') with no 'o' on it; [ELEVSTEP] blocked>0 (the guard now does ALL the cliff exclusion). PLUS Balamb on-foot (B-Garden + Fire Cavern) regression. Two failure modes the [ROUTEMAP] distinguishes: route cuts onto the ex-ledge -> the guard missed an entry edge (re-add the .81 AABB); route fails at a road pinch -> the .85 override removal blocked a needed cell (re-add it). If clean, the NEXT build flips ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false and pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward) as ONE commit. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.91: #69 build 4 -- RETIRE the road's cost-preference so the height-step guard (mechanism 2) does the routing. .90 proved the guard: it blocked 629 cliff edges and [ELEVMAP] showed the real false-coast cliff = a 1570-unit adjacent step vs WM_CLIMB_STEP=400 (walkable steps <400, Dollet reached) -- 400 well-centered. But the route still leaned on the road (the .86 cost preference: road step = cost 1, clearance-exempt). This build DROPS that branch in PlanPathFine: EVERY cell now pays the same clearance+steepness cost, and WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY 20->0 -- so the route is shaped by GEOMETRY (guard excludes cliffs, clearance centres the corridor, steepness pulls to the flat floor), not by the road being cheaper. KEPT this build (dropped as cleanup in build 5): the height-step guard's road-to-road exemption, the .85 road-walkable override, and the .81 DOLLET_COAST AABB -- so if Dollet stops arriving it was the cost change, not those. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG on. BAT .91: far on-foot Galbadia->Dollet -- MUST still ARRIVE, now routed by the guard+clearance not the road's cost advantage; the [ROUTEMAP] route must still avoid every '^'/false-land cell (the guard, not the road, keeps it off); [ELEVSTEP] blocked>0. PLUS a Balamb on-foot regression (road-less continent; should be ~identical since the offroad penalty was its only road-related term). If Dollet still reached + clean route, mechanism 2 is carrying the route alone -> build 5 drops the .85 override + .81 AABB + the now-dead road-to-road exemption + the offroad const, sets ROUTE_MAP_DIAG false, and pushes the #69 chain (.88-onward) as ONE commit. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.90: #69 build 3 -- MECHANISM 2: height-step edge adjacency (the CORE terrain-ID fix). New per-cell s_elevFine (avg of the poly's 3 vertex elevations) populated in RasterizeTriFine alongside s_steepFine; PlanPathFine now blocks an inter-cell edge when the absolute floor-height difference |s_elevFine[a]-s_elevFine[b]| > WM_CLIMB_STEP (=400, tunable) so a false-coast cliff SELF-EXCLUDES even though both cells read 'land' -- the grid is NODE-based, the cliff is an EDGE. Road-to-road steps are EXEMPT (keeps the Timber->Dollet ribbon connected while WM_CLIMB_STEP is calibrated; stepping OFF the road onto a non-road cliff is still guarded). Diagnostics (gated ROUTE_MAP_DIAG): [ELEVSTEP] logs how many cliff edges the guard blocked during expansion; [ELEVMAP] (in DumpRouteMap) logs the window's max adjacent elev-step + location + the S/D cell elevations, so the REAL false-coast cliff-step magnitude is visible to set WM_CLIMB_STEP. This is the geometry-based replacement that will let later builds retire the .85/.86 road crutch + the .81 Dollet AABB. BAT .90: on-foot far (>=15km) Galbadia->Dollet -- the route should STILL reach Dollet (the road carries it via the exemption, so no disconnect risk yet); expect [ELEVSTEP] blocked>0 (guard firing) and [ELEVMAP] to reveal the cliff step at the false coast; ALSO Balamb on-foot regression (route ~identical; guard only blocks genuine cliff edges). If blocked>0 + Dollet reached + no [ROUTEMAP] cut onto a '^'/false-land cell, mechanism 2 is wired correctly and build 4 retires the road crutch (offroad->0, drop .85/.86) so the guard does the real work; if the [ELEVMAP] cliff step is far above/below 400, tune WM_CLIMB_STEP. Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.89: #69 build 2 -- WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY 40->20 (start retiring the road's flat cost-advantage; road stays clearance-exempt so it still wins the corridor; ROUTE_MAP_DIAG ON). BAT .89: far Galbadia->Dollet (route ~unchanged, no cut off the road onto a false-land/'^' cell) + Balamb regression. // v0.18.3.88: #69 build 1 -- steepness-weighted planning (steepPen = s_steepFine/8 on the non-road step cost only; road still cost 1, additive, no Dollet regression). BAT'd CLEAN: far ~13.2km (dist=13235) Galbadia->Dollet on-foot, [ROUTEMAP] threaded the canyon up the open lane to Dollet hugging steep~=0, arrived sweep-assisted (= the #68 executor end-game, expected). Detail in CHANGELOG.md. // v0.18.3.87: #67 -- YAW-BASED SCREEN-RELATIVE 8-WAY on-foot steering (the executor fix the road work isolated). The .86 road-preference BAT SOLVED ROUTING: the [PLAN]/[ROUTEMAP] route follows road cells up col 99 then bends east along the road to Dollet, and F11 showed Squall PHYSICALLY STANDING ON THE PAVED ROAD -- but he still wedged at the START (idx=0/32, ~19.5km from this farther save), oscillating, and the trace shows EXACTLY why and it is NOT routing: he is ON the road, the road runs due north here (tgtBrg ~4070 ~= 358deg), but hdg=3752 is FROZEN (the fixed per-region CAMERA YAW ~= 330deg), so the .80 heading turn-then-go pressed pure UP -- which on foot WALKS at the yaw (NNW), ~28deg off the road -- and that drift carried him off the thin road ribbon into the cliff beside it (d+0 on contact; reverse shoved him back; UP walked him into it again). The heading-law can only press pure cardinals (UP, or R/L pivot), so it can never aim the DIAGONAL needed to track a road bearing between N and NE. This is the .80 unify failure: .80 routed on-foot onto the vehicle heading law on the (correct) finding that UP walks at the yaw, but a yaw you can READ is not a facing you can STEER -- L/R are screen-relative WALK keys, not a rotation, so turn-then-go is meaningless on foot. FIX (the correct use of .79's camera-yaw disassembly, which .80 misused): steer in SCREEN space. screenAngle = (targetBearing - yaw) & 0xFFF is the target's direction relative to screen-up (= what UP walks); press the NEAREST OF 8 ARROW COMBOS (cardinals + diagonals: sector = ((screenAngle+256)>>9)&7, UP / UP+RIGHT / RIGHT / DOWN+RIGHT / DOWN / DOWN+LEFT / LEFT / UP+LEFT) so he walks toward the target and STAIRCASES along the road's bends. At the .86 wedge screenAngle ~= +318 (28deg CW of screen-up) -> sector 1 = UP+RIGHT -> walks NNE, AWAY from the cliff and along the road, instead of NNW into it. The on-foot branch is RE-SPLIT off the vehicle heading law (the dead if(false&&isOnFoot) screen-basis/probe block stays as dead code; a new else-if(isOnFoot) holds the yaw steering; vehicles keep the unchanged heading turn-then-go else). Routing/cursor/steer-target+LOS-clamp/stuck/replan/reverse-burst/final-approach ALL unchanged; the reverse-burst un-wedge (DOWN=screen-down) is kept for foot recovery. New [YAWDRIVE] trace logs yaw/tgtBrg/screenAngle/keys. ONE handedness assumption: RIGHT walks screen-right = yaw+90 CW; if the BAT shows him tracking bends the WRONG way (steering consistently to the wrong side at a turn), swap RIGHT<->LEFT (one line). BAT .87: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [YAWDRIVE] to press UP+RIGHT (not pure UP) at the start, Squall to walk NNE ALONG the road instead of NNW into the cliff, idx to CLIMB past 0, dist to fall past 19.5km, ideally ARRIVAL staircasing along the road; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- vehicles are untouched, but confirm foot still works on a road-less continent where the route is open ground). Watch: (a) handedness (wrong-way at a turn -> swap R/L); (b) do 2-key diagonals combine into diagonal movement (if not, he'll jerk between the two cardinals -- still nets toward target, but the fallback is time-multiplexed cardinal staircasing). If he now follows the road but stalls at a TIGHT bend, that's a local-geometry/lookahead tune, not the steering model. After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.86: #67 -- ROAD-PREFERENCE in the planner (build 3 of the road pivot; makes the route FOLLOW the road instead of leaving it). The .85 road-walkable override BAT SOLVED routing in principle (the planner now CAN route the road -- 623 road cells forced walkable, the ribbon reconnected) but the drive still wedged ~13.5km out at the START, oscillating, idx=0/20, never advancing. F11 at the wedge = Squall at the FOOT OF A COASTAL CLIFF, and the [ROADMAP]/[ROUTEMAP] showed why: the start fine(101,65) IS a road cell (row 65 cols 100-105 are road) and the road winds continuously NE up to Dollet, BUT the route LEFT the road at fine(101,63) -- which is LAND, not road -- where the road jogs EAST to cols 104-107; the planner cut straight up col 101 onto the non-road false-land cliff (a couple cells shorter than following the road's bend) and drove into the rock. ROOT: the clearance-weighted Dijkstra had NO road awareness -- it cut corners off the road wherever a non-road shortcut was shorter, straight into the false-land cliffs the road exists to avoid. FIX: road-preference in PlanPathFine's step cost -- a step onto a road cell (s_roadFine, already populated unconditionally since .84) costs 1 with NO clearance penalty (the road IS the corridor, needs no centering); a step onto a NON-road cell pays the clearance penalty PLUS a flat WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY=40, so any multi-cell off-road shortcut loses to staying on the road and the route FOLLOWS the road's bends to Dollet. Penalty is finite, so non-road terrain stays usable for the short start/end hops and on road-less continents (Balamb: with no road cells competing, every step pays the same flat penalty -> relative costs and the route are UNCHANGED -- no regression). This addresses the .81 lesson that road-attraction fights the clearance penalty by ZEROING the clearance term on road cells. NOTE the on-foot EXECUTOR is still .80's heading turn-then-go, which can't truly steer on foot (hdg=0x0203ED02 is the fixed per-region CAMERA YAW, not a controllable facing; the .85 trace showed it frozen at 3783 pressing U into the cliff) -- but at the .85 wedge BOTH the heading-law and a correct yaw-based steerer would press the same arrow (target ~16deg off screen-up), so the EXECUTOR was not the differentiator there; the ROUTE pointing into the cliff was. Once the route follows the road (real walkable ground), if the executor then mis-steers at the road's turns, the NEXT build is the yaw-based screen-relative steering (read the yaw at 0x0203ED02, arrow = nearest(targetBearing - yaw); replaces .80's turn-then-go on foot, vehicles keep the heading law) -- the correct use of the camera-yaw disassembly, which .80 misused as a steerable facing. BAT .86: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect the [ROUTEMAP] route to STAY on the road (follow the eastward jog at fine(101,63)->cols 104-107, NOT cut straight up col 101 into the cliff), the drive to actually progress past 13.5km along the road, and ideally ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- road-preference changes every planned route's cost, though road-less routes should be identical). If the route now hugs the road but the executor still oscillates at a road turn, that isolates the executor -> do the yaw-based steering next. After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.85: #67 -- ROAD-WALKABLE OVERRIDE (build 2 of the road pivot; the executor/planner now route on the reconnected road). The .84 [ROADMAP] dump CONFIRMED the road: terrain types 27/28 present (3605+1875 polys), 623 road fine cells, the ribbon reaches Dollet (R adjacent to D at fine(113,57)), and the Dollet-drive start fine(102,64) sits ON the road. It also revealed (a) WHY the planner kept hitting the cliff -- the road does NOT go straight up fine col 102; at rows 62-64 it jogs EAST to cols 104-107 and climbs to Dollet from there, while col 102 reads as walkable LAND in the grid but is the in-game cliff (and col 102 dead-ends into ocean at row 58, so straight-north was never a through path); and (b) that a large stretch of the road was being FORCE-BLOCKED by our own grid -- the [ROADMAP] '!' (road-over-blocked) cells cluster at rows 59-69 cols 104-111, EXACTLY the AABB the .81 Dollet false-coast patch blocks (the patch sits on top of the real road), plus a few road cells running through steep-mtn-classified cells. THIS build makes the road authoritative: after rasterization AND the .81 patch, every s_roadFine cell is forced to SEG_LAND steep=0 (walkable), overriding both the steep-mtn misclassification and the .81 patch wherever they fall on the road -- so the road becomes one continuous walkable ribbon from the start to Dollet, while non-road cells (incl. the rest of the false ledge) keep their classification. Applied BEFORE the clearance BFS so reachability/clearance/planner all see the road as the open corridor; through the cliff pinch the road is now the ONLY walkable path, so the existing clearance-weighted Dijkstra is funneled onto it with NO planner rewrite. New [ROADMAP] 'road-walkable override' log line reports how many road cells were forced (and how many had been blocked). The [ROADMAP] dump (still on) now renders the previously-'!' cells as 'R' = built-in confirmation. BAT .85: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect the [ROADMAP] override line (wasBlk>0, ~the .81 patch's road cells + steep-mtn road cells), the dump's '!' cells flipped to 'R', the [PLAN]/[ROUTEMAP] route to jog EAST onto the road around the col-102 cliff (not drive straight into it), dist past 12km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- road-forcing changes the grid, though Balamb's route is on a different continent). If the route now follows the road but the EXECUTOR wanders off it in the OPEN stretches (steering works on open ground per .66, so this is unlikely to block), build 3 adds an explicit road-preference/restriction to the planner. If he still wedges, the [HDG-DIAG]/[ROUTEMAP] show whether it's the route (still off the road) or the executor (on the road but mis-steering). After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.84: #67 -- ROAD extraction (load-only diagnostic; PIVOT from inferred-terrain steering to the road as ground-truth). Three steering BATs (.80/.82/.83) all wedged ~12km because the planner routes up col 102 into an in-game CLIFF that the 1024u grid mis-classifies as walkable LAND -- confirmed by F11 (Squall at the foot of a sheer rock face) + the trace (he walks freely north to the row-63/64 boundary then cannot cross; reverse frees him south; he never enters fine row 63). ROOT (answer to 'why do we keep hitting mountains'): the mod does NOT read true walkability -- it rasterizes wmx polygons into a coarse 1024u grid and GUESSES walkable from terrain-type + a steepness threshold (WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK) tuned for reachability not collision; both guesses fail at cliffs (a cliff face + the flat ground at its base land in adjacent cells; the rasterizer marks a cell land if any land poly touches it) and narrow passes. Aaron's fix (correct): the wmx tags ROAD polys (terrain 27/28 = Road/Railroad) -- the one signal both reliably walkable AND running exactly Timber->Dollet. Route ALONG the road instead of inferring terrain, and use the road as ground-truth to refine the grid (road cells = walkable; off-road 'land' cells wedged between the road and a mountain band are suspect cliff). THIS build is step 1, pure observation, ZERO behavior change: LoadTerrainGrid now captures road polys into a new s_roadFine[192][256] overlay (RasterizeTriRoad: centre-in-triangle PLUS the 3 vertex + centroid cells, so a sub-cell-thin ribbon still registers), tallies a terrain-type histogram, and (ROAD_MAP_DIAG) logs [ROADMAP]: road fine-cell count, the type:polys histogram (confirms 27/28 exist + how many), and an ASCII map of the Dollet corridor (rows 45..76 cols 88..121) with the road ribbon over the fine class grid (R=road, !=road-over-a-cell-we-currently-block, D=Dollet, ~/./f/m/^). BAT .84: load a GALBADIA world-map save (NO driving needed -- the dump fires once at terrain load), send Logs/ff8_world.log. Read [ROADMAP]: (1) histogram confirms road=27/28 present; (2) road fine cells > 0 and the ribbon forms a continuous chain to D; (3) any '!' = road cells we wrongly block. Build 2 then routes the Dollet drive ALONG s_roadFine + forces road cells walkable + flags suspect off-road cliff cells. ROAD_MAP_DIAG retires before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.83: #67 -- FORWARD-COLLISION GUARD on the steering law (a step-back redesign toward terrain-reactive execution). The .82 LOS-clamp WORKED (the steer target moved to the clamped near cell fine(102,63), confirmed in [HDG-DIAG] steer(-26112,-33280)) but he STILL wedged ~12km out at the SAME cliff corner, and the trace pinpointed the proximate cause: target NNW, hdg due-north 36, off=318 -- JUST inside the ~320 STEER_DEADZONE -- so the law chose STRAIGHT and walked him due north into the blocked fine(103,63) the route goes WEST around; the stuck-reverse backed him off then the unchanged law walked him right back (reverse->re-aim->re-wedge), hdg frozen at 36 the entire time (he never pivoted because off never exceeded the deadzone). Re-eval (Aaron-requested): the executor is an open-loop bearing-follower BLIND to the terrain directly in front of the character -- no target-selection tweak fixes that, because 'roughly aimed' within the deadzone can still be aimed into a wall. FIX (first step toward reactive local avoidance): before the final STRAIGHT branch commits UP, probe the fine cell ~1 cell ahead of the CURRENT facing (heading->world vector, one WM_FINE_CELL out); if it's blocked for foot/car (SEG_OCEAN or steep SEG_MOUNTAIN), set fwdGuard and press toward the target side (err sign: RIGHT if err>=0 else LEFT) instead of UP. On this region's screen-relative controls (hdg is the FIXED per-region camera yaw -- frozen at 36 through STRAIGHT/PIVOT/REVERSE incl. a 600u reverse, the .73 behavior, NOT .80's rotate-the-facing) that walks him sideways WEST into the open col-102 corridor; on a turn-then-go region it pivots him there. The guard reads the grid in front of him so it fires ONLY near a wall -- open-road steering + the deadzone are unchanged (no orbit/oscillation regression, preserves .60/.78). Reuses the existing pivot key choice; no new state, no planner/target change (the .82 clamp stays). [HDG-DIAG] gains a GUARD marker (a PIVOT line with off<=deadzone + GUARD = the guard fired). BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] at the corner to show GUARD with keys=--L- (or --R-) when the cell ahead is blocked, him to move WEST around the cliff instead of pinning on it, dist past 12km, ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- the guard is in every planned drive's steering, but only acts adjacent to a wall). If he moves west but then can't make the next turn, the next step is the full feeler-fan controller (probe several headings, pick the open one best toward target) + a blocked-direction memory for convergent recovery. If he STILL pins, the trace shows whether GUARD fired and which key -- tells us if the issue is the guard or the control model. After a clean BAT set all #67 diags false and push .54-onward as ONE commit. // v0.18.3.82: #67 -- TIGHT-CORNER navigation: clamp the steer target to a clear line of sight + give on-foot the REVERSE un-wedge. The .81 patch WORKED (route went up the west canyon, dist 15.8km->11km) but he wedged at a real cliff corner fine(103,64): the F11 shows him pressed against a rock massif with open grass to his WEST, and [HDG-DIAG] shows the steer target aimed NNW (-26112,-35328) straight ACROSS the blocked fine(103,63) cliff that the winding route goes AROUND, hdg frozen at 36 (due north), pos sawing east-west, idx stuck at 0/29. Root: (1) the far ~2400u lookahead target sat on the far side of a cliff corner, so he pressed north INTO the rock; on the world map's tank controls rotation needs forward motion, so nosed into terrain he could neither advance nor pivot out. (2) WORSE -- on-foot had NO working recovery: the .80 unify routed on-foot STEERING to the vehicle heading law (if (false && isOnFoot) dead-branched the screen-relative path) but left on-foot RECOVERY pointing at the SIDESTEP, whose only consumer is inside that dead branch -- so the stuck check logged 'SIDESTEP left' and pressed NOTHING. TWO complementary fixes (separable in the trace): (a) wire the pre-written FineLineClearFootCar clamp -- walk the lookahead target back to the farthest route cell reachable by a clear straight line, so he aims ALONG the open corridor (here due WEST around the cliff) and turns there in open ground BEFORE reaching the rock; engages only near walls (open ground keeps the far target -> no orbit, preserves .78). (b) on-foot stuck recovery now fires the SAME DOWN reverse-burst as vehicles (tank controls: only reverse frees a nosed-in character; a lateral sidestep can't, and DOWN moves him on foot per .71) -- backs him off into open ground where the pivot can rotate him around the corner. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] steer target to track the open corridor (NOT a fixed NNW point across a cliff), keys=---D reverse bursts if he does wedge, dist past 11km, ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes lookahead + recovery for every planned drive). If he still wedges: the trace shows whether the clamp moved the steer target (steerX/Y) and whether reverse fired (D key) -- tells us which half to push. After a clean BAT set all #67 diags false and push .54-onward as ONE commit. // v0.18.3.81: #67 -- HARDCODED Dollet false-coast no-walk patch (Aaron-approved one-off). After the .80 heading-unify BAT still wedged ~15.8km out at the east->north coastal corner, offline wmx.obj analysis found the ROOT cause is the PLANNER, not the executor: the route runs up a thin coastal cliff ledge SE of Dollet that reads as walkable LAND in wmx but is impassable on foot in-game, and NO geometric rule separates it from real land (validated offline across 7 rules -- blanket cliff-block AND coastal-ledge-block both DISCONNECT same-continent Dollet from the start; 512-res reconnects the road but breaks the Balamb Town->Fire Cavern regression; road-attraction reroutes only with the clearance penalty disabled and is parameter-fragile). The Timber->Dollet canyon road (wmx terrain types 27/28) one cell WEST is the real foot approach; the planner ignored it because the fine grid discards the road tag and the ledge looked more direct. Since Dollet is a one-off (coast on one side, canyon road on the other), LoadTerrainGrid now marks the ledge AABB impassable by COORDINATE (DOLLET_COAST_X0/X1/Y0/Y1 in world_map_state.inl), implemented as forced-steep MOUNTAIN (steep=0xFFFF) so the existing foot/car block rule (cls==SEG_MOUNTAIN && steep>WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK) catches it with ZERO change to IsFineTraversable; Garden/Ragnarok bypass the fine grid so are unaffected. Offline-validated against the LIVE clearance-weighted planner cost: with the patch the route runs WEST up the canyon to Dollet (not north up the coast) and Dollet stays reachable; Balamb is untouched (different cells, different continent). BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [TERRAIN] 'Dollet false-coast patch: blocked N fine cells', the [ROUTEMAP] route to run west/up the canyon rather than north up the coast, dist to drop past 15.8km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes the Galbadia-area grid only, but confirm Balamb destinations still route). If it arrives, set all #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) false and push the .54-onward chain as one commit. // v0.18.3.80: #67 -- UNIFY on-foot steering onto the heading-based turn-then-go (the vehicle law). The v0.18.3.79 [YAWPROBE] BAT was decisive and POSITIVE: with the character actually walking (footsteps confirmed), holding UP walked a STRAIGHT line whose COMPASS bearing == the camera yaw (0x0203ED02), matched within 1deg at TWO different yaws in the SAME run (the camera rotated mid-run: yaw 316deg -> walked 315deg; yaw 355deg -> walked 356deg); holding UP did NOT rotate the camera (yaw steady through each forward burst); and RIGHT INCREASED the yaw (clockwise). So 0x0203ED02 is the LIVE on-foot facing -- NOT frozen (the .72/.73 'frozen' reads were the character wedged/pressing UP into terrain, which doesn't rotate) and NOT screen-relative-per-region. The relationship has ZERO offset in the game's own bearing units: desiredYaw == the target's TorusBearing (0=N, CW), exactly what the existing VEHICLE steering already computes (err=TorusBearing-heading; if |off|>STEER_DEADZONE pivot RIGHT when err>=0 since RIGHT raises the yaw, else LEFT; else UP) and which already works for cars. FIX: the on-foot executor branch (the .74-.78 screen-basis + .77 greedy arrow-probe, built on the now-disproven frozen-heading reading) is BYPASSED -- gated `if (false && isOnFoot)`, left in place as dead code like the retired DCAL calibration -- so on foot now falls through to the SAME heading turn-then-go as vehicles. No other behavior change; routing/cursor/waypoint/stuck/replan/sweep/reverse-burst/final-approach all unchanged. The on-foot [HDG-DIAG] trace (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG) now logs hdg/tgtBrg/err/off/keys/band for the foot drive. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] hdg to track tgtBrg, err to shrink as he aligns then STRAIGHT frames carrying him along the route, dist dropping past 15.8km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this routes foot through the previously vehicle-only branch). If he wedges at a tight corner and can't pivot out (the old .66-.72 failure mode: turning needs a little open space), that is now a GEOMETRY/recovery issue, not a steering-law issue -- the clearance router already avoids tight corners and the reverse-burst un-wedge exists; tune those, do NOT revert to the probe. Set all #67 diags false before the push. // v0.18.3.79: #67 -- DIAGNOSTIC build, no steering change. Offline FF8_EN.exe disassembly (2026-06-22) located the world-map CAMERA: 0x0203ED02 = camera YAW (0..4096 = 360deg; live value 501 ~= 44deg), fed into the view-matrix builder at .text 0x552D20 (PSX RotMatrix/ApplyMatrix lib), SET on region transitions (writers 0x548155/0x54A1C8/0x54D030) and eased toward a target by a follow routine at 0x558590 (<=16 units/frame); 0x0203FE52 = camera PITCH (~45deg downward tilt). KEY REFRAME: the mod has been reading 0x0203ED02 ALL ALONG as GetWorldMapHeading() and wrongly dismissing it as a frozen, useless 'character heading' -- it is the CAMERA YAW, frozen on foot because the on-foot world-map camera holds a FIXED yaw PER REGION, which is exactly why holding one arrow walks a STRAIGHT line whose WORLD direction differs by region (Aaron-confirmed). This supersedes the entire heading/basis/probe steering arc (.66-.78): with the yaw readable, navigation needs no empirical basis and no orbit-prone probe -- read the yaw each frame, rotate the world->target bearing by -yaw, press the nearest arrow, re-read on region change. This build REPLACES the F12 diagnostic body only (world_map_camera_scan.inl, SAME external symbols TriggerCameraScan/UpdateCameraScan/CamScanPause/CamScanResume so dinput8.cpp + Poll() need no rewiring): retired the dead .73 G/H camera-rotate scan; new [YAWPROBE] holds UP ~1.2s (sampling pos+yaw every 250ms), then RIGHT, then UP again, logging the net world vector per hold + the computed offset(UP-walk bearing vs yaw) + turn sense. It CONFIRMS the yaw drives movement and MEASURES the one constant the steering rewrite needs. BAT: on the world map standing STILL (not driving), press F12, hold still ~6s (fully automated, TTS on start/complete; if a random encounter interrupts, fight it and the probe auto-restarts), send Logs/ff8_world.log; then WALK into the next region and press F12 again -- in the [YAWPROBE] SUMMARY the yaw should DIFFER between regions but offsetUP should MATCH (proves yaw-driven movement) and sense(RIGHT-UP) should be ~+/-90. No steering/routing change this build. Next build = the yaw-based steering rewrite using the measured offset. CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG + the remaining #67 diags retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.78: #67 -- fix the greedy-probe ORBIT by aiming at a FAR route target instead of the adjacent cell. The .77 BAT (greedy arrow-probe, built+run) proved the PROBE STEERING WORKS -- it correctly presses cardinals and moves him on open ground -- but he STILL hung ~15.8km out, and the trace + F11 screenshot revealed why, and it's NOT a wall: the F11 shows him standing on open green grass with a clear field to the north (cliffs/Dollet road far in the background), and the [SRDIAG] trace shows idx=0/22 the WHOLE time (cursor never advances past the first route cell) with tgtBrg swinging wildly (231->255->329->152->...) every quarter-second. That swing is the signature of ORBITING: the steer target was the IMMEDIATELY ADJACENT route cell (DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS=1, ~1024u away), so every ~180u walk step swung the bearing to it ~15deg, the probe re-decided every 250ms window, and he circled the first waypoint on row 70 instead of committing to a direction and crossing north into the open field. FIX: aim the steer target at the first route cell at least DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST (~2400u) ahead along the path (the OLD pre-.64 rule), not the adjacent cell. A ~2400u target swings only ~4deg per step, so the bearing is STABLE and the probe locks onto the northward cardinal and carries him up the corridor, advancing the cursor. The far target was cut to 1 cell back in .64 ONLY because the THEN heading-pivot steering drove straight at a far target and jammed into walls -- the greedy probe does NOT jam (it rejects walled cardinals via the <MIN_MOVE test and staircases around obstacles), so a far target is now both SAFE and NECESSARY to stop the orbit. One change; the probe logic, cursor advance, stuck/replan, sidestep, and vehicle path are all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] expect tgtBrg to now hold ROUGHLY STEADY (not swing 100s of units every line), idx to CLIMB past 0 as he advances up the route, dist to drop past 15.8km, and him to walk north across the open field toward the canyon instead of circling; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes every planned drive's target selection). If he still orbits, raise DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST further or lower DRIVE_PROBE_ALIGN_MIN so the probe will take wider detours; if he commits to a direction but jams against real geometry, that's a grid/route issue at that cell. Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.77: #67 -- PIVOT the on-foot steering executor off the maintained screen->world basis to a GREEDY EMPIRICAL ARROW PROBE. Three BATs (.74 stale basis / .75 corrupt basis / .76 guard-frozen basis) all produced the SAME outcome: clean eastward progress to ~15.8km then sawing east-west at the route corner, never turning north -- which per the project's own rule (3 identical outcomes = wrong diagnosis, pivot) means the maintained-basis approach is the wrong tool. The .76 trace was decisive: even with the guard HOLDING the basis stable at uHat~(1,0), pressing the basis-predicted U-L- combo drove him WEST (basis predicted NNE) and he oscillated dist 15887<->17404 on row 70, never going north. A predicted screen->world basis is unreliable exactly where the camera swings (the corner), and 2-key diagonals don't combine as the basis assumes. The route itself is FINE (planner threads the gap; DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS=1 aims at the adjacent next cell) -- the executor just can't convert 'go toward that cell' into the right arrow. NEW APPROACH: trust ONLY measured progress. Hold ONE cardinal arrow for DRIVE_PROBE_WINDOW_MS(250); measure the window's displacement; KEEP the arrow if it both moved him (>=DRIVE_PROBE_MIN_MOVE=40u) AND lined up with the target dir (align >= DRIVE_PROBE_ALIGN_MIN=0.55, ~within 57deg); else ROTATE to the next cardinal (UP->RIGHT->DOWN->LEFT). No basis, no trig, no diagonals -- immune to camera swing and wall-slide (it only believes what it measures). At the corner this naturally STAIRCASES: the walled direction fails the MIN_MOVE test and it takes the open cardinal that actually progresses toward the next route cell. DRIVE_PROBE_MAX_FAILS(8) non-progressing windows in a row = genuine pocket -> re-plan. Calibration (the UP/RIGHT probe) is DISARMED (its basis is now unused); RefreshBasisAxis/OrthonormalizeAgainst/the basis state are now dead (left in place, like prior unused helpers). Cursor/steer-target/sidestep/stuck/replan/vehicle path all UNCHANGED. [SRDIAG] now logs arrow=UP/RIGHT/DOWN/LEFT + fails count instead of uHat/rHat. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [SRDIAG] to settle on a single arrow that drops dist, STAIRCASE through the corner (arrow flips between two cardinals as it rounds it) WITHOUT the backward rout, and progress past 15.8km; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). If he still can't round the corner, the trace shows which arrows the probe tried and whether ANY progressed (if none, it's a true terrain pocket -> grid/route fix; if one did but he didn't follow, tune the window/align). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.76: #67 -- add a SLIDE-REJECTION guard to the screen-relative basis refresh. The .75 BAT was real progress: the refresh fix WORKED on open ground -- uHat/rHat tracked smoothly (they were frozen in .74) and he made the best on-foot progress yet, dist 16754->15807 (~950u) following the route east then starting north. But at the route corner he stalled against a blocked cell and then exactly the predicted wall-slide corruption hit: uHat rotated ~90deg from (-0.05,-1.00) to (1.00,-0.09) as the per-tick refresh learned from wall-slide/jitter motion (his real motion follows the wall, not the pressed arrow), and the corrupted basis then drove him BACKWARD -- dist climbed 15807->17404, farther out than he started. FIX: only refine the basis when the observed motion AGREES with the pressed arrow's predicted screen direction (dot > DRIVE_BASIS_AGREE_MIN=0.50, ~within 60deg) -- gentle camera tracking on open ground always agrees (~0.95) and passes; perpendicular/reversed wall-slide motion disagrees and is rejected, preserving the good basis. No other behavior change; threshold/Gram-Schmidt/calibration/decision/sidestep/vehicle path all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] uHat/rHat should track smoothly on open ground AND now HOLD steady (not swing ~90deg) when he contacts a wall, dist should keep dropping past 15.8km without the backward rout, and he should reach the route corner with a correct basis. Watch: (a) does uHat stay sane through the corner now; (b) does he still STALL at the corner (the .75 trace showed him nosing north into a blocked cell ~fine(104,69)=^ before the route's eastward leg -- if so the next fix is path-following/corner, NOT the basis) or does the sidestep round it; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.75: #67 -- FIX the screen-relative steering's dead basis-refresh (root cause of the .74 jam). The .74 BAT [SRDIAG] trace showed the approach WORKING at first (dist 15337->15028 while moving ~186u/frame) then wedging at the same ~15km coastal neck, and the smoking gun: uHat/rHat NEVER changed across the entire 15km drive (logged uHat(0.35,-0.93) rHat(0.93,0.36) on every line). The live basis-refresh was gated by DRIVE_BASIS_REFRESH_MIN=40, but per-tick walk motion is only ~15u (186u per 200ms / ~12 ticks), so refresh NEVER fired -- the basis stayed frozen at the drive-start camera angle while the world camera swung over 15km, so by the neck 'press UP' no longer meant north and steering drove him into the coast (keys=U--- forever, d+3-5, tgtBrg drifting). FIX: DRIVE_BASIS_REFRESH_MIN 40->10 so the basis tracks actual per-tick walking motion (still excludes wedge jitter <1u/tick); and after refreshing one axis from single-arrow motion, Gram-Schmidt the OTHER axis perpendicular to it (new OrthonormalizeAgainst) so the frame rotates rigidly with the camera and can't collapse (steering presses UP most of the time, so only uHat would refresh otherwise). No other behavior change; calibration, decision, sidestep, vehicle path all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] expect uHat/rHat to now MOVE smoothly as he drives (tracking the camera) and dist to keep dropping past 15km; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). Watch uHat in the trace: if it tracks smoothly and he progresses, the stale-basis bug is fixed; if instead uHat swings WILDLY when he slides along a wall, the next step is a slide-rejection guard (only refine when observed motion agrees with the basis). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.74: #67 -- REBUILD on-foot world-map steering as SCREEN-RELATIVE self-calibrating control (the real fix the .73 diagnostic pointed to). The .73 [CAMDIAG] BAT was decisive and NEGATIVE for the camera approach: injecting the manual's world-map camera keys (G=CCW, H=CW) did NOTHING -- across all 5 H taps and 5 G taps the hdng(0x0203ED00)/save(0x01CFFEB8) windows never changed and WM_HEADING stayed frozen at 3595; the only foot-window changes were the residual of the preceding UP-walk settling, not a per-tap camera ramp. So G/H don't rotate the camera in this build and there's no camera-angle field to read. BUT the scan confirmed the walk basis is clean and stable: UP-walk measured (-286,-286) three times (NW here), vs (+132,-132)=NE at the Dollet jam -- i.e. on foot the arrows WALK screen-relative and the screen->world mapping differs by region (the world camera faces different ways in different places), which is exactly why every heading-based steering attempt failed. FIX (foot drives only; vehicles keep the heading law): UpdateAutoDrive now MEASURES the screen->world basis from the character's own motion and presses the arrow combo whose screen direction points at the target. At drive start it calibrates -- press UP ~320ms -> world delta = screen-up unit vector uHat; settle; press RIGHT ~320ms -> screen-right unit vector rHat (retries up to 3x; perpendicular fallback if a probe can't move, corrected by live refresh) -- logged [SRDIAG] CAL. Then each tick it decomposes the wrapped world vector to the steer target onto (uHat,rHat): press UP if the screen-up component > cos(67.5deg), DOWN if < -that, RIGHT/LEFT likewise (8-way, diagonals allowed), and refreshes uHat/rHat live from any single-arrow motion (EMA) so the basis tracks the camera as it swings across the map. No heading, no pivot, no camera control, no reverse-into-loop. The on-foot stuck recovery is now a SIDESTEP (slide laterally past an obstacle, alternating sides) on the first stuck window, then the existing mid-route re-plan; vehicles keep the DOWN reverse burst. ALL routing/arrival/sweep/planner-eligibility scaffolding is UNCHANGED (the route was always fine; only the executor was broken). New state + constants in world_map_state.inl (DriveCalPhase, s_camU*/s_camR*, sidestep, DRIVE_CAL_*/STEER_AXIS_C/DRIVE_BASIS_*); helpers WrapWorldDelta + RefreshBasisAxis in world_map_drive.inl. Throttled [SRDIAG] trace logs pos/d+moved/tgtBrg/up+rt components/uHat/rHat/keys/SIDESTEP. BAT: on the world map on foot, select a far destination (Dollet) and press \ to auto-drive -- expect a brief UP-then-RIGHT calibration wobble at the start ([SRDIAG] CAL lines), then [SRDIAG] frames where up/rt pick the arrows toward the target and d+ stays POSITIVE (real progress, no eternal loop), and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- simple-coord, planner-ineligible, so only sidestep recovery). Read [SRDIAG]: if uHat/rHat look sane and the chosen arrows drive him toward tgtBrg, it's working; an inverted axis sign would show immediately as wrong arrows vs tgtBrg and is a one-line fix. The remaining #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.73: #67 -- DIAGNOSTIC build, no steering change yet. The v0.18.3.72 BAT trace was decisive: WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02) is FROZEN at 501 across the whole jam -- unchanged through PIVOT, STRAIGHT and REVERSE frames AND while he walked 600+ units in every direction during the DOWN bursts -- so it is NOT the on-foot facing, and the entire closed-loop heading-pivot steering (.66-.72) has been steering against a dead constant. On-foot world-map movement is DIRECTIONAL WALK (arrows walk screen-relative; measured from the trace: UP -> world (+132,-132), DOWN -> world (-132,+138), camera tilted ~45deg). UP walks him NE into the east coast -> pins; stuck-check fires DOWN -> walks him SW into open land; reverse ends -> UP walks him back to the same pin -> eternal loop. Reverse was never going to fix a steering loop that can't see its facing. Aaron's direction: have the mod OWN the camera (G/H rotate it -- confirmed via the FF8 PC manual: G=counter-clockwise, H=clockwise, NOT the Q/E first guessed) so it can both perceive AND control orientation. This build replaces the F12 diagnostic: RETIRED world_map_heading_scan.inl (HEADING_SCAN_DIAG 0 -- it injected RIGHT/LEFT, which are WALK keys on foot, so it rotated nothing and never found a facing) and added world_map_camera_scan.inl (CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG 1). F12 (dinput8.cpp) now calls WorldMap::TriggerCameraScan. The new scan, fully automated while the player holds still (~5s, TTS on start + completion, pause/resume around encounters): measures a short UP-walk vector (A), taps H x5 (clockwise) sampling 3 memory windows (foot 0x0203EE00 / hdng 0x0203ED00 / save 0x01CFFEB8) after each tap, measures UP-walk (B), taps G x5 (counter-clockwise), measures UP-walk (C). Reads from [CAMDIAG] in ff8_world.log: (1) the window offset whose b-delta ramps through the H taps and reverses through the G taps = the camera-angle field we can perceive; (2) whether the A/B/C UP-walk vectors rotate = movement is camera-relative; (3) per-tap rotation from B vs A. Decides the architecture: if G/H rotate the camera and a value tracks it, the real fix locks the camera to a canonical angle so UP always means a known world direction; else fall back to the empirically-derived walk basis with direct screen-relative arrow steering (like F9 field nav). BAT: on the world map standing still (NOT driving), press F12, hold still ~5s (you may hear a Gil readout from the G taps -- expected; if an encounter interrupts, fight it and it auto-restarts), then send Logs/ff8_world.log. No steering/routing behavior change this build. CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG + the remaining #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.72: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- make the reverse un-wedge ACTUALLY FIRE. The .71 BAT proved it still never fired, and the log showed exactly why: instead of a reverse burst, every mid-route stuck check fell through to '[DRIVE] Stuck mid-route -- re-planning ... recovery 5/8, 6/8' (a futile re-plan that recomputes the identical 21-cell route every time). Cause: the reverse had a separate 4-use budget (MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE) that got spent early in the drive and NEVER reset -- each random encounter (~every few seconds here) resumes him on the same neck and re-plans but does NOT reset the budget (only StartAutoDrive does, and the progress watermark only renews on getting closer, which never happens), so after 4 bursts the reverse was permanently locked out for the rest of the drive. FIX: drop the separate 4-use budget; fire the reverse on EVERY mid-route stuck check while there is still give-up headroom (s_driveStuckCount < DRIVE_STUCK_MAX), and let stuckCount keep climbing so it still gives up after DRIVE_STUCK_MAX if reversing never helps. stuckCount resets on resume, so each post-encounter segment gets a fresh set of attempts -- the reverse can no longer be starved. Still DOWN-only; the plan is DOWN backs him off the wall into open ground, then the normal PIVOT re-aims him (rotation appears to need open space -- turning in place while pinned does nothing, which is why the heading stays frozen at the wedge). NOTE: the route is confirmed FINE (the .71 [ROUTEMAP] again showed the single open corridor between ocean and blocked mountains). NOTE 2: random encounters are firing every few seconds at this spot, chopping the drive into short segments -- a separate concern, but segments are usually long enough for at least one 3s stuck check. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the jam expect '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst (check N/6)' then [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; the decisive reads are (1) does his pos/dist SHIFT on the REVERSE frames (DOWN moves him) and (2) once he's backed off, does hdg finally start CHANGING as the PIVOT re-aims him (rotation works in open space) -- if both, he should round the corner and continue. If reverse moves him but he still can't rotate after backing off, that's the next thing to chase. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.71: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REVERSE un-wedge, now actually triggered. Aaron confirmed pressing DOWN moves the character on the world map, so the reverse primitive is sound; the bug was that the recovery NEVER FIRED in the .68/.69/.70 BATs because it was keyed on a hard-wedge detector (no-movement in .68, net-displacement-250u in .69) that the wall-oscillation kept defeating. The .70 clearance change also had no effect on the route -- the [ROUTEMAP] showed it threading the ONE open north-south corridor between the ocean (east) and a band of blocked mountain cells (west); col 105 is already the clearance-optimal lane, so a stronger bias had nothing better to move to. Routing is fine; the blocker is purely the start-cell wedge (he resumes on a narrow water/cliff neck, hdg frozen 254, can't turn out -- a full second of LEFT-only never moved the heading -- and 'forward' slides him along the waterline). FIX: trigger the reverse burst from the PROVEN stuck-check (it logs 'Stuck check N/6' reliably every 3s) instead of the dead hard-wedge path, and make the burst DOWN-only (no simultaneous turn -- turning while wedged does nothing and muddied .68/.69; a clean backward push into open ground, then normal steering re-aims). On a mid-route stuck check (dist>=DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST, budget MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE left) it sets s_driveWedgeReverseUntil=now+REVERSE_BURST_MS, logs '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4', resets the give-up counter, and returns so DOWN is pressed from the next tick; the progress watermark renews the budget once a reverse gets him >=WEDGE_PROGRESS_EPS closer, else it falls through to re-plan/give-up. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the jam expect '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst 1/4' then [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; the decisive read is whether d+ goes POSITIVE and his position/dist actually shifts on those frames (reverse frees him -> deadlock broken, tune from there) or stays ~0 (something still blocks it). If he backs off and then re-aims and continues, that's the win. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.70: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- CLEARANCE-PREFERRING PLANNER (Aaron-requested): prioritize an open, clean path over minimizing distance. The .69 BAT (wedge detector still didn't fire -- this run the oscillation amplitude was ~840u E-W, far above WEDGE_NET_EPS=250, so the net-displacement anchor kept resetting) came with F11 screenshots that finally showed the real problem: the player is sawing east-west along a thin SHORELINE strip at the water's edge with a WIDE-OPEN grass field right beside him -- the route hugs the coast when open ground is adjacent, and 'forward' just slides him along the waterline (hdg frozen 7, Y pinned ~-26360, X swinging 840u, dist stuck ~15km). That is a routing problem, not a wedge-recovery problem: the route should go through the open grass, not along the water. PlanPathFine already biases toward clearance (s_clearFine = Chebyshev dist to nearest ocean/steep-mountain), but far too weakly (WM_CLEAR_PENALTY=6, WM_CLEAR_TARGET=3 made a wall/water-adjacent cell cost only 13 vs 1 open). Raised to WM_CLEAR_PENALTY=20, WM_CLEAR_TARGET=5: a clearance-1 (edge-hugging) cell now costs ~81 vs 1 for open ground (clearance>=5), so the Dijkstra detours FAR to stay clear of water/mountain edges and only dips to low clearance at the unavoidable coastal destination itself. Reachability is unchanged (costs stay finite; IsFineTraversable untouched) -- only the cost weighting changed, so a genuinely narrow-only corridor is still taken, just at high cost. Tunable; the [ROUTEMAP] dump at plan time shows the new route immediately. BAT: drive to Dollet -- check [ROUTEMAP] in ff8_world.log shows the route now running through OPEN ground (inland) rather than along the coast, and the player should stay on open grass where the steering already works (it drove 19->17km smoothly on open ground in .66) and ARRIVE; ALSO a Balamb drive (regression -- the stronger bias changes every route). If he still pins on the new open route, next step is the progress-based wedge detector (the .68/.69 reverse recovery never got to fire -- DOWN is still untested). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.69: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- wedge detector FIX: key the hard-wedge on NET displacement, not raw movement. The .68 BAT proved the reverse un-wedge NEVER fired -- no [DRIVE] burst line, no D key the whole run -- because the jam is a wall-VIBRATION, not stillness: the player bounces ~60u east-west every frame (per-frame d+ 30-62) while netting ~zero forward progress (dist pinned at 14960), and those bounces kept resetting the .68 move-timer so the hard-wedge (no-movement for HARD_WEDGE_MS) never tripped. The mod's own slow stuck-check DID see it (moved 6/9 units in 3000ms) but that only feeds give-up. Fix: detect the wedge by NET displacement from an anchor that only resets when he genuinely relocates WEDGE_NET_EPS=250u, so vibration-in-place trips hardWedge after HARD_WEDGE_MS and the reverse finally fires. Secondary finding (informs .70): pressing LEFT to turn while MOVING 60+ units did NOT rotate him (hdg frozen at 201 all run), so turning is dead while jammed -- relocating via reverse is the needed escape, and this BAT is the decisive test of whether DOWN does anything on the world map. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the corner expect [DRIVE] 'Hard wedge -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4' + [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; READ whether d+ (and especially the net pos change) goes POSITIVE on those frames. If yes, reverse works -> tune from there. If d+~0 on REVERSE too, DOWN is inert on the world map -> .70 attacks it from routing. Next (.70, Aaron-requested): clearance-preferring planner -- bias the route toward open ground over shortest distance so it avoids pinch corners in the first place. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.68: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REVERSE un-wedge recovery. The .67 route-map dump answered Aaron's routing question: the route is directionally correct (NE to Dollet) but runs along the coast for the north leg (the inland is mountain-broken in the grid), and the recurring 15km jam is at the east->north CORNER against the coast. The trace pinned the mechanism: on the world map the character only ROTATES while moving forward, so when he noses into the coast at the corner he can't move, can't turn, and his heading FREEZES (hdg stuck at 501 the whole jam) -- he just presses forward into the same water forever. The closed-loop steering itself works (drove 19km->17km smoothly on the open leg before a random encounter); what was missing is wedge recovery. Fix: add a DOWN-arrow (reverse) injection + a wedge-recovery branch in UpdateAutoDrive. On a HARD wedge (no real movement for HARD_WEDGE_MS=1200, longer than the WALL_SLIDE_MS slide window), reverse off the wall for REVERSE_BURST_MS=600 while turning toward the target (back-and-aim) to regain open ground + motion, then resume normal steering. Bounded to MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE=4 bursts per wedge; the budget renews only on genuine progress (got WEDGE_PROGRESS_EPS=500u closer), so a true no-progress jam still falls through to the existing stuck-detection / re-plan / give-up instead of reversing forever. Also dropped the counterproductive wall-jam forced-RIGHT nudge (it shoved him away from a left-turn target). Key injection: SetDriveKeys gains a 4th 'down' param (VK_DOWN 0x28 / scan 0x50, extended); ReleaseAllDriveKeys releases it. [HDG-DIAG] gains a REVERSE band + a D key column. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- at the coastal corner expect [DRIVE] 'Hard wedge -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4' lines and [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames where d+ goes POSITIVE (he backs off) and hdg starts changing again, then him rounding the corner and continuing. If reverse moves him (d+>0 on REVERSE frames) the deadlock is broken even if tuning is needed; if d+ stays ~0 on REVERSE too, DOWN does nothing on the world map and we need a different un-wedge. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG / ROUTE_MAP_DIAG / HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.67: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- DIAGNOSTIC: route-map dump to verify the planner actually picks the right path to Dollet. The .66 closed-loop steering BAT jammed ~15km out on a coastal spit (F11 screenshot: tall cliff NW, ocean E, narrow shoreline strip), and [HDG-DIAG] showed hdg FROZEN at 502 the whole time while keys cycled U/L/R -- he could not rotate out. But the deeper question (Aaron) is whether the ROUTE is even correct: you reach Dollet via an inland canyon, not by hugging the coast, and the log only prints the route's endpoints (21 cells fine(105,70)->fine(112,57)), never the intermediate cells, so neither of us can tell if the path threads the canyon or scrapes the shore. New DumpRouteMap (gated ROUTE_MAP_DIAG, called in PlanPathFine at plan time) logs an ASCII map of the REAL fine terrain grid spanning the planned route with the route overlaid (.=land f=forest m=gentle-mtn ~=ocean; CAPS/X/^=blocked for this vehicle; S=start D=Dollet G=goal o=route; row 0=north, col 0=west), so we can SEE whether the chosen path is the canyon or a coast-hug AND whether the grid's walkable/blocked classification is even right. No steering/routing behavior change. BAT: start a Dollet auto-drive -- the [ROUTEMAP] block logs IMMEDIATELY at plan time, so no need to drive into the jam; just send Logs/ff8_world.log. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG (plus DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + HEADING_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.66: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REBUILD steering as closed-loop control on the REAL facing. The .65 F12 heading scan was decisive: WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02, exactly what GetWorldMapHeading already reads) IS the live facing -- holding RIGHT raised it ~80/sample (3595->4051->wrap->99, clockwise), holding LEFT reversed it, in the SAME compass units as TorusBearing (0=N, clockwise). The earlier 'frozen' DIAGNOSIS was wrong in its conclusion: the value wasn't dead, the character simply wasn't rotating during those drives -- he was jammed pressing forward+turn into terrain, which the engine won't rotate, so his facing genuinely stayed at his resting value (3595 in .62-.64, 540 in .61 = just each save's start facing). The motion-derived workaround then layered wall-slide corruption on top. Fix: UpdateAutoDrive now steers closed-loop on GetWorldMapHeading with a TURN-THEN-GO law -- err = targetBearing - heading (wrapped to [-2048,2048]); when |err| > STEER_DEADZONE, PIVOT turn-only (RIGHT if err>=0 since RIGHT raises the heading, else LEFT; NO forward, so the engine rotates him freely and the heading updates -- exactly the scan's clean turn-only phase); once aligned, drive forward; within FINAL_APPROACH_FORWARD_DIST just walk straight; a wall-jam watchdog forces a pivot if he stops moving while aimed. A true heading lets the pivot stop the instant we're aligned (kills the oscillation), and turn-only sidesteps the arc-into-wall jam that locked rotation before. ALL routing (clearance Dijkstra + path lookahead) is reused UNCHANGED -- this is the steering law only, general, not per-town. The motion-derived heading machinery (s_driveMoveHeading + runtime turn-sign calibration + duty-cycle) is gone from the steering path. New throttled [HDG-DIAG] trace logs the real heading vs target bearing + err + keys to confirm it tracks during a live drive. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] hdg to rotate toward tgtBrg while PIVOTing, err to shrink and stay small on STRAIGHT frames, and him to thread the canyon and ARRIVE; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG and the F12 heading scan (HEADING_SCAN_DIAG) both retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.65: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- STEP BACK from steering tuning to a live-facing DISCOVERY diagnostic. Ten builds of steering work all fought one missing sensor: we cannot read which way the character is FACING. WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02) is frozen -- the .61 diagnostic proved it returns a constant all session -- so every steering decision since has run on a motion-derived heading that lags the turn and is corrupted whenever he slides along a wall (his motion then follows the wall, not his facing): the source of the .63/.64 oscillation-then-wedge. The real facing DOES exist in memory (FFNx's world.cpp reads the live camera out of sub_4023D0; the savemap WORLDMAP struct carries a rotation field) -- we just have the wrong address. New world_map_heading_scan.inl finds it deterministically and blind-accessibly: on the world map, press F12 -- while the player holds still it INJECTS a known turn (RIGHT ~1.6s then LEFT ~1.6s, turn-only via SetDriveKeys(0,0,1)/(0,1,0) so he pivots roughly in place, no forward/gas) and dumps three candidate memory windows (foot@0x0203EE00 256B, hdng@0x0203ED00 64B, save@0x01CFFEB8 32B) interpreted as int16 words every 150ms, logging each changed word's per-sample (s) and per-baseline (b) delta under [HSCAN]. The live facing is the offset whose s-delta holds ONE sign across the RIGHT samples and FLIPS across the LEFT samples (frame counters ramp the same sign both phases; position words move only if he translated). Announces \"Heading scan complete\" on finish; pauses and resumes around random encounters via Poll()'s existing world-map exit/entry detection (HScanPause/HScanResume -- the same mechanism auto-drive uses), re-snapshotting the baseline and restarting the interrupted turn phase on resume. F12 wired in dinput8.cpp gated on !alt (it was the free per-session diagnostic key; no old F12 handler to retire). No steering/routing change this build. BAT: on the world map standing still, press F12, let it run ~4s (if an encounter interrupts, fight it -- the scan announces \"paused\" then \"resuming\" and continues), then send Logs/ff8_world.log; the [HSCAN] block reveals the live facing address, which the next build rebuilds steering around as proper closed-loop control (no motion-derivation, no wall-corruption, no oscillation). All routing is reused as-is; this is NOT route-hardcoding. // v0.18.3.64: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- damp the steering oscillation + tighten the lookahead. The .63 BAT was real progress: sign=+1 held, the player drove the RIGHT direction and covered ~3km toward Dollet (19km->16km), then PINNED at a tight canyon bend, oscillating in place -- [DRIVE-DIAG] showed his motion heading swinging ~180deg past the target and back every frame (mh 2749<->746, keys U-R<->UL-), d+ ~2-6, dist stuck at 16446, until the stuck-check fired. Two causes: (1) bang-bang turning with no damping -- holding the turn key every frame over-rotates ~180deg past the target, then reverses and overshoots back; (2) the 3-cell lookahead again aimed at a point across the canyon wall, so he mashed into it and his motion-derived heading read the wall-deflected motion. Two fixes: aim at the NEXT path cell only (DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS 3->1) -- consecutive BFS path cells are 4-adjacent and walkable, so the straight line to the target can never cross a wall; and DUTY-CYCLE the turn (TURN_DUTY_ON_MS 60 / OFF 120, ~33%) so straight forward frames between turn pulses let the heading catch up and he converges instead of spinning. Full turn still applies while wall-jammed. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] err to actually SHRINK and stay small (not swing +1600/-450), d+ to stay positive (forward progress), dist to keep dropping past 16km to arrival; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.63: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 follow-up to the .62 root-cause fix. The .62 BAT was the breakthrough -- the motion-derived heading made the player MOBILE for the first time (he traversed the continent; mh tracked his motion while the raw read stayed frozen at a constant 3595) -- but he drove to Galbadia Garden instead of Dollet. The [DRIVE-DIAG] trace showed two faults. (1) The runtime turn-sign calibration FLIPPED THE SIGN WRONG: it learns the sign by watching the derived heading rotate when turning, but in the canyon he slides along walls where movement direction follows the WALL not his facing, so the calibration read garbage; with sign=-1 the steering pressed LEFT for a right-side target, curving him away from Dollet. (2) At the start he was aimed almost dead-on at the target (err=+56) but JAMMED against the canyon wall, and the far 2400u straight-line lookahead pointed across that wall, so the wall-slide spun him ~180deg the wrong way before he broke free and committed to the wrong direction. Two fixes: the turn sign is now FIXED at +1 (TorusBearing is compass-style -- north 0, clockwise, confirmed by tgtBrg=477 for a NE target -- so pressing RIGHT turns clockwise = raises the bearing) and the wall-corrupted runtime calibration is removed; and the steering target is now DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS(3) cells AHEAD ALONG THE ROUTE instead of the first cell >=2400u in a straight line, so the aim stays inside the winding corridor rather than cutting across a wall. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] sign=+1 throughout, err to shrink as he follows the canyon, and him to head down the canyon toward Dollet rather than toward Galbadia Garden; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.62: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 ROOT-CAUSE FIX -- steer by a motion-derived heading instead of the frozen GetWorldMapHeading(). The v0.18.3.61 diagnostic proved the heading read returns a constant 540 for the ENTIRE drive (head=540 dH=+0 on every line) while position and keys varied, so every relBearing across all six prior BATs was computed against a fake facing and the steering turned the WRONG way -- it curved the player west (away from eastward Dollet) into the coastline and jammed pressing UP into collision (d+4 for ~5s). Fix: ignore GetWorldMapHeading entirely for steering and derive the facing from the bearing of the player's recent position delta (his real movement direction is exactly what steering needs), re-sampled every MOVE_HEADING_MIN_DELTA(50) units. The one unknown -- does RIGHT raise or lower that bearing -- is LEARNED at runtime: when a turn rotates the derived heading by >TURN_CAL_MIN_DELTA(80), s_driveTurnSign is set from the observed direction, so a wrong initial guess self-corrects within a sample and cannot steer wrong-way for long. Always press forward (no pivot-in-place) so motion -- and thus the heading + calibration -- never stalls; plus a wall-slide (force a turn after WALL_SLIDE_MS(600) with no movement) to nose off terrain. Replaces BOTH the mid-route and final-approach steering branches (both fed off the broken heading). [DRIVE-DIAG] now logs mh(derived) vs raw(broken) heading + signed err + learned sign. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] mh to TRACK his motion (not stuck at 540), err to shrink, and him to actually move toward Dollet; also BAT a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.61: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 DIAGNOSTIC build (no behavior change beyond logging). Six BATs of the on-foot Dollet auto-drive have failed, each diagnosed from sparse data (a 'moved N units in 3000ms' stuck-check every 3s plus one F11), and the fixes have not converged because the actual per-tick steering behavior is not visible -- the v0.18.3.60 BAT left the player essentially stationary (16 units / 3s) near the coastal start, recovery-replanning to 6/8 before a random encounter, and the logs cannot distinguish pivot-in-place (turning, never committing forward) from forward-into-collision (UP pressed but blocked by walkmesh geometry the 1024u grid cannot see) -- which need opposite fixes. This build adds a throttled [DRIVE-DIAG] trace (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG, ~5 lines/sec) in UpdateAutoDrive logging position + delta-moved, heading + delta, target bearing, relBearing/off, keys pressed (U/L/R), and the band taken (STRAIGHT/ARC/PIVOT/final). The per-line delta-moved tells the three cases apart: PIVOT band with dH changing + d+~0 = pivoting in place; STRAIGHT/ARC with U pressed but d+~0 = walking into collision; d+ large = real progress. No steering/routing change. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet, let it sit stuck ~10s, then send ff8_world.log (the [DRIVE-DIAG] block) + an F11 -- the trace will show exactly why he is not moving, and the next build is a targeted fix. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG must be set false (or the trace removed) before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.60: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 4 -- mid-route steering law: dead-zone + arc + clean wrap, to kill the heading-overshoot oscillation. The v0.18.3.59 BAT routed correctly (clearance-weighted 23 cells to Dollet fine(112,57)) but the drive OSCILLATED in place at the start: the player rocked across the cell-103/104 boundary (~33u E-W), creeping north only a few units, then a random encounter (MODE_SWIRL) interrupted; F11 = on foot near the coast (loco byte noise=35). Diagnosed offline against the real route -- NOT the routing or lookahead (the lookahead pointed the same way, NE ~45deg off north, from both cells he rocked between); the steering law itself. The old law walked forward (UP) only within ~17.5deg of the target and turned in place otherwise, at a fixed rate with no damping, so the heading overshot the target and rocked across it while rarely engaging forward -- weaving in place instead of driving. Across five prior BATs the routing failures (.55/.56) are fixed and the follow failures (.57 drift, .58 crawl, .59 oscillation) all trace to this one discrete turn-law. Fix: replace ONLY the mid-route branch (dist >= DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST) with an arc law -- fold relBearing into an absolute offset + side (no hard L/R boundary to rock on), then within STEER_DEADZONE (~28deg) drive STRAIGHT with no turn (damps the overshoot -- stop correcting once roughly aimed); within STEER_FWD_CONE (~50deg) drive forward AND turn (arc); beyond it pivot in place toward the target (sharp bends, as before). Final-approach branch UNCHANGED. STEER_DEADZONE/STEER_FWD_CONE are tunable (widen deadzone if it still oscillates, narrow if it drifts into walls). This changes EVERY drive's long-distance steering, so BAT must ALSO regression-check a known-good Balamb drive. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet (thread the canyon + ARRIVE; [DRIVE] re-planning lines = recovery not failure) AND a Balamb destination (no regression). // v0.18.3.59: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 3 -- clearance-centred routing + min-distance lookahead + stuck recovery (pre-plan the route, then drive smoothly along it). Four BATs of the fine planner each peeled a distinct bug; the last two exposed a real tension -- a far lookahead DRIFTS off the path into a wall (v0.18.3.57) while a one-cell lookahead CRAWLS (v0.18.3.58): the steering law walks forward only when the target is within ~45deg of heading and turns in place otherwise, and a 1024u target swings the bearing as the player inches, so it spins. Offline analysis showed the walkable route to Dollet is a genuinely narrow winding canyon (~1-2 fine cells wide; even a clearance-centred route averages only 1.7 cells of wall clearance), so the grid's 1024u approximation of the real walkmesh is least accurate exactly there. Three coordinated changes: (1) PlanPathFine is now a clearance-weighted Dijkstra -- s_clearFine (Chebyshev distance to the nearest blocked cell, computed once at grid-load by multi-source BFS) makes a step into a low-clearance cell cost 1 + WM_CLEAR_PENALTY*(WM_CLEAR_TARGET-clearance), so the route threads the corridor CENTRE instead of scraping walls; (2) the drive steers at the first path cell >= DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST (2400u) ahead -- far enough for a stable bearing (no crawl), close enough that the straight-line cut stays inside the corridor (no drift); (3) mid-route stuck recovery re-plans from the player's CURRENT position (a fresh clearance route from where he actually is) up to DRIVE_MAX_REPLANS times before giving up. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: the clearance Dijkstra routes 30 cells to Dollet fine(112,57) (avg clearance 1.70, reaches the target); the offline follow-sim with the real steering law arrives with ~720u deviation at this lookahead. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- expect [WALKFINE] ...clearance max/wall-hugging..., [PLAN] Fine path ...clearance-weighted, and the player to thread the canyon and ARRIVE; any [DRIVE] re-planning lines are recovery, not failure. // v0.18.3.58: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 2 -- steer to the NEXT path cell, not a far line-of-sight lookahead. The v0.18.3.57 BAT routed correctly (27 fine cells around the cliff to Dollet) but the drive still failed ~12km out: the LOS lookahead (farthest path cell with a clear cell-centre straight line, up to 8 ahead) steered the player diagonally OFF the straight-north path corridor -- the 1024-unit cell-centre Bresenham looked clear, but the game's finer cliff geometry between cell centres blocked the diagonal, so the player drifted ~3 cells east and jammed against a cliff (stuck at fine(103,64) with the cliff cell at fine(103,63) directly north). A long lookahead cuts corners; that recurred across BATs. Fix: the drive now steers to s_drivePath[idx+1] only -- consecutive path cells are 4-adjacent and walkable, so the player can't cut a corner or drift off-corridor, and the cursor's per-tick re-projection to the nearest path cell steers him back on if a collision nudges him off. Trades steering smoothness for faithfully hugging the route (FineLineWalkable now unused). BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- the player should follow the path corridor around the cliff and ARRIVE. // v0.18.3.57: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX -- route to the destination coordinate, not the goal region. The v0.18.3.56 BAT failed to reach Dollet: PlanPathFine routed to the nearest cell of the goal REGION, but Dollet's region 0x01 is large and reaches right next to the player, so the fine path was only 3 cells north and the drive then steered NE into the same cliff. Fix: PlanPathFine now floods the reachable component from the player and routes to the visited fine cell NEAREST the destination coordinate (s_driveTargetX/Y) -- the target itself when reachable, which the catalog already vetted. s_driveGoalSegs is retained for arrival detection only. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: Dollet now routes 20 fine cells AROUND the cliff to the actual target fine(112,57) (dist 0), zero blocked cells on the path. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- expect ff8_world.log '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells ... nearest reachable to target' and arrival, not a hang. // v0.18.3.56: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- point the auto-drive planner at the fine slope-aware grid. The coarse 32x24 A* (PlanPath over s_terrainGrid, segment-centre waypoints) routed straight lines through mountains it couldn't see -- the v0.18.3.55 BAT hung the Dollet drive at the base of a steep cliff because the planner laid a waypoint due north into terrain the fine grid already knew was impassable. PlanPathFine replaces it: a 4-connected torus BFS over the 256x192 fine grid using the SAME IsFineTraversable rule as catalog reachability (s_walkClassFine + s_steepFine), to the nearest walkable fine cell in the goal region (s_driveGoalSegs), storing the route as packed fine cells in s_drivePath. The drive follows it with a line-of-sight lookahead (FineLineWalkable Bresenham, up to 8 cells): it steers toward the farthest path cell reachable in a clear walkable straight line, smoothing diagonal staircases AND guaranteeing it never aims the player across a blocked cell (a fixed lookahead would cut the cliff corner). On final approach / path end it steers to the real destination coord. Region/goal selection (MatchProgramForCatalog/CollectGoalSegments) is unchanged -- only the routing grid changed; geometric-trigger destinations (Fire Cavern, early Balamb Garden) still use simple-coord steering and are unaffected. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: the Dollet route is 20 fine cells AROUND the cliff (fine(101,65)->fine(112,57), zero blocked cells); LOS lookahead crosses no blocked cell at any cursor. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet and to Galbadia Garden -- ff8_world.log should show '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells', and the drive should route around mountains and ARRIVE instead of hanging. // v0.18.3.55: #67 BAT 2 stage 1 -- slope-aware fine grid (mountains). The fine reachability grid now carries a per-cell steepness (the elevation spread of the wmx polygon at the cell, read from the vertex elevation field previously discarded during the wmx load). IsFineTraversable blocks a MOUNTAIN-class cell whose steepness exceeds WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK (256) for foot/chocobo/car -- steep mountain faces are impassable; gentle mountain cells (passes/plateaus) stay walkable, so the map doesn't over-fragment (blanket type-29 blocking isolated Esthar and split Edea from Centra -- real passes exist). Garden/Ragnarok ignore it (hover/fly). Threshold calibrated offline against destination reachability: at 256, every Galbadia and Balamb catalog destination stays reachable AND the full reachable set is unchanged vs no blocking; it is refined in-game via the [WM-CALIB] steepness trace (now logs steep=N plus a BLOCKED flag per cell). Empirical terrain re-eval also confirmed the six undocumented terrain types present (17/18/23/24/25/31) all sit at land elevations and are gentle -- genuine walkable land, NOT hidden water; all map water is the ocean class already blocked. This stage touches reachability/catalog only; the AD planner still routes on the coarse grid (stage 2 points it at the fine grid). BAT: load a Galbadia save then a Balamb save and cycle the catalog -- expect the SAME destinations as v0.18.3.54 (none lost); check ff8_world.log [WALKFINE] (mountain steep-blocked count) and [WM-CALIB] (it should never log the player standing on a BLOCKED cell -- if it does, the threshold is too low).
